window.DOMINANCE_TOPICS = [{"slug":"cv-introduction","title":"Introduction to the Heart and Circulation","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"1-6","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-do-we-need-a-circulatory-system","title":"1. Why do we need a circulatory system?"},{"id":"2-the-two-major-circulatory-pathways","title":"2. The two major circulatory pathways"},{"id":"3-the-heart-and-its-four-chambers","title":"3. The heart and its four chambers"},{"id":"4-why-is-the-left-ventricular-wall-thicker-than-the-right","title":"4. Why is the left ventricular wall thicker than the right?"},{"id":"5-systole-and-diastole","title":"5. Systole and diastole"},{"id":"6-cardiac-muscle-and-the-conducting-system","title":"6. Cardiac muscle and the conducting system"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Introduction to the Heart and Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-do-we-need-a-circulatory-system\">1. Why do we need a circulatory system?</h2>\n<p>Every cell in the body requires oxygen and nutrients to function. At the same time, cells produce metabolic waste products, including carbon dioxide, which must be removed.</p>\n<p>The <strong>circulatory system</strong> makes this continuous transport possible.</p>\n<p>It consists of three major components:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Heart:</strong> Pumps blood through the circulation.</li>\n<li><strong>Blood vessels:</strong> Provide pathways through which blood travels.</li>\n<li><strong>Blood:</strong> Transports oxygen, nutrients, hormones, waste products and heat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For example, during exercise, skeletal muscles require more oxygen and nutrients to meet their increased energy demands. The circulatory system helps deliver these substances while transporting carbon dioxide and other metabolic products away.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-the-two-major-circulatory-pathways\">2. The two major circulatory pathways</h2>\n<p>Blood circulates through two connected pathways:</p>\n<p><strong>Pulmonary circulation:</strong> Carries blood from the right side of the heart to the lungs, where carbon dioxide is released and oxygen is taken up. The oxygenated blood then returns to the left side of the heart.</p>\n<p><strong>Systemic circulation:</strong> Carries oxygenated blood from the left side of the heart to tissues throughout the body. After delivering oxygen and collecting carbon dioxide, the blood returns to the right side of the heart.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs01-figure-01\">\n  <a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/2101_Blood_Flow_Through_the_Heart.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Open full-size pulmonary and systemic circulation diagram\">\n    <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/2101_Blood_Flow_Through_the_Heart.jpg\" alt=\"Diagram of systemic and pulmonary circulation, showing blood travelling between the right heart and lungs, then the left heart and body organs.\" />\n  </a>\n  <figcaption><strong>Figure 01 — Pulmonary and systemic circulation.</strong> Follow the blood flow through the lungs and body tissues. Tap to enlarge. <span class=\"figure-credit\">OpenStax College, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2101_Blood_Flow_Through_the_Heart.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">original illustration</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a>; unmodified.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p>These two circuits are arranged <strong>in series</strong>, meaning blood passes through one circuit and then the other in a continuous pathway.</p>\n<p>Consequently, under stable conditions, the right and left ventricles must pump approximately equal volumes of blood per minute. Otherwise, blood would progressively accumulate in one circulation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-the-heart-and-its-four-chambers\">3. The heart and its four chambers</h2>\n<p>The heart is a muscular organ that functions as two coordinated pumps.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Right atrium:</strong> Receives blood returning from the body through the superior and inferior venae cavae.</li>\n<li><strong>Right ventricle:</strong> Pumps blood towards the lungs through the pulmonary trunk and pulmonary arteries.</li>\n<li><strong>Left atrium:</strong> Receives oxygenated blood returning from the lungs through the pulmonary veins.</li>\n<li><strong>Left ventricle:</strong> Pumps oxygenated blood into the aorta for distribution throughout the body.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>atria primarily receive blood and assist ventricular filling</strong>, while the <strong>ventricles generate the pressure needed to pump blood into the arteries</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs01-figure-02\">\n  <a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Blausen_0457_Heart_SectionalAnatomy.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Open full-size labelled heart anatomy diagram\">\n    <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Blausen_0457_Heart_SectionalAnatomy.png\" alt=\"Sectional anatomy of the human heart showing four chambers, cardiac valves, great vessels, interventricular septum, and the thicker left ventricular wall.\" />\n  </a>\n  <figcaption><strong>Figure 02 — Internal structure of the heart.</strong> Identify the chambers, valves, major vessels and differences in ventricular wall thickness. Tap to enlarge. <span class=\"figure-credit\">Blausen Medical Communications, Inc., <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blausen_0457_Heart_SectionalAnatomy.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">original illustration</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a>; unmodified.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<h3>How does blood flow through the heart?</h3>\n<p>Follow the pathway:</p>\n<p><strong>Body tissues → Venae cavae → Right atrium → Tricuspid valve → Right ventricle → Pulmonary valve → Pulmonary trunk and arteries → Lungs → Pulmonary veins → Left atrium → Mitral valve → Left ventricle → Aortic valve → Aorta → Body tissues.</strong></p>\n<p>The cardiac valves help maintain one-way blood flow by preventing blood from flowing backwards.</p>\n<p>Notice that the pulmonary arteries carry relatively deoxygenated blood, while the pulmonary veins carry oxygenated blood.</p>\n<p>This is because <strong>arteries carry blood away from the heart, whereas veins carry blood towards the heart</strong>, regardless of oxygen content.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-why-is-the-left-ventricular-wall-thicker-than-the-right\">4. Why is the left ventricular wall thicker than the right?</h2>\n<p>Although both ventricles pump approximately the same volume of blood per minute, they must generate different pressures.</p>\n<p>To understand why, consider <strong>vascular resistance</strong>, which refers to the opposition that blood vessels offer to blood flow.</p>\n<p>Imagine water flowing through a pipe. When the pipe narrows, it becomes harder to maintain the same flow unless a greater pressure difference is applied.</p>\n<p>A similar principle applies to blood vessels.</p>\n<p>The systemic circulation contains many small arteries and arterioles whose narrow lumens and muscular walls help regulate blood flow to different organs. Contraction or relaxation of their vascular smooth muscle changes the vessel diameter and therefore its resistance to blood flow.</p>\n<p>Collectively, the systemic vascular network offers substantially more resistance than the pulmonary vascular network.</p>\n<p>The pulmonary circulation, by comparison, has a relatively low-resistance network of vessels that allows blood to pass through the lungs at much lower pressures.</p>\n<p><strong>What does this mean for the ventricles?</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>left ventricle</strong> must generate higher pressure to maintain blood flow through the systemic circulation. Its muscular wall is therefore thicker.</p>\n<p>The <strong>right ventricle</strong> pumps blood through the lower-resistance pulmonary circulation, so it normally requires much less pressure and has a thinner muscular wall.</p>\n<p>The lower pressure in the pulmonary circulation is also beneficial because the lungs contain delicate capillaries surrounding the alveoli. Excessively high pressure in these capillaries can force more fluid into the surrounding lung tissue, potentially causing <strong>pulmonary oedema (fluid accumulation in the lungs)</strong> and interfering with gas exchange.</p>\n<p><strong>Key distinction:</strong> The left ventricle normally generates greater pressure, not a greater volume of blood per minute.</p>\n<p><em>The factors determining vascular resistance will be explored further in Blood Flow and Hemodynamics.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"5-systole-and-diastole\">5. Systole and diastole</h2>\n<p>The pumping activity of the heart involves alternating periods of contraction and relaxation.</p>\n<p><strong>Systole</strong> refers to the period of contraction of a heart chamber, while <strong>diastole</strong> refers to its period of relaxation.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Atrial systole:</strong> The atria contract, contributing the final portion of blood needed to complete ventricular filling.</li>\n<li><strong>Atrial diastole:</strong> The atria relax and receive blood returning from the body and lungs. During ventricular filling, blood can also pass from the relaxed atria into the ventricles.</li>\n<li><strong>Ventricular systole:</strong> The ventricles contract and generate pressure, allowing blood to be ejected into the pulmonary trunk (right ventricle) and aorta (left ventricle).</li>\n<li><strong>Ventricular diastole:</strong> The ventricles relax and fill with blood in preparation for the next contraction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Importantly, <strong>atrial systole normally occurs towards the end of ventricular diastole</strong>. The atria complete their contribution to ventricular filling before the ventricles contract.</p>\n<p><em>The detailed sequence of pressure changes, valve movements and phases of the heartbeat will be explained in the Cardiac Cycle chapter.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"6-cardiac-muscle-and-the-conducting-system\">6. Cardiac muscle and the conducting system</h2>\n<p>For the heart to pump blood effectively, it must generate sufficient contractile force, and its chambers must contract in a coordinated sequence.</p>\n<p>Two important components make this possible:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cardiac muscle (myocardium):</strong> Produces the force required for contraction and blood ejection.</li>\n<li><strong>Specialised cardiac conducting system:</strong> Generates and distributes electrical impulses that coordinate the heartbeat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Electrical activation normally precedes contraction, allowing the atria and ventricles to pump in an organised sequence.</p>\n<p><em>The cellular mechanisms of contraction and the electrical conducting pathways will be covered in the Cardiac Muscle Physiology and Cardiac Conduction chapters.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Why must the right and left ventricles pump approximately equal volumes of blood per minute?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The two circulations are arranged <strong>in series</strong>: blood ejected by the right ventricle passes through the lungs and then returns to the left ventricle. If one side persistently pumped more than the other, blood would accumulate in the circulation receiving the greater output, raising its pressures. Approximately equal long-term outputs therefore prevent progressive congestion.</p></details>\n<p><strong>2. Why does the left ventricle have a thicker muscular wall than the right ventricle?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>The left ventricle must generate higher pressure</strong> because systemic vascular resistance is much greater than pulmonary vascular resistance. More myocardial muscle permits greater pressure generation. Both ventricles normally pump approximately the same volume per minute; wall thickness differs primarily because of the pressure load, not because the left ventricle must pump more blood.</p></details>\n<p><strong>3. A vessel carries oxygenated blood towards the heart. Is it an artery or a vein? Explain.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>A vein.</strong> Arteries are defined by carrying blood away from the heart; veins carry blood towards it, regardless of oxygen content. Pulmonary veins are the relevant example: they return oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium.</p></details>\n<p><strong>4. Does ventricular contraction always mean blood is being ejected?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> Early ventricular contraction raises intraventricular pressure while both the AV and semilunar valves are closed. This is <em>isovolumetric contraction</em>. Ejection starts only when ventricular pressure exceeds the pressure in the pulmonary artery or aorta and opens the corresponding outflow valve.</p></details>\n<p><strong>5. During atrial systole, are the ventricles also contracting? Explain the timing.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Normally, no.</strong> Atrial systole occurs near the end of ventricular diastole. The AV nodal conduction delay helps ensure atrial contraction contributes its final portion of ventricular filling before ventricular systole starts. Most filling has already occurred passively.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Systemic and pulmonary circulations operate in series:</strong> long-term right and left ventricular outputs must be approximately equal.</li><li><strong>Arteries and veins:</strong> defined by direction of blood movement relative to the heart, not oxygenation.</li><li><strong>Left versus right ventricle:</strong> similar flow, different pressure loads; the left ventricle has a thicker muscular wall.</li><li><strong>Systole and diastole:</strong> contraction and relaxation are coordinated but differ between atria and ventricles.</li><li><strong>Atrial systole:</strong> normally completes ventricular filling near the end of ventricular diastole.</li><li><strong>Electrical activation precedes mechanical contraction:</strong> the conducting system coordinates timing, while myocardium supplies force.</li></ol>\n<hr />\n<p><em>Lecture connection: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 1–6. The resistance analogy and fluid-filtration explanation are supplementary foundations; detailed hemodynamics and cardiac-cycle mechanisms appear in their corresponding chapters.</em></p>\n","plain":"Introduction to the Heart and Circulation 1. Why do we need a circulatory system? Every cell in the body requires oxygen and nutrients to function. At the same time, cells produce metabolic waste products, including carbon dioxide, which must be removed. The circulatory system makes this continuous transport possible. It consists of three major components: Heart: Pumps blood through the circulation. Blood vessels: Provide pathways through which blood travels. Blood: Transports oxygen, nutrients, hormones, waste products and heat. For example, during exercise, skeletal muscles require more oxygen and nutrients to meet their increased energy demands. The circulatory system helps deliver these substances while transporting carbon dioxide and other metabolic products away. 2. The two major circulatory pathways Blood circulates through two connected pathways: Pulmonary circulation: Carries blood from the right side of the heart to the lungs, where carbon dioxide is released and oxygen is taken up. The oxygenated blood then returns to the left side of the heart. Systemic circulation: Carries oxygenated blood from the left side of the heart to tissues throughout the body. After delivering oxygen and collecting carbon dioxide, the blood returns to the right side of the heart. Figure 01 — Pulmonary and systemic circulation. Follow the blood flow through the lungs and body tissues. Tap to enlarge. OpenStax College, original illustration , CC BY 3.0 ; unmodified. These two circuits are arranged in series , meaning blood passes through one circuit and then the other in a continuous pathway. Consequently, under stable conditions, the right and left ventricles must pump approximately equal volumes of blood per minute. Otherwise, blood would progressively accumulate in one circulation. 3. The heart and its four chambers The heart is a muscular organ that functions as two coordinated pumps. Right atrium: Receives blood returning from the body through the superior and inferior venae cavae. Right ventricle: Pumps blood towards the lungs through the pulmonary trunk and pulmonary arteries. Left atrium: Receives oxygenated blood returning from the lungs through the pulmonary veins. Left ventricle: Pumps oxygenated blood into the aorta for distribution throughout the body. The atria primarily receive blood and assist ventricular filling , while the ventricles generate the pressure needed to pump blood into the arteries . Figure 02 — Internal structure of the heart. Identify the chambers, valves, major vessels and differences in ventricular wall thickness. Tap to enlarge. Blausen Medical Communications, Inc., original illustration , CC BY 3.0 ; unmodified. How does blood flow through the heart? Follow the pathway: Body tissues → Venae cavae → Right atrium → Tricuspid valve → Right ventricle → Pulmonary valve → Pulmonary trunk and arteries → Lungs → Pulmonary veins → Left atrium → Mitral valve → Left ventricle → Aortic valve → Aorta → Body tissues. The cardiac valves help maintain one-way blood flow by preventing blood from flowing backwards. Notice that the pulmonary arteries carry relatively deoxygenated blood, while the pulmonary veins carry oxygenated blood. This is because arteries carry blood away from the heart, whereas veins carry blood towards the heart , regardless of oxygen content. 4. Why is the left ventricular wall thicker than the right? Although both ventricles pump approximately the same volume of blood per minute, they must generate different pressures. To understand why, consider vascular resistance , which refers to the opposition that blood vessels offer to blood flow. Imagine water flowing through a pipe. When the pipe narrows, it becomes harder to maintain the same flow unless a greater pressure difference is applied. A similar principle applies to blood vessels. The systemic circulation contains many small arteries and arterioles whose narrow lumens and muscular walls help regulate blood flow to different organs. Contraction or relaxation of their vascular smooth muscle changes the vessel diameter and therefore its resistance to blood flow. Collectively, the systemic vascular network offers substantially more resistance than the pulmonary vascular network. The pulmonary circulation, by comparison, has a relatively low-resistance network of vessels that allows blood to pass through the lungs at much lower pressures. What does this mean for the ventricles? The left ventricle must generate higher pressure to maintain blood flow through the systemic circulation. Its muscular wall is therefore thicker. The right ventricle pumps blood through the lower-resistance pulmonary circulation, so it normally requires much less pressure and has a thinner muscular wall. The lower pressure in the pulmonary circulation is also beneficial because the lungs contain delicate capillaries surrounding the alveoli. Excessively high pressure in these capillaries can force more fluid into the surrounding lung tissue, potentially causing pulmonary oedema (fluid accumulation in the lungs) and interfering with gas exchange. Key distinction: The left ventricle normally generates greater pressure, not a greater volume of blood per minute. The factors determining vascular resistance will be explored further in Blood Flow and Hemodynamics. 5. Systole and diastole The pumping activity of the heart involves alternating periods of contraction and relaxation. Systole refers to the period of contraction of a heart chamber, while diastole refers to its period of relaxation. Atrial systole: The atria contract, contributing the final portion of blood needed to complete ventricular filling. Atrial diastole: The atria relax and receive blood returning from the body and lungs. During ventricular filling, blood can also pass from the relaxed atria into the ventricles. Ventricular systole: The ventricles contract and generate pressure, allowing blood to be ejected into the pulmonary trunk (right ventricle) and aorta (left ventricle). Ventricular diastole: The ventricles relax and fill with blood in preparation for the next contraction. Importantly, atrial systole normally occurs towards the end of ventricular diastole . The atria complete their contribution to ventricular filling before the ventricles contract. The detailed sequence of pressure changes, valve movements and phases of the heartbeat will be explained in the Cardiac Cycle chapter. 6. Cardiac muscle and the conducting system For the heart to pump blood effectively, it must generate sufficient contractile force, and its chambers must contract in a coordinated sequence. Two important components make this possible: Cardiac muscle (myocardium): Produces the force required for contraction and blood ejection. Specialised cardiac conducting system: Generates and distributes electrical impulses that coordinate the heartbeat. Electrical activation normally precedes contraction, allowing the atria and ventricles to pump in an organised sequence. The cellular mechanisms of contraction and the electrical conducting pathways will be covered in the Cardiac Muscle Physiology and Cardiac Conduction chapters. Understanding Check 1. Why must the right and left ventricles pump approximately equal volumes of blood per minute? Reveal explanation The two circulations are arranged in series : blood ejected by the right ventricle passes through the lungs and then returns to the left ventricle. If one side persistently pumped more than the other, blood would accumulate in the circulation receiving the greater output, raising its pressures. Approximately equal long-term outputs therefore prevent progressive congestion. 2. Why does the left ventricle have a thicker muscular wall than the right ventricle? Reveal explanation The left ventricle must generate higher pressure because systemic vascular resistance is much greater than pulmonary vascular resistance. More myocardial muscle permits greater pressure generation. Both ventricles normally pump approximately the same volume per minute; wall thickness differs primarily because of the pressure load, not because the left ventricle must pump more blood. 3. A vessel carries oxygenated blood towards the heart. Is it an artery or a vein? Explain. Reveal explanation A vein. Arteries are defined by carrying blood away from the heart; veins carry blood towards it, regardless of oxygen content. Pulmonary veins are the relevant example: they return oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium. 4. Does ventricular contraction always mean blood is being ejected? Reveal explanation No. Early ventricular contraction raises intraventricular pressure while both the AV and semilunar valves are closed. This is isovolumetric contraction . Ejection starts only when ventricular pressure exceeds the pressure in the pulmonary artery or aorta and opens the corresponding outflow valve. 5. During atrial systole, are the ventricles also contracting? Explain the timing. Reveal explanation Normally, no. Atrial systole occurs near the end of ventricular diastole. The AV nodal conduction delay helps ensure atrial contraction contributes its final portion of ventricular filling before ventricular systole starts. Most filling has already occurred passively. Chapter Essentials Systemic and pulmonary circulations operate in series: long-term right and left ventricular outputs must be approximately equal. Arteries and veins: defined by direction of blood movement relative to the heart, not oxygenation. Left versus right ventricle: similar flow, different pressure loads; the left ventricle has a thicker muscular wall. Systole and diastole: contraction and relaxation are coordinated but differ between atria and ventricles. Atrial systole: normally completes ventricular filling near the end of ventricular diastole. Electrical activation precedes mechanical contraction: the conducting system coordinates timing, while myocardium supplies force. Lecture connection: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 1–6. The resistance analogy and fluid-filtration explanation are supplementary foundations; detailed hemodynamics and cardiac-cycle mechanisms appear in their corresponding chapters."},{"slug":"cardiac-muscle","title":"Cardiac Muscle Physiology","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"4 and 18-20; added foundations","coverage":"supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-makes-cardiac-muscle-different","title":"1. What makes cardiac muscle different?"},{"id":"2-intercalated-discs-how-separate-cells-function-together","title":"2. Intercalated discs: how separate cells function together"},{"id":"3-the-cardiac-muscle-action-potential","title":"3. The cardiac muscle action potential"},{"id":"4-excitationcontraction-coupling-from-electrical-signal-to-force","title":"4. Excitation–contraction coupling: from electrical signal to force"},{"id":"5-how-does-cardiac-muscle-relax","title":"5. How does cardiac muscle relax?"},{"id":"6-why-cant-cardiac-muscle-undergo-normal-tetanus","title":"6. Why can't cardiac muscle undergo normal tetanus?"},{"id":"7-how-does-sympathetic-stimulation-affect-cardiac-muscle","title":"7. How does sympathetic stimulation affect cardiac muscle?"},{"id":"8-connecting-cardiac-muscle-physiology-to-the-pumping-heart","title":"8. Connecting cardiac muscle physiology to the pumping heart"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1 id=\"cardiac-muscle-physiology\">Cardiac Muscle Physiology</h1>\n<p><strong>Lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf — slides 4, 18 and 20. <strong>Essential supplementary physiology:</strong> cellular structure, action potentials, calcium handling and sympathetic effects.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-makes-cardiac-muscle-different\">1. What makes cardiac muscle different?</h2>\n<p>The heart must contract repeatedly throughout life, generating enough force to circulate blood while allowing sufficient time for its chambers to refill between beats. Its cells must respond to electrical stimulation, communicate with neighbouring cells and relax effectively after each contraction.</p>\n<p>Cardiac muscle is <strong>striated and involuntary</strong>, with short, branched cells that typically contain one centrally located nucleus. Like skeletal muscle, its contractile proteins are organised into sarcomeres containing actin and myosin.</p>\n<p>However, cardiac muscle has several distinctive features:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Intercalated discs</strong> connect neighbouring cardiomyocytes mechanically and electrically.</li><li><strong>Prolonged action potentials and refractory periods</strong> prevent normal tetanic contraction.</li><li><strong>Calcium-induced calcium release</strong> links electrical excitation to contraction.</li><li><strong>Numerous mitochondria</strong> support its continuous demand for ATP.</li></ul>\n<p>Cardiac muscle exhibits excitability, conductivity and contractility. Automaticity—the ability to generate impulses spontaneously—is particularly important in specialised pacemaker cells rather than every working ventricular myocyte.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-intercalated-discs-how-separate-cells-function-together\">2. Intercalated discs: how separate cells function together</h2>\n<p>A single cardiac muscle cell cannot pump blood effectively in isolation. To generate coordinated force, neighbouring cells must remain firmly connected and transmit electrical signals to one another.</p>\n<p>These connections occur at <strong>intercalated discs</strong>, which contain three important junctional structures.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Fascia adherens</strong></td><td>Anchor actin filaments and transmit contractile force between cells.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Desmosomes</strong></td><td>Hold neighbouring cells together, resisting separation during repeated contractions.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Gap junctions</strong></td><td>Permit ionic current to pass between cells, allowing electrical excitation to spread.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>When one cardiomyocyte becomes electrically excited, local current passes through gap junctions to neighbouring cells, bringing their membranes towards threshold and propagating the action potential. Fascia adherens and desmosomes maintain mechanical continuity as those cells contract.</p>\n<p>Thus, cardiac muscle behaves as a <strong><span data-term=\"functional-syncytium\">functional syncytium</span></strong>: many separate cells act together as a coordinated unit. The atria and ventricles form two electrically distinct functional syncytia. Their usual connection is through the specialised atrioventricular conducting pathway, allowing atrial contraction to precede ventricular contraction.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs02-figure-01\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7e/1020_Cardiac_Muscle.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7e/1020_Cardiac_Muscle.jpg\" alt=\"Branched cardiac muscle fibres and an enlarged intercalated disc showing desmosomes and gap junctions.\" width=\"1018\" height=\"476\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 01. Cardiac muscle and intercalated discs.</strong> Desmosomes and gap junctions are labelled; fascia adherens are discussed in the text but not separately labelled. <span class=\"figure-credit\">Image: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1020_Cardiac_Muscle.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenStax</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2 id=\"3-the-cardiac-muscle-action-potential\">3. The cardiac muscle action potential</h2>\n<p>For a ventricular cardiomyocyte to contract, its membrane must first undergo an <strong>action potential</strong>—a temporary change in membrane potential resulting from ionic movements.</p>\n<p>Unlike the brief action potential of skeletal muscle, a ventricular action potential lasts approximately <strong>200–300 milliseconds</strong> and has a prolonged plateau. Its resting membrane potential is approximately <strong>−90 mV</strong>.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Main electrical event</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Phase 0 — Rapid depolarisation</strong></td><td>Fast voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open, producing rapid Na⁺ influx.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Phase 1 — Initial repolarisation</strong></td><td>Fast Na⁺ channels inactivate, while transient outward K⁺ current produces a brief fall in membrane potential.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Phase 2 — Plateau</strong></td><td>Inward Ca²⁺ current through L-type channels partly balances outward K⁺ current.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Phase 3 — Repolarisation</strong></td><td>Ca²⁺ entry declines, and outward K⁺ currents restore the negative membrane potential.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Phase 4 — Resting phase</strong></td><td>A relatively stable membrane potential is maintained largely by resting K⁺ conductance.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"why-does-the-plateau-occur\">Why does the plateau occur?</h3>\n<p>During phase 2, Ca²⁺ entering the cell tends to maintain depolarisation while K⁺ leaving the cell tends to repolarise it. Because these currents partly balance one another, membrane potential changes relatively slowly. The resulting plateau prolongs the action potential and refractory period, while the Ca²⁺ entering through L-type channels also helps trigger contraction.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs02-figure-02\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/26/2026_Action_Potential_Heart_Contraction.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/26/2026_Action_Potential_Heart_Contraction.jpg\" alt=\"Ventricular action potential and refractory period with a comparison of skeletal and cardiac muscle electrical and mechanical activity.\" width=\"1837\" height=\"2019\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 02. Electrical activity, refractoriness and contraction.</strong> Compare the long cardiac refractory period with the mechanical twitch; the graph does not separately number all five phases. <span class=\"figure-credit\">Image: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2026_Action_Potential_Heart_Contraction.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenStax College</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p><strong>Important distinction:</strong> This is a ventricular <em>contractile-cell</em> action potential. Sinoatrial nodal pacemaker cells have no stable resting membrane potential, and their phase 0 upstroke relies primarily on Ca²⁺ entry rather than fast Na⁺ entry. Their behaviour belongs in the Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity chapter.</p>\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"v7Q9BrNfIpQ\" data-media-title=\"Cardiac Action Potential — Alila Medical Media\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO</span><h3>Cardiac Action Potential — Alila Medical Media</h3><p>Revisit the ventricular action potential: identify the fast Na⁺ upstroke, Ca²⁺-dependent plateau and prolonged refractory period. The chapter text above remains the complete explanation.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Watch video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v7Q9BrNfIpQ\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not downloaded or rehosted.</p></div>\n<h2 id=\"4-excitationcontraction-coupling-from-electrical-signal-to-force\">4. Excitation–contraction coupling: from electrical signal to force</h2>\n<p>An action potential must initiate changes inside the cardiomyocyte that activate the contractile proteins <strong>(actin and myosin)</strong>. This is <strong><span data-term=\"ecc\">excitation–contraction coupling (ECC)</span></strong>, and its essential link is <strong>Ca²⁺</strong>.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>The action potential reaches the T-tubules.</strong> Electrical excitation spreads along the sarcolemma and into T-tubules, carrying depolarisation into the cell.</li><li><strong>L-type Ca²⁺ channels open.</strong> Depolarisation permits a small influx of extracellular Ca²⁺ through L-type channels.</li><li><strong>Ca²⁺ triggers further release.</strong> The incoming Ca²⁺ activates <strong>RyR2 (Ryanodine Receptor Type 2)</strong> channels on the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR), releasing much more stored Ca²⁺. This is <strong><span data-term=\"cicr\">CICR (Calcium-Induced Calcium Release)</span></strong>. Entering Ca²⁺ is the trigger; the SR supplies much of the Ca²⁺ that activates contraction.</li><li><strong>Ca²⁺ binds troponin C.</strong> Tropomyosin moves away from the myosin-binding sites on actin.</li><li><strong>Actin–myosin cross-bridge cycling produces force.</strong> Myosin interacts with actin using energy from ATP hydrolysis, shortening sarcomeres and generating contraction.</li></ol>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs02-figure-03\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/10/Cardiac_calcium_cycling_and_excitation-contraction_coupling.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/10/Cardiac_calcium_cycling_and_excitation-contraction_coupling.png\" alt=\"Diagram showing cardiac T-tubule L-type calcium channels, ryanodine receptor, sodium–calcium exchanger, sarcoplasmic reticulum, SERCA and phospholamban.\" width=\"756\" height=\"567\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 03. Cardiac calcium cycling.</strong> L-type channels, RyR, NCX, SERCA and phospholamban are shown; troponin C and actin–myosin interactions are explained in the adjacent text. <span class=\"figure-credit\">Image: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cardiac_calcium_cycling_and_excitation-contraction_coupling.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">PeaBrainC</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h3 id=\"why-is-extracellular-ca-especially-important-in-cardiac-muscle\">Why is extracellular Ca²⁺ especially important in cardiac muscle?</h3>\n<p>In skeletal muscle, an action potential activates voltage-sensitive proteins in the T-tubule membrane. These proteins are physically connected to Ca²⁺-release channels on the SR, so activation directly opens those channels and releases stored Ca²⁺. In other words, skeletal muscle does not first require Ca²⁺ to enter from outside to trigger SR release.</p>\n<p>Cardiac muscle works differently: extracellular Ca²⁺ must enter through L-type channels first, and that incoming Ca²⁺ activates RyR2 channels to release more Ca²⁺ from the SR. <strong>Skeletal muscle uses direct mechanical coupling; cardiac muscle uses CICR.</strong> Reducing Ca²⁺ entry into cardiac muscle therefore generally weakens contraction, even if the SR still contains stored Ca²⁺.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-how-does-cardiac-muscle-relax\">5. How does cardiac muscle relax?</h2>\n<p>The myocardium must relax after each contraction so its chambers can refill. Cytosolic Ca²⁺ must fall: Ca²⁺ dissociates from troponin C, tropomyosin again limits myosin-binding sites on actin, and interactions between the contractile proteins <strong>(actin and myosin)</strong> decrease.</p>\n<p>Two major mechanisms remove cytosolic Ca²⁺:</p>\n<ul><li><strong><span data-term=\"serca\">SERCA2a (Sarco/Endoplasmic Reticulum Calcium ATPase, isoform 2a)</span>:</strong> Uses ATP to pump Ca²⁺ back into the SR for storage and reuse.</li><li><strong><span data-term=\"ncx\">NCX (Na⁺–Ca²⁺ Exchanger)</span>:</strong> Normally uses the Na⁺ gradient to extrude Ca²⁺ from the cell.</li></ul>\n<p>Other pathways, including the plasma membrane Ca²⁺ ATPase, make smaller contributions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-does-impaired-serca2a-activity-matter\">Why does impaired SERCA2a activity matter?</h3>\n<p><strong>Slower relaxation:</strong> When Ca²⁺ is returned to the SR more slowly, cytosolic Ca²⁺ remains elevated longer, delaying relaxation and potentially impairing ventricular filling.</p>\n<p><strong>Potentially weaker later contractions:</strong> Less Ca²⁺ may be stored in the SR for the next heartbeat. Consequently, less Ca²⁺ may be released to activate actin–myosin interactions on subsequent beats.</p>\n<p>Effective pumping therefore depends on <strong>both strong contraction and efficient relaxation</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-why-cant-cardiac-muscle-undergo-normal-tetanus\">6. Why can't cardiac muscle undergo normal tetanus?</h2>\n<p>Skeletal muscle can undergo sustained tetanic contraction because its electrical refractory period is much shorter than its mechanical twitch. Repeated stimuli can arrive while force is still being generated.</p>\n<p>Cardiac muscle has a prolonged action potential and <strong>long refractory period</strong> that overlaps most of its contraction. It cannot normally be re-excited rapidly enough for skeletal-type fused tetanus.</p>\n<p>This protects the heart's rhythmic pumping: if the ventricles stayed contracted continuously, they could not relax, refill and eject blood in successive beats. See Figure 02 for the electrical and mechanical timing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-how-does-sympathetic-stimulation-affect-cardiac-muscle\">7. How does sympathetic stimulation affect cardiac muscle?</h2>\n<p>During exercise or stress, the heart may need to beat faster, contract more strongly and relax more quickly.</p>\n<p>Noradrenaline and adrenaline stimulate <strong>β₁-adrenergic receptors</strong> on cardiomyocytes:</p>\n<p><strong>Noradrenaline/adrenaline → β₁ receptor → Gs protein → adenylyl cyclase → increased cAMP (cyclic adenosine monophosphate) → activation of PKA (Protein Kinase A).</strong></p>\n<p>PKA phosphorylates proteins involved in Ca²⁺ handling, leading to two particularly important effects:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Stronger contraction:</strong> Enhanced L-type Ca²⁺ channel activity increases trigger Ca²⁺ entry, promoting greater SR Ca²⁺ release and stronger actin–myosin interaction.</li><li><strong>Faster relaxation:</strong> PKA phosphorylation of <strong><span data-term=\"phospholamban\">phospholamban</span></strong> reduces its usual inhibition of SERCA2a, allowing Ca²⁺ to return to the SR more rapidly.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"inotropy-lusitropy-and-chronotropy\">Inotropy, lusitropy and chronotropy</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>What it describes</th><th>Positive effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong><span data-term=\"inotropy\">Inotropy</span></strong></td><td>Myocardial contractile strength</td><td>Stronger contraction</td></tr><tr><td><strong><span data-term=\"lusitropy\">Lusitropy</span></strong></td><td>Rate of myocardial relaxation</td><td>Faster relaxation</td></tr><tr><td><strong><span data-term=\"chronotropy\">Chronotropy</span></strong></td><td>Heart rate</td><td>Faster heart rate</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Increased Ca²⁺ availability enhances inotropy, while faster SR Ca²⁺ reuptake improves lusitropy. Sympathetic stimulation of pacemaker cells also increases chronotropy. Together these help the heart meet increased circulatory demands.</p>\n<p><strong>Important distinction:</strong> Increased inotropy is improved intrinsic contractile ability under comparable loading conditions—not simply stronger contraction because the ventricle contains more blood. This becomes important when studying the Frank–Starling mechanism.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-connecting-cardiac-muscle-physiology-to-the-pumping-heart\">8. Connecting cardiac muscle physiology to the pumping heart</h2>\n<h3 id=\"why-doesnt-ventricular-contraction-immediately-mean-ejection\">Why doesn't ventricular contraction immediately mean ejection?</h3>\n<p>Electrical activation initiates contraction, but ventricular pressure must first exceed pressure in the corresponding outflow artery before blood can be ejected. The lecturer notes that right ventricular ejection can begin before left ventricular ejection because pressure in the pulmonary artery is considerably lower than in the aorta.</p>\n<p>Therefore, <strong>the onset of contraction and the onset of ejection are not necessarily simultaneous</strong>. The detailed pressure changes belong in the Cardiac Cycle chapter.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-happens-to-diastole-when-heart-rate-increases\">What happens to diastole when heart rate increases?</h3>\n<p>As heart rate rises, each cardiac cycle becomes shorter. Both systole and diastole can shorten, but <strong>diastole usually shortens proportionately more</strong>. This matters because ventricular filling and much of left ventricular coronary perfusion occur during diastole.</p>\n<p>Moderate increases in heart rate are accommodated by adjustments such as greater contractility and faster relaxation. Excessive tachycardia, however, can reduce filling and myocardial perfusion, so beating more often does not always increase the volume pumped per minute.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. A cardiomyocyte contracts normally, but neighbouring cells fail to activate together. Which intercalated-disc component is most likely affected?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Gap junctions.</strong> They form low-resistance pathways for ionic current to pass directly between neighbouring cardiomyocytes. If this electrical coupling is disrupted, an action potential may not spread reliably, so contractions lose coordination. <strong>Fascia adherens and desmosomes</strong> instead mainly maintain mechanical attachment and transmission of force.</p></details>\n<p><strong>2. Which ion produces the rapid ventricular phase 0 upstroke, and how is this different from the phase 2 plateau?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Phase 0:</strong> fast voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open, producing a large inward Na⁺ current and rapid depolarisation. <strong>Phase 2:</strong> a smaller, sustained inward Ca²⁺ current through L-type channels is roughly balanced by outward K⁺ current, maintaining the plateau. The plateau prolongs electrical activity and helps support Ca²⁺-dependent contraction.</p></details>\n<p><strong>3. What happens if RyR2 channels fail to open despite normal Ca²⁺ entry through L-type channels?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Contraction becomes much weaker.</strong> Ca²⁺ entering through L-type channels is primarily the trigger for opening ryanodine receptor type 2 (RyR2) channels on the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Without that amplified Ca²⁺ release, the cytosolic Ca²⁺ rise is too small to activate the normal number of actin–myosin interactions.</p></details>\n<p><strong>4. Explain how impaired SERCA2a can delay relaxation and weaken later contractions.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Relaxation is delayed</strong> because SERCA2a normally pumps cytosolic Ca²⁺ back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR). If its activity falls, Ca²⁺ remains elevated in the cytosol longer, delaying detachment of Ca²⁺ from troponin. Reduced SR refilling can also leave less Ca²⁺ available for release on later beats, potentially reducing force.</p></details>\n<p><strong>5. Why can skeletal muscle undergo tetanus while cardiac muscle normally cannot?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Skeletal muscle can summate contractions</strong> because its refractory period ends well before a twitch finishes; repeated stimuli can then produce fused tetanus. Cardiac muscle has a prolonged action-potential plateau and refractory period that overlaps most of contraction, preventing a new propagated action potential soon enough for normal tetanic summation. This preserves time for relaxation and filling.</p></details>\n<p><strong>6. How can sympathetic stimulation increase contractile strength and speed of relaxation at the same time?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>β₁-receptor activation → cAMP → protein kinase A (PKA).</strong> PKA promotes L-type Ca²⁺ entry and larger SR Ca²⁺ release, producing a stronger contraction (<em>positive inotropy</em>). PKA also phosphorylates phospholamban, relieving its inhibition of SERCA2a so Ca²⁺ returns to the SR faster (<em>positive lusitropy</em>). The same signal therefore supports both stronger contraction and faster relaxation.</p></details>\n<p><strong>7. Why can extreme tachycardia lower cardiac output despite a faster heart rate?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Cardiac output equals <strong>heart rate × stroke volume (CO = HR × SV)</strong>. Extreme tachycardia shortens diastole substantially, leaving less time for ventricular filling. This can lower end-diastolic volume and stroke volume. If stroke volume decreases proportionally more than heart rate increases, cardiac output falls despite the faster rhythm.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Intercalated discs:</strong> Fascia adherens and desmosomes provide mechanical coupling; gap junctions enable electrical communication.</li><li><strong>Functional syncytium:</strong> Separate cardiomyocytes act as coordinated electrical units; the atria and ventricles form distinct functional syncytia.</li><li><strong>Ventricular action potential:</strong> Phase 0—Na⁺ influx; phase 2—Ca²⁺ influx balanced by K⁺ efflux; phase 3—mainly K⁺-mediated repolarisation.</li><li><strong>Excitation–contraction coupling:</strong> L-type Ca²⁺ entry triggers RyR2-mediated SR release; Ca²⁺ binds troponin C to permit actin–myosin interaction.</li><li><strong>Relaxation:</strong> SERCA2a returns Ca²⁺ to the SR; NCX removes Ca²⁺ from the cell.</li><li><strong>Refractoriness:</strong> The prolonged refractory period prevents normal tetanic contraction and preserves rhythmic pumping.</li><li><strong>Sympathetic effects:</strong> β₁–cAMP–PKA signalling increases contractile strength and speeds relaxation.</li><li><strong>Key terms:</strong> Inotropy is contractile strength; lusitropy is relaxation rate; chronotropy is heart rate.</li><li><strong>Fast heart rates:</strong> Diastole shortens disproportionately, and excessive tachycardia may compromise ventricular filling and coronary perfusion.</li></ol>","plain":"Cardiac Muscle Physiology Lecture: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf — slides 4, 18 and 20. Essential supplementary physiology: cellular structure, action potentials, calcium handling and sympathetic effects. 1. What makes cardiac muscle different? The heart must contract repeatedly throughout life, generating enough force to circulate blood while allowing sufficient time for its chambers to refill between beats. Its cells must respond to electrical stimulation, communicate with neighbouring cells and relax effectively after each contraction. Cardiac muscle is striated and involuntary , with short, branched cells that typically contain one centrally located nucleus. Like skeletal muscle, its contractile proteins are organised into sarcomeres containing actin and myosin. However, cardiac muscle has several distinctive features: Intercalated discs connect neighbouring cardiomyocytes mechanically and electrically. Prolonged action potentials and refractory periods prevent normal tetanic contraction. Calcium-induced calcium release links electrical excitation to contraction. Numerous mitochondria support its continuous demand for ATP. Cardiac muscle exhibits excitability, conductivity and contractility. Automaticity—the ability to generate impulses spontaneously—is particularly important in specialised pacemaker cells rather than every working ventricular myocyte. 2. Intercalated discs: how separate cells function together A single cardiac muscle cell cannot pump blood effectively in isolation. To generate coordinated force, neighbouring cells must remain firmly connected and transmit electrical signals to one another. These connections occur at intercalated discs , which contain three important junctional structures. Structure Role Fascia adherens Anchor actin filaments and transmit contractile force between cells. Desmosomes Hold neighbouring cells together, resisting separation during repeated contractions. Gap junctions Permit ionic current to pass between cells, allowing electrical excitation to spread. When one cardiomyocyte becomes electrically excited, local current passes through gap junctions to neighbouring cells, bringing their membranes towards threshold and propagating the action potential. Fascia adherens and desmosomes maintain mechanical continuity as those cells contract. Thus, cardiac muscle behaves as a functional syncytium : many separate cells act together as a coordinated unit. The atria and ventricles form two electrically distinct functional syncytia. Their usual connection is through the specialised atrioventricular conducting pathway, allowing atrial contraction to precede ventricular contraction. Figure 01. Cardiac muscle and intercalated discs. Desmosomes and gap junctions are labelled; fascia adherens are discussed in the text but not separately labelled. Image: OpenStax · CC BY 4.0 · unmodified. 3. The cardiac muscle action potential For a ventricular cardiomyocyte to contract, its membrane must first undergo an action potential —a temporary change in membrane potential resulting from ionic movements. Unlike the brief action potential of skeletal muscle, a ventricular action potential lasts approximately 200–300 milliseconds and has a prolonged plateau. Its resting membrane potential is approximately −90 mV . Phase Main electrical event Phase 0 — Rapid depolarisation Fast voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open, producing rapid Na⁺ influx. Phase 1 — Initial repolarisation Fast Na⁺ channels inactivate, while transient outward K⁺ current produces a brief fall in membrane potential. Phase 2 — Plateau Inward Ca²⁺ current through L-type channels partly balances outward K⁺ current. Phase 3 — Repolarisation Ca²⁺ entry declines, and outward K⁺ currents restore the negative membrane potential. Phase 4 — Resting phase A relatively stable membrane potential is maintained largely by resting K⁺ conductance. Why does the plateau occur? During phase 2, Ca²⁺ entering the cell tends to maintain depolarisation while K⁺ leaving the cell tends to repolarise it. Because these currents partly balance one another, membrane potential changes relatively slowly. The resulting plateau prolongs the action potential and refractory period, while the Ca²⁺ entering through L-type channels also helps trigger contraction. Figure 02. Electrical activity, refractoriness and contraction. Compare the long cardiac refractory period with the mechanical twitch; the graph does not separately number all five phases. Image: OpenStax College · CC BY 3.0 · unmodified. Important distinction: This is a ventricular contractile-cell action potential. Sinoatrial nodal pacemaker cells have no stable resting membrane potential, and their phase 0 upstroke relies primarily on Ca²⁺ entry rather than fast Na⁺ entry. Their behaviour belongs in the Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity chapter. OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO Cardiac Action Potential — Alila Medical Media Revisit the ventricular action potential: identify the fast Na⁺ upstroke, Ca²⁺-dependent plateau and prolonged refractory period. The chapter text above remains the complete explanation. Watch video Open on YouTube © Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not downloaded or rehosted. 4. Excitation–contraction coupling: from electrical signal to force An action potential must initiate changes inside the cardiomyocyte that activate the contractile proteins (actin and myosin) . This is excitation–contraction coupling (ECC) , and its essential link is Ca²⁺ . The action potential reaches the T-tubules. Electrical excitation spreads along the sarcolemma and into T-tubules, carrying depolarisation into the cell. L-type Ca²⁺ channels open. Depolarisation permits a small influx of extracellular Ca²⁺ through L-type channels. Ca²⁺ triggers further release. The incoming Ca²⁺ activates RyR2 (Ryanodine Receptor Type 2) channels on the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR), releasing much more stored Ca²⁺. This is CICR (Calcium-Induced Calcium Release) . Entering Ca²⁺ is the trigger; the SR supplies much of the Ca²⁺ that activates contraction. Ca²⁺ binds troponin C. Tropomyosin moves away from the myosin-binding sites on actin. Actin–myosin cross-bridge cycling produces force. Myosin interacts with actin using energy from ATP hydrolysis, shortening sarcomeres and generating contraction. Figure 03. Cardiac calcium cycling. L-type channels, RyR, NCX, SERCA and phospholamban are shown; troponin C and actin–myosin interactions are explained in the adjacent text. Image: PeaBrainC · CC BY-SA 4.0 · unmodified. Why is extracellular Ca²⁺ especially important in cardiac muscle? In skeletal muscle, an action potential activates voltage-sensitive proteins in the T-tubule membrane. These proteins are physically connected to Ca²⁺-release channels on the SR, so activation directly opens those channels and releases stored Ca²⁺. In other words, skeletal muscle does not first require Ca²⁺ to enter from outside to trigger SR release. Cardiac muscle works differently: extracellular Ca²⁺ must enter through L-type channels first, and that incoming Ca²⁺ activates RyR2 channels to release more Ca²⁺ from the SR. Skeletal muscle uses direct mechanical coupling; cardiac muscle uses CICR. Reducing Ca²⁺ entry into cardiac muscle therefore generally weakens contraction, even if the SR still contains stored Ca²⁺. 5. How does cardiac muscle relax? The myocardium must relax after each contraction so its chambers can refill. Cytosolic Ca²⁺ must fall: Ca²⁺ dissociates from troponin C, tropomyosin again limits myosin-binding sites on actin, and interactions between the contractile proteins (actin and myosin) decrease. Two major mechanisms remove cytosolic Ca²⁺: SERCA2a (Sarco/Endoplasmic Reticulum Calcium ATPase, isoform 2a) : Uses ATP to pump Ca²⁺ back into the SR for storage and reuse. NCX (Na⁺–Ca²⁺ Exchanger) : Normally uses the Na⁺ gradient to extrude Ca²⁺ from the cell. Other pathways, including the plasma membrane Ca²⁺ ATPase, make smaller contributions. Why does impaired SERCA2a activity matter? Slower relaxation: When Ca²⁺ is returned to the SR more slowly, cytosolic Ca²⁺ remains elevated longer, delaying relaxation and potentially impairing ventricular filling. Potentially weaker later contractions: Less Ca²⁺ may be stored in the SR for the next heartbeat. Consequently, less Ca²⁺ may be released to activate actin–myosin interactions on subsequent beats. Effective pumping therefore depends on both strong contraction and efficient relaxation . 6. Why can't cardiac muscle undergo normal tetanus? Skeletal muscle can undergo sustained tetanic contraction because its electrical refractory period is much shorter than its mechanical twitch. Repeated stimuli can arrive while force is still being generated. Cardiac muscle has a prolonged action potential and long refractory period that overlaps most of its contraction. It cannot normally be re-excited rapidly enough for skeletal-type fused tetanus. This protects the heart's rhythmic pumping: if the ventricles stayed contracted continuously, they could not relax, refill and eject blood in successive beats. See Figure 02 for the electrical and mechanical timing. 7. How does sympathetic stimulation affect cardiac muscle? During exercise or stress, the heart may need to beat faster, contract more strongly and relax more quickly. Noradrenaline and adrenaline stimulate β₁-adrenergic receptors on cardiomyocytes: Noradrenaline/adrenaline → β₁ receptor → Gs protein → adenylyl cyclase → increased cAMP (cyclic adenosine monophosphate) → activation of PKA (Protein Kinase A). PKA phosphorylates proteins involved in Ca²⁺ handling, leading to two particularly important effects: Stronger contraction: Enhanced L-type Ca²⁺ channel activity increases trigger Ca²⁺ entry, promoting greater SR Ca²⁺ release and stronger actin–myosin interaction. Faster relaxation: PKA phosphorylation of phospholamban reduces its usual inhibition of SERCA2a, allowing Ca²⁺ to return to the SR more rapidly. Inotropy, lusitropy and chronotropy Term What it describes Positive effect Inotropy Myocardial contractile strength Stronger contraction Lusitropy Rate of myocardial relaxation Faster relaxation Chronotropy Heart rate Faster heart rate Increased Ca²⁺ availability enhances inotropy, while faster SR Ca²⁺ reuptake improves lusitropy. Sympathetic stimulation of pacemaker cells also increases chronotropy. Together these help the heart meet increased circulatory demands. Important distinction: Increased inotropy is improved intrinsic contractile ability under comparable loading conditions—not simply stronger contraction because the ventricle contains more blood. This becomes important when studying the Frank–Starling mechanism. 8. Connecting cardiac muscle physiology to the pumping heart Why doesn't ventricular contraction immediately mean ejection? Electrical activation initiates contraction, but ventricular pressure must first exceed pressure in the corresponding outflow artery before blood can be ejected. The lecturer notes that right ventricular ejection can begin before left ventricular ejection because pressure in the pulmonary artery is considerably lower than in the aorta. Therefore, the onset of contraction and the onset of ejection are not necessarily simultaneous . The detailed pressure changes belong in the Cardiac Cycle chapter. What happens to diastole when heart rate increases? As heart rate rises, each cardiac cycle becomes shorter. Both systole and diastole can shorten, but diastole usually shortens proportionately more . This matters because ventricular filling and much of left ventricular coronary perfusion occur during diastole. Moderate increases in heart rate are accommodated by adjustments such as greater contractility and faster relaxation. Excessive tachycardia, however, can reduce filling and myocardial perfusion, so beating more often does not always increase the volume pumped per minute. Understanding Check 1. A cardiomyocyte contracts normally, but neighbouring cells fail to activate together. Which intercalated-disc component is most likely affected? Reveal explanation Gap junctions. They form low-resistance pathways for ionic current to pass directly between neighbouring cardiomyocytes. If this electrical coupling is disrupted, an action potential may not spread reliably, so contractions lose coordination. Fascia adherens and desmosomes instead mainly maintain mechanical attachment and transmission of force. 2. Which ion produces the rapid ventricular phase 0 upstroke, and how is this different from the phase 2 plateau? Reveal explanation Phase 0: fast voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open, producing a large inward Na⁺ current and rapid depolarisation. Phase 2: a smaller, sustained inward Ca²⁺ current through L-type channels is roughly balanced by outward K⁺ current, maintaining the plateau. The plateau prolongs electrical activity and helps support Ca²⁺-dependent contraction. 3. What happens if RyR2 channels fail to open despite normal Ca²⁺ entry through L-type channels? Reveal explanation Contraction becomes much weaker. Ca²⁺ entering through L-type channels is primarily the trigger for opening ryanodine receptor type 2 (RyR2) channels on the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Without that amplified Ca²⁺ release, the cytosolic Ca²⁺ rise is too small to activate the normal number of actin–myosin interactions. 4. Explain how impaired SERCA2a can delay relaxation and weaken later contractions. Reveal explanation Relaxation is delayed because SERCA2a normally pumps cytosolic Ca²⁺ back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR). If its activity falls, Ca²⁺ remains elevated in the cytosol longer, delaying detachment of Ca²⁺ from troponin. Reduced SR refilling can also leave less Ca²⁺ available for release on later beats, potentially reducing force. 5. Why can skeletal muscle undergo tetanus while cardiac muscle normally cannot? Reveal explanation Skeletal muscle can summate contractions because its refractory period ends well before a twitch finishes; repeated stimuli can then produce fused tetanus. Cardiac muscle has a prolonged action-potential plateau and refractory period that overlaps most of contraction, preventing a new propagated action potential soon enough for normal tetanic summation. This preserves time for relaxation and filling. 6. How can sympathetic stimulation increase contractile strength and speed of relaxation at the same time? Reveal explanation β₁-receptor activation → cAMP → protein kinase A (PKA). PKA promotes L-type Ca²⁺ entry and larger SR Ca²⁺ release, producing a stronger contraction ( positive inotropy ). PKA also phosphorylates phospholamban, relieving its inhibition of SERCA2a so Ca²⁺ returns to the SR faster ( positive lusitropy ). The same signal therefore supports both stronger contraction and faster relaxation. 7. Why can extreme tachycardia lower cardiac output despite a faster heart rate? Reveal explanation Cardiac output equals heart rate × stroke volume (CO = HR × SV) . Extreme tachycardia shortens diastole substantially, leaving less time for ventricular filling. This can lower end-diastolic volume and stroke volume. If stroke volume decreases proportionally more than heart rate increases, cardiac output falls despite the faster rhythm. Chapter Essentials Intercalated discs: Fascia adherens and desmosomes provide mechanical coupling; gap junctions enable electrical communication. Functional syncytium: Separate cardiomyocytes act as coordinated electrical units; the atria and ventricles form distinct functional syncytia. Ventricular action potential: Phase 0—Na⁺ influx; phase 2—Ca²⁺ influx balanced by K⁺ efflux; phase 3—mainly K⁺-mediated repolarisation. Excitation–contraction coupling: L-type Ca²⁺ entry triggers RyR2-mediated SR release; Ca²⁺ binds troponin C to permit actin–myosin interaction. Relaxation: SERCA2a returns Ca²⁺ to the SR; NCX removes Ca²⁺ from the cell. Refractoriness: The prolonged refractory period prevents normal tetanic contraction and preserves rhythmic pumping. Sympathetic effects: β₁–cAMP–PKA signalling increases contractile strength and speeds relaxation. Key terms: Inotropy is contractile strength; lusitropy is relaxation rate; chronotropy is heart rate. Fast heart rates: Diastole shortens disproportionately, and excessive tachycardia may compromise ventricular filling and coronary perfusion."},{"slug":"cardiac-conduction","title":"Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"7-10","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-does-the-heart-need-a-conducting-system","title":"1. Why does the heart need a conducting system?"},{"id":"2-the-pathway-of-cardiac-electrical-conduction","title":"2. The pathway of cardiac electrical conduction"},{"id":"3-why-is-the-sa-node-the-normal-pacemaker","title":"3. Why is the SA node the normal pacemaker?"},{"id":"4-if-other-conducting-cells-can-fire-spontaneously-why-doesnt-each-set-its-own-rhythm","title":"4. If other conducting cells can fire spontaneously, why doesn't each set its own rhythm?"},{"id":"5-why-does-the-av-node-delay-electrical-conduction","title":"5. Why does the AV node delay electrical conduction?"},{"id":"6-why-is-ventricular-conduction-so-rapid","title":"6. Why is ventricular conduction so rapid?"},{"id":"7-how-does-the-autonomic-nervous-system-influence-pacemaker-activity","title":"7. How does the autonomic nervous system influence pacemaker activity?"},{"id":"8-clinical-significance-of-conduction-disturbances","title":"8. Clinical significance of conduction disturbances"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1 id=\"cardiac-conduction-and-automaticity\">Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity</h1>\n<p><strong>Lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 7–10. <strong>Supplementary foundations:</strong> pacemaker action potentials, autonomic modulation and clinical consequences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-the-heart-need-a-conducting-system\">1. Why does the heart need a conducting system?</h2>\n<p>The heart has four chambers, but efficient pumping requires more than all four chambers contracting: <strong>they must contract in the correct sequence</strong>.</p>\n<p>The atria must be electrically activated before the ventricles so atrial contraction can complete the final portion of ventricular filling. The ventricles must then be activated in a coordinated manner to generate pressure for blood ejection.</p>\n<p>The <strong>specialised cardiac conducting system</strong> generates and distributes electrical impulses through the myocardium. Unlike skeletal muscle, the heart does not need a new motor nerve impulse to initiate every beat. Specialised cardiac cells can spontaneously generate electrical impulses—<strong><span data-term=\"automaticity\">automaticity</span></strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-the-pathway-of-cardiac-electrical-conduction\">2. The pathway of cardiac electrical conduction</h2>\n<p><strong>SA node → atrial myocardium → AV node → bundle of His → right and left bundle branches → Purkinje fibres → ventricular myocardium.</strong></p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Location or function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Sinoatrial (SA) node</strong></td><td>Near the junction of the superior vena cava and right atrium; normally initiates each heartbeat.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Atrial conducting pathways</strong></td><td>Distribute excitation through both atria and towards the AV node.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Atrioventricular (AV) node</strong></td><td>Lower right atrial septal region, near the coronary sinus opening and septal tricuspid leaflet; slows conduction before ventricular activation.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bundle of His</strong></td><td>Conducts impulses from the AV node into the interventricular septum.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Right and left bundle branches</strong></td><td>Carry excitation towards their respective ventricles.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Purkinje fibres</strong></td><td>Rapidly distribute excitation through the ventricular myocardium.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs03-figure-01\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/2018_Conduction_System_of_Heart.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/2018_Conduction_System_of_Heart.jpg\" alt=\"Sectioned heart showing the SA node, atrial pathways including Bachmann's bundle, AV node, His bundle, bundle branches and Purkinje fibres.\" width=\"2021\" height=\"1354\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 01. Specialised cardiac conducting system.</strong> Follow the impulse from the right atrial SA node into the His–Purkinje network. The three internodal pathways shown are traditional teaching descriptions, not necessarily three separately insulated bundles. <span class=\"figure-credit\">Image: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2018_Conduction_System_of_Heart.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenStax College</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h3 id=\"what-about-the-internodal-pathways\">What about the internodal pathways?</h3>\n<p>The lecture names three traditional internodal pathways: <strong>anterior</strong> (associated with Bachmann's bundle), <strong>middle</strong> (Wenckebach) and <strong>posterior</strong> (Thorel). These names help follow the lecturer's slides. However, the atria do not necessarily contain three separate, insulated conducting bundles corresponding exactly to these pathways; excitation also spreads through ordinary atrial myocardium. <strong>Bachmann's bundle</strong> is particularly important for spreading excitation from the right to the left atrium, allowing the atria to be activated close together in time.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-is-the-sa-node-the-normal-pacemaker\">3. Why is the SA node the normal pacemaker?</h2>\n<p>The SA node normally sets the rhythm because its cells spontaneously reach threshold faster than other potential pacemaker regions.</p>\n<p>An ordinary ventricular contractile cell has a relatively stable resting membrane potential and usually requires an incoming impulse for a new action potential. <strong>SA nodal cells lack a stable resting membrane potential</strong>: after repolarisation, the membrane gradually becomes less negative until it reaches threshold. This gradual rise is <strong><span data-term=\"pacemaker-potential\">spontaneous diastolic depolarisation (the pacemaker potential)</span></strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"how-does-the-sa-nodal-action-potential-occur\">How does the SA nodal action potential occur?</h3>\n<p><strong>Phase 4 — Spontaneous diastolic depolarisation:</strong> After repolarisation, the negative SA nodal membrane potential activates <strong>HCN (hyperpolarisation-activated cyclic nucleotide-gated) channels</strong>. These carry the <strong><span data-term=\"funny-current\">funny current (I_f)</span></strong>. Although hyperpolarisation <em>opens</em> the channels, the current they produce at typical SA nodal potentials is net inward, bringing positive charge into the cell and gradually moving membrane potential towards threshold. A reduction in outward K⁺ current and inward Ca²⁺ currents also contribute.</p>\n<p><strong>Phase 0 — Depolarisation:</strong> On reaching threshold, mainly <strong>L-type Ca²⁺ channels</strong> open. This differs from working ventricular cardiomyocytes, whose rapid phase 0 depends primarily on fast Na⁺ channels.</p>\n<p><strong>Phase 3 — Repolarisation:</strong> Ca²⁺ channels inactivate and outward K⁺ currents restore a negative membrane potential. The next pacemaker cycle can then begin.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs03-figure-02\">\n<a href=\"https://www.mdpi.com/cells/cells-13-00004/article_deploy/html/images/cells-13-00004-g001.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://www.mdpi.com/cells/cells-13-00004/article_deploy/html/images/cells-13-00004-g001.png\" alt=\"Side-by-side SA nodal pacemaker and ventricular cardiomyocyte action potentials: spontaneous phase 4 depolarisation and calcium upstroke in nodal cells contrasted with sodium upstroke and plateau in ventricular cells, above an ECG.\" width=\"1500\" height=\"1125\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 02. Pacemaker versus ventricular action potentials.</strong> Compare the spontaneously rising phase 4 of the SA node with the stable resting potential, fast phase 0 and phase 2 plateau of the ventricular cardiomyocyte. The lower ECG trace is supplementary and will be explained later. <span class=\"figure-credit\">Image: <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3390/cells13010004\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Jonker, Barnett, Boink &amp; Christoffels, <em>Cells</em> (2024), Figure 1</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2 id=\"4-if-other-conducting-cells-can-fire-spontaneously-why-doesnt-each-set-its-own-rhythm\">4. If other conducting cells can fire spontaneously, why doesn't each set its own rhythm?</h2>\n<p>The SA node is not the only region with automaticity. Other specialised conducting cells can also generate impulses, but their inherent rates are generally slower.</p>\n<p>The SA node normally reaches threshold <strong>before</strong> these slower pacemaker regions. When an impulse reaches them, it activates and resets their electrical activity, preventing them from independently determining the rhythm. <strong>The SA node dominates because it normally fires faster, not because the other cells have lost automaticity.</strong></p>\n<p>A related mechanism is <strong><span data-term=\"overdrive-suppression\">overdrive suppression</span></strong>, in which sustained faster activation suppresses subsidiary automaticity, sometimes delaying an escape rhythm after faster pacing stops. Resetting by incoming impulses and overdrive suppression are related but not identical.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-if-the-sa-node-fails\">What if the SA node fails?</h3>\n<p>A slower subsidiary pacemaker may take over:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Pacemaker region</th><th>Typical rate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SA node</td><td>Normal adult resting sinus rate: 60–100 beats/min</td></tr><tr><td>AV junction</td><td>Junctional escape rhythm: approximately 40–60 beats/min</td></tr><tr><td>Ventricular pacemaker</td><td>Ventricular escape rhythm: approximately 20–40 beats/min</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>These ranges are guides, not fixed values. A subsidiary pacemaker can preserve ventricular activity if the normal pacemaker or its conduction fails, but slower rate and altered coordination may reduce pumping efficiency.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-why-does-the-av-node-delay-electrical-conduction\">5. Why does the AV node delay electrical conduction?</h2>\n<p>The AV node conducts relatively slowly, partly because nodal action potential upstrokes are Ca²⁺-dependent and cell-to-cell conduction is less rapid than in the His–Purkinje system.</p>\n<p>Why is the delay useful? <strong>The atria must complete their contraction before the ventricles contract.</strong> After atrial activation, atrial contraction pushes the final portion of blood into the ventricles. The AV node briefly delays the impulse so this contribution to ventricular filling can occur before ventricular systole begins. Without an appropriate delay, excessive overlap of atrial and ventricular contractions could reduce the effectiveness of atrial contribution.</p>\n<p>Importantly, <strong>most ventricular filling occurs passively</strong> before atrial systole. Atrial contraction normally contributes the remaining portion; it does not provide most of the blood entering the ventricles.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-doesnt-the-impulse-simply-spread-from-atria-to-ventricles\">Why doesn't the impulse simply spread from atria to ventricles?</h3>\n<p>The cardiac fibrous skeleton electrically insulates most atrial myocardium from ventricular myocardium. Under normal conditions, impulses cross via the specialised AV conducting pathway, preserving the atrial-to-ventricular activation sequence.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-why-is-ventricular-conduction-so-rapid\">6. Why is ventricular conduction so rapid?</h2>\n<p>After the AV node, the impulse enters the <strong>His–Purkinje system</strong>, where rapid Na⁺-dependent depolarisation and effective cell-to-cell coupling permit fast conduction. The Purkinje network distributes excitation widely through the ventricles so large regions of myocardium become activated within a short period. This lets the ventricles develop pressure and eject blood efficiently.</p>\n<p>Activation normally begins in regions of the interventricular septum, then spreads through the Purkinje network to the ventricular endocardium and through the working myocardium. The precise sequence is more complex than a single wave travelling upwards from the apex; its ECG implications are discussed later.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-should-conduction-be-slow-in-one-region-and-fast-in-another\">Why should conduction be slow in one region and fast in another?</h3>\n<p><strong>The AV node conducts slowly</strong>, briefly delaying ventricular activation so the atria can complete their contraction and deliver the final portion of blood into the ventricles.</p>\n<p><strong>The His–Purkinje network then conducts rapidly</strong>, distributing excitation throughout both ventricles within a short time so that the ventricular myocardium generates pressure and ejects blood effectively.</p>\n<p>In short, **slow AV nodal conduction provides correct <em>timing between chambers</em>, while rapid His–Purkinje conduction provides coordinated <em>activation within the ventricles</em>.**</p>\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"ScZVGHDisa0\" data-media-title=\"Cardiac Conduction and ECG — Alila Medical Media\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO</span><h3>Cardiac Conduction and ECG — Alila Medical Media</h3><p>Trace SA node → AV node → His–Purkinje fibres, and notice why atrial activation precedes ventricular activation. ECG details are supplementary. The chapter text above remains the complete explanation.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Watch video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ScZVGHDisa0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not downloaded or rehosted.</p></div>\n<h2 id=\"7-how-does-the-autonomic-nervous-system-influence-pacemaker-activity\">7. How does the autonomic nervous system influence pacemaker activity?</h2>\n<p>The SA node is automatic, but its firing rate is modifiable. The key relationship is simple: <strong>the faster phase 4 reaches threshold, the sooner the next action potential occurs</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sympathetic-stimulation\">Sympathetic stimulation</h3>\n<p>Noradrenaline activates <strong>β₁-adrenergic receptors</strong>, increasing cAMP-mediated signalling in pacemaker cells. This enhances pacemaker currents and generally steepens phase 4 depolarisation. The SA node reaches threshold sooner and fires more frequently, increasing heart rate. Sympathetic stimulation also increases AV nodal conduction.</p>\n<h3 id=\"parasympathetic-stimulation\">Parasympathetic stimulation</h3>\n<p>Vagal acetylcholine activates <strong>M₂ muscarinic receptors</strong>, reducing cAMP-dependent inward currents and increasing K⁺ conductance through acetylcholine-sensitive K⁺ channels. This can make the membrane more negative and slow phase 4 depolarisation. Threshold is reached later, so heart rate falls. AV nodal conduction also slows.</p>\n<ul><li><strong><span data-term=\"chronotropy\">Chronotropy</span>:</strong> Effect on heart rate.</li><li><strong><span data-term=\"dromotropy\">Dromotropy</span>:</strong> Effect on conduction velocity, particularly AV nodal conduction.</li></ul>\n<p>Sympathetic stimulation usually produces positive chronotropy and dromotropy; vagal stimulation produces negative chronotropy and dromotropy.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-clinical-significance-of-conduction-disturbances\">8. Clinical significance of conduction disturbances</h2>\n<p>Electrical dysfunction may occur because the normal pacemaker fails, an impulse is delayed or transmission is interrupted.</p>\n<p><strong>SA nodal dysfunction:</strong> The normal pacemaker fires too slowly or pauses, potentially allowing a subsidiary pacemaker to take over.</p>\n<p><strong>AV conduction block:</strong> Impulses may reach the ventricles more slowly, some may fail to conduct, or atrial-to-ventricular conduction may be completely interrupted. In complete AV block, atria and ventricles may operate under separate pacemakers, with a slower escape rhythm maintaining ventricular activity.</p>\n<p>These disturbances affect effective pumping because both heart rate and the coordination of contraction matter. Their ECG manifestations belong in the ECG chapter.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Why does the SA node dominate even though other conducting cells can generate impulses?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>The SA node normally reaches threshold first.</strong> Its spontaneous phase 4 depolarisation generally produces a faster intrinsic rhythm than that of subsidiary pacemakers. Each SA-initiated impulse arrives before slower cells discharge independently and resets their electrical activity. This is why the SA node usually controls the rhythm, although other pacemakers can take over if it fails.</p></details>\n<p><strong>2. Why does hyperpolarisation activate the funny current even though it helps depolarise the cell?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>HCN channels are activated by sufficiently negative membrane potentials</strong>—hence the name <em>hyperpolarisation-activated</em>. At typical SA nodal voltages, the mixed Na⁺/K⁺ current passing through them is <strong>net inward</strong>, bringing positive charge into the cell. The membrane therefore gradually becomes less negative and moves towards threshold. Channel opening and the direction of the resulting current are different concepts.</p></details>\n<p><strong>3. Why is the SA nodal phase 0 different from that of a ventricular contractile cell?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>SA nodal phase 0 is mainly mediated by L-type Ca²⁺ influx</strong>; working ventricular muscle has a rapid phase 0 dominated by fast voltage-gated Na⁺ influx. The cells express different functional channel patterns and resting membrane properties. Consequently, the SA nodal upstroke is slower than the ventricular contractile-cell upstroke.</p></details>\n<p><strong>4. Why is AV nodal conduction slow but His–Purkinje conduction fast?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>The AV node provides timing:</strong> its slow conduction briefly separates atrial activation from ventricular activation, allowing atrial contraction to complete its contribution to filling. <strong>The His–Purkinje network provides coordinated distribution:</strong> it rapidly excites widely separated ventricular regions so pressure develops efficiently. Delayed chamber-to-chamber conduction and rapid conduction within the ventricles serve different purposes.</p></details>\n<p><strong>5. How does sympathetic stimulation increase heart rate?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Noradrenaline activates <strong>β₁-adrenergic receptors</strong> in pacemaker cells, increasing <strong>cAMP</strong>. This enhances depolarising pacemaker currents (including HCN-mediated I<sub>f</sub> and Ca²⁺ currents), so phase 4 generally rises towards threshold more rapidly. The SA node reaches threshold sooner, fires more often, and <strong>heart rate increases</strong>.</p></details>\n<p><strong>6. Why can vagal stimulation slow an automatically firing SA node?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Automaticity means spontaneous discharge, not a fixed rate.</strong> Vagal acetylcholine acts on M₂ receptors, reducing cAMP-mediated depolarising currents and opening acetylcholine-sensitive K⁺ channels. The membrane can become more negative and phase 4 rises more slowly, so reaching threshold takes longer and the SA node fires less often.</p></details>\n<p><strong>7. If atrial impulses cannot reach the ventricles, must the ventricles stop immediately?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Not necessarily.</strong> In complete AV block, impulses from the atria do not conduct to the ventricles, but a subsidiary junctional or ventricular pacemaker may generate an <em>escape rhythm</em>. This maintains some ventricular contractions independently of the atria; it is often slower and may reduce cardiac output. An escape rhythm is a potential protective response, not a guarantee of adequate circulation.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Normal pathway:</strong> SA node → atrial myocardium → AV node → His bundle → bundle branches → Purkinje fibres → ventricular myocardium.</li><li><strong>SA node:</strong> Normally sets heart rate by reaching threshold sooner than other pacemaker regions.</li><li><strong>Automaticity:</strong> Specialised cardiac cells generate impulses without requiring external nerve stimulation.</li><li><strong>SA nodal action potential:</strong> Phase 4 spontaneous depolarisation; phase 0 mainly Ca²⁺ influx; phase 3 K⁺-mediated repolarisation.</li><li><strong>Funny current:</strong> Hyperpolarisation activates HCN channels, producing a net inward current that contributes to the pacemaker potential.</li><li><strong>AV delay:</strong> Allows atrial contraction to complete its contribution to ventricular filling before ventricular systole.</li><li><strong>His–Purkinje network:</strong> Rapid impulse distribution allows coordinated ventricular activation.</li><li><strong>Subsidiary pacemakers:</strong> Can generate escape rhythms if normal pacemaker activity or conduction fails.</li><li><strong>Autonomic effects:</strong> Sympathetic β₁ stimulation accelerates SA firing and AV conduction; parasympathetic M₂ stimulation slows both.</li><li><strong>Chronotropy versus dromotropy:</strong> Chronotropy affects heart rate; dromotropy affects conduction velocity.</li></ol>","plain":"Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity Lecture: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 7–10. Supplementary foundations: pacemaker action potentials, autonomic modulation and clinical consequences. 1. Why does the heart need a conducting system? The heart has four chambers, but efficient pumping requires more than all four chambers contracting: they must contract in the correct sequence . The atria must be electrically activated before the ventricles so atrial contraction can complete the final portion of ventricular filling. The ventricles must then be activated in a coordinated manner to generate pressure for blood ejection. The specialised cardiac conducting system generates and distributes electrical impulses through the myocardium. Unlike skeletal muscle, the heart does not need a new motor nerve impulse to initiate every beat. Specialised cardiac cells can spontaneously generate electrical impulses— automaticity . 2. The pathway of cardiac electrical conduction SA node → atrial myocardium → AV node → bundle of His → right and left bundle branches → Purkinje fibres → ventricular myocardium. Structure Location or function Sinoatrial (SA) node Near the junction of the superior vena cava and right atrium; normally initiates each heartbeat. Atrial conducting pathways Distribute excitation through both atria and towards the AV node. Atrioventricular (AV) node Lower right atrial septal region, near the coronary sinus opening and septal tricuspid leaflet; slows conduction before ventricular activation. Bundle of His Conducts impulses from the AV node into the interventricular septum. Right and left bundle branches Carry excitation towards their respective ventricles. Purkinje fibres Rapidly distribute excitation through the ventricular myocardium. Figure 01. Specialised cardiac conducting system. Follow the impulse from the right atrial SA node into the His–Purkinje network. The three internodal pathways shown are traditional teaching descriptions, not necessarily three separately insulated bundles. Image: OpenStax College · CC BY 3.0 · unmodified. What about the internodal pathways? The lecture names three traditional internodal pathways: anterior (associated with Bachmann's bundle), middle (Wenckebach) and posterior (Thorel). These names help follow the lecturer's slides. However, the atria do not necessarily contain three separate, insulated conducting bundles corresponding exactly to these pathways; excitation also spreads through ordinary atrial myocardium. Bachmann's bundle is particularly important for spreading excitation from the right to the left atrium, allowing the atria to be activated close together in time. 3. Why is the SA node the normal pacemaker? The SA node normally sets the rhythm because its cells spontaneously reach threshold faster than other potential pacemaker regions. An ordinary ventricular contractile cell has a relatively stable resting membrane potential and usually requires an incoming impulse for a new action potential. SA nodal cells lack a stable resting membrane potential : after repolarisation, the membrane gradually becomes less negative until it reaches threshold. This gradual rise is spontaneous diastolic depolarisation (the pacemaker potential) . How does the SA nodal action potential occur? Phase 4 — Spontaneous diastolic depolarisation: After repolarisation, the negative SA nodal membrane potential activates HCN (hyperpolarisation-activated cyclic nucleotide-gated) channels . These carry the funny current (I_f) . Although hyperpolarisation opens the channels, the current they produce at typical SA nodal potentials is net inward, bringing positive charge into the cell and gradually moving membrane potential towards threshold. A reduction in outward K⁺ current and inward Ca²⁺ currents also contribute. Phase 0 — Depolarisation: On reaching threshold, mainly L-type Ca²⁺ channels open. This differs from working ventricular cardiomyocytes, whose rapid phase 0 depends primarily on fast Na⁺ channels. Phase 3 — Repolarisation: Ca²⁺ channels inactivate and outward K⁺ currents restore a negative membrane potential. The next pacemaker cycle can then begin. Figure 02. Pacemaker versus ventricular action potentials. Compare the spontaneously rising phase 4 of the SA node with the stable resting potential, fast phase 0 and phase 2 plateau of the ventricular cardiomyocyte. The lower ECG trace is supplementary and will be explained later. Image: Jonker, Barnett, Boink Christoffels, Cells (2024), Figure 1 · CC BY 4.0 · unmodified. 4. If other conducting cells can fire spontaneously, why doesn't each set its own rhythm? The SA node is not the only region with automaticity. Other specialised conducting cells can also generate impulses, but their inherent rates are generally slower. The SA node normally reaches threshold before these slower pacemaker regions. When an impulse reaches them, it activates and resets their electrical activity, preventing them from independently determining the rhythm. The SA node dominates because it normally fires faster, not because the other cells have lost automaticity. A related mechanism is overdrive suppression , in which sustained faster activation suppresses subsidiary automaticity, sometimes delaying an escape rhythm after faster pacing stops. Resetting by incoming impulses and overdrive suppression are related but not identical. What if the SA node fails? A slower subsidiary pacemaker may take over: Pacemaker region Typical rate SA node Normal adult resting sinus rate: 60–100 beats/min AV junction Junctional escape rhythm: approximately 40–60 beats/min Ventricular pacemaker Ventricular escape rhythm: approximately 20–40 beats/min These ranges are guides, not fixed values. A subsidiary pacemaker can preserve ventricular activity if the normal pacemaker or its conduction fails, but slower rate and altered coordination may reduce pumping efficiency. 5. Why does the AV node delay electrical conduction? The AV node conducts relatively slowly, partly because nodal action potential upstrokes are Ca²⁺-dependent and cell-to-cell conduction is less rapid than in the His–Purkinje system. Why is the delay useful? The atria must complete their contraction before the ventricles contract. After atrial activation, atrial contraction pushes the final portion of blood into the ventricles. The AV node briefly delays the impulse so this contribution to ventricular filling can occur before ventricular systole begins. Without an appropriate delay, excessive overlap of atrial and ventricular contractions could reduce the effectiveness of atrial contribution. Importantly, most ventricular filling occurs passively before atrial systole. Atrial contraction normally contributes the remaining portion; it does not provide most of the blood entering the ventricles. Why doesn't the impulse simply spread from atria to ventricles? The cardiac fibrous skeleton electrically insulates most atrial myocardium from ventricular myocardium. Under normal conditions, impulses cross via the specialised AV conducting pathway, preserving the atrial-to-ventricular activation sequence. 6. Why is ventricular conduction so rapid? After the AV node, the impulse enters the His–Purkinje system , where rapid Na⁺-dependent depolarisation and effective cell-to-cell coupling permit fast conduction. The Purkinje network distributes excitation widely through the ventricles so large regions of myocardium become activated within a short period. This lets the ventricles develop pressure and eject blood efficiently. Activation normally begins in regions of the interventricular septum, then spreads through the Purkinje network to the ventricular endocardium and through the working myocardium. The precise sequence is more complex than a single wave travelling upwards from the apex; its ECG implications are discussed later. Why should conduction be slow in one region and fast in another? The AV node conducts slowly , briefly delaying ventricular activation so the atria can complete their contraction and deliver the final portion of blood into the ventricles. The His–Purkinje network then conducts rapidly , distributing excitation throughout both ventricles within a short time so that the ventricular myocardium generates pressure and ejects blood effectively. In short, **slow AV nodal conduction provides correct timing between chambers , while rapid His–Purkinje conduction provides coordinated activation within the ventricles .** OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO Cardiac Conduction and ECG — Alila Medical Media Trace SA node → AV node → His–Purkinje fibres, and notice why atrial activation precedes ventricular activation. ECG details are supplementary. The chapter text above remains the complete explanation. Watch video Open on YouTube © Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not downloaded or rehosted. 7. How does the autonomic nervous system influence pacemaker activity? The SA node is automatic, but its firing rate is modifiable. The key relationship is simple: the faster phase 4 reaches threshold, the sooner the next action potential occurs . Sympathetic stimulation Noradrenaline activates β₁-adrenergic receptors , increasing cAMP-mediated signalling in pacemaker cells. This enhances pacemaker currents and generally steepens phase 4 depolarisation. The SA node reaches threshold sooner and fires more frequently, increasing heart rate. Sympathetic stimulation also increases AV nodal conduction. Parasympathetic stimulation Vagal acetylcholine activates M₂ muscarinic receptors , reducing cAMP-dependent inward currents and increasing K⁺ conductance through acetylcholine-sensitive K⁺ channels. This can make the membrane more negative and slow phase 4 depolarisation. Threshold is reached later, so heart rate falls. AV nodal conduction also slows. Chronotropy : Effect on heart rate. Dromotropy : Effect on conduction velocity, particularly AV nodal conduction. Sympathetic stimulation usually produces positive chronotropy and dromotropy; vagal stimulation produces negative chronotropy and dromotropy. 8. Clinical significance of conduction disturbances Electrical dysfunction may occur because the normal pacemaker fails, an impulse is delayed or transmission is interrupted. SA nodal dysfunction: The normal pacemaker fires too slowly or pauses, potentially allowing a subsidiary pacemaker to take over. AV conduction block: Impulses may reach the ventricles more slowly, some may fail to conduct, or atrial-to-ventricular conduction may be completely interrupted. In complete AV block, atria and ventricles may operate under separate pacemakers, with a slower escape rhythm maintaining ventricular activity. These disturbances affect effective pumping because both heart rate and the coordination of contraction matter. Their ECG manifestations belong in the ECG chapter. Understanding Check 1. Why does the SA node dominate even though other conducting cells can generate impulses? Reveal explanation The SA node normally reaches threshold first. Its spontaneous phase 4 depolarisation generally produces a faster intrinsic rhythm than that of subsidiary pacemakers. Each SA-initiated impulse arrives before slower cells discharge independently and resets their electrical activity. This is why the SA node usually controls the rhythm, although other pacemakers can take over if it fails. 2. Why does hyperpolarisation activate the funny current even though it helps depolarise the cell? Reveal explanation HCN channels are activated by sufficiently negative membrane potentials —hence the name hyperpolarisation-activated . At typical SA nodal voltages, the mixed Na⁺/K⁺ current passing through them is net inward , bringing positive charge into the cell. The membrane therefore gradually becomes less negative and moves towards threshold. Channel opening and the direction of the resulting current are different concepts. 3. Why is the SA nodal phase 0 different from that of a ventricular contractile cell? Reveal explanation SA nodal phase 0 is mainly mediated by L-type Ca²⁺ influx ; working ventricular muscle has a rapid phase 0 dominated by fast voltage-gated Na⁺ influx. The cells express different functional channel patterns and resting membrane properties. Consequently, the SA nodal upstroke is slower than the ventricular contractile-cell upstroke. 4. Why is AV nodal conduction slow but His–Purkinje conduction fast? Reveal explanation The AV node provides timing: its slow conduction briefly separates atrial activation from ventricular activation, allowing atrial contraction to complete its contribution to filling. The His–Purkinje network provides coordinated distribution: it rapidly excites widely separated ventricular regions so pressure develops efficiently. Delayed chamber-to-chamber conduction and rapid conduction within the ventricles serve different purposes. 5. How does sympathetic stimulation increase heart rate? Reveal explanation Noradrenaline activates β₁-adrenergic receptors in pacemaker cells, increasing cAMP . This enhances depolarising pacemaker currents (including HCN-mediated I f and Ca²⁺ currents), so phase 4 generally rises towards threshold more rapidly. The SA node reaches threshold sooner, fires more often, and heart rate increases . 6. Why can vagal stimulation slow an automatically firing SA node? Reveal explanation Automaticity means spontaneous discharge, not a fixed rate. Vagal acetylcholine acts on M₂ receptors, reducing cAMP-mediated depolarising currents and opening acetylcholine-sensitive K⁺ channels. The membrane can become more negative and phase 4 rises more slowly, so reaching threshold takes longer and the SA node fires less often. 7. If atrial impulses cannot reach the ventricles, must the ventricles stop immediately? Reveal explanation Not necessarily. In complete AV block, impulses from the atria do not conduct to the ventricles, but a subsidiary junctional or ventricular pacemaker may generate an escape rhythm . This maintains some ventricular contractions independently of the atria; it is often slower and may reduce cardiac output. An escape rhythm is a potential protective response, not a guarantee of adequate circulation. Chapter Essentials Normal pathway: SA node → atrial myocardium → AV node → His bundle → bundle branches → Purkinje fibres → ventricular myocardium. SA node: Normally sets heart rate by reaching threshold sooner than other pacemaker regions. Automaticity: Specialised cardiac cells generate impulses without requiring external nerve stimulation. SA nodal action potential: Phase 4 spontaneous depolarisation; phase 0 mainly Ca²⁺ influx; phase 3 K⁺-mediated repolarisation. Funny current: Hyperpolarisation activates HCN channels, producing a net inward current that contributes to the pacemaker potential. AV delay: Allows atrial contraction to complete its contribution to ventricular filling before ventricular systole. His–Purkinje network: Rapid impulse distribution allows coordinated ventricular activation. Subsidiary pacemakers: Can generate escape rhythms if normal pacemaker activity or conduction fails. Autonomic effects: Sympathetic β₁ stimulation accelerates SA firing and AV conduction; parasympathetic M₂ stimulation slows both. Chronotropy versus dromotropy: Chronotropy affects heart rate; dromotropy affects conduction velocity."},{"slug":"cardiac-cycle","title":"The Cardiac Cycle","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"11-21","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-actually-drives-the-cardiac-cycle","title":"1. What actually drives the cardiac cycle?"},{"id":"2-follow-one-complete-heartbeat","title":"2. Follow one complete heartbeat"},{"id":"3-the-cycle-on-one-compact-map","title":"3. The cycle on one compact map"},{"id":"4-how-much-blood-does-the-ventricle-pump","title":"4. How much blood does the ventricle pump?"},{"id":"5-reading-the-wiggers-diagram-without-memorising-the-curves","title":"5. Reading the Wiggers diagram without memorising the curves"},{"id":"6-what-about-the-right-heart-and-faster-heart-rates","title":"6. What about the right heart and faster heart rates?"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1 id=\"the-cardiac-cycle\">The Cardiac Cycle</h1>\n<p><strong>Lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 11–21. <strong>Supplementary clarification:</strong> pressure-gradient reasoning, Wiggers interpretation and standard ejection-phase terminology.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-actually-drives-the-cardiac-cycle\">1. What actually drives the cardiac cycle?</h2>\n<p>The <strong>cardiac cycle</strong> is one complete sequence of ventricular filling, contraction, ejection and relaxation. To understand it, follow what happens to <strong>pressure</strong>, the <strong>valves</strong> and the <strong>amount of blood in the ventricle</strong>.</p>\n<p>Start with the left heart: <strong>left atrium → mitral valve → left ventricle → aortic valve → aorta</strong>. Blood flows through an open valve when pressure upstream is higher than pressure downstream. Valves open and close <em>passively</em> as these pressures change; they do not receive individual nerve impulses telling them when to move.</p>\n<div class=\"pressure-rules\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Left-heart pressure rules\"><div><strong>Mitral valve opens</strong><span>P<sub>LA</sub> &gt; P<sub>LV</sub></span><small>Blood enters the ventricle</small></div><div><strong>Aortic valve opens</strong><span>P<sub>LV</sub> &gt; P<sub>aorta</sub></span><small>Blood leaves the ventricle</small></div></div>\n<p>Here <strong>P</strong> means pressure, <strong>LA</strong> is left atrium and <strong>LV</strong> is left ventricle. When either pressure difference reverses, the corresponding valve closes. The two valves respond to <em>different</em> pressure differences, which is why <strong>both may be closed at once</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-follow-one-complete-heartbeat\">2. Follow one complete heartbeat</h2>\n<p>We begin with the ventricle relaxed and about to receive blood. Each phase ends only when a change in electrical activity or pressure creates the conditions for the next event.</p>\n<h3 id=\"stage-1-why-does-blood-enter-the-ventricle\">Stage 1 — Why does blood enter the ventricle?</h3>\n<p>During ventricular <strong>diastole</strong>, the myocardium relaxes and ventricular pressure falls. Once P<sub>LV</sub> falls below P<sub>LA</sub>, the <strong>mitral valve opens</strong>. Pulmonary venous blood has continued entering the left atrium while the mitral valve was closed, so blood is available to flow into the ventricle.</p>\n<p>At first, filling is <strong>rapid</strong>: blood moves down the atrial-to-ventricular pressure gradient, and the relaxed, compliant ventricle can accept an increasing volume without much rise in pressure. As filling progresses, the pressure difference narrows and the flow rate slows. This later, slower period is <strong><span data-term=\"diastasis\">diastasis</span></strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>During filling:</strong> mitral valve open; aortic valve closed; ventricular <strong>volume rises</strong>, while pressure remains relatively low.</p>\n<div class=\"transition-line\"><strong>What happens next?</strong> Atrial depolarisation occurs and the atria contract, adding the final portion of ventricular filling.</div>\n<h3 id=\"stage-2-why-contract-the-atria-if-filling-is-already-occurring\">Stage 2 — Why contract the atria if filling is already occurring?</h3>\n<p>Most filling normally occurs <strong>passively</strong> before atrial contraction. Near the end of diastole, atrial systole pushes an additional volume into the ventricle—the <strong><span data-term=\"atrial-kick\">atrial kick</span></strong>. It matters especially when a ventricle is stiff or relaxes poorly.</p>\n<p>The lecturer illustrates approximately <strong>70% passive filling and 30% atrial contribution</strong>. These are teaching estimates, not constant percentages: atrial contraction often contributes roughly <strong>10–30%</strong> at rest, depending on age, rate and ventricular properties.</p>\n<p>The <strong>P wave</strong> is atrial depolarisation; atrial contraction follows and produces the <strong>a wave</strong> in the atrial pressure tracing. After filling ends, the ventricle contains its <strong><span data-term=\"edv\">end-diastolic volume (EDV)</span></strong>—approximately 130 mL in the lecturer's example.</p>\n<div class=\"transition-line\"><strong>What happens next?</strong> Ventricular depolarisation (QRS complex) initiates contraction and raises ventricular pressure.</div>\n<h3 id=\"stage-3-why-doesnt-ventricular-contraction-immediately-eject-blood\">Stage 3 — Why doesn't ventricular contraction immediately eject blood?</h3>\n<p>As ventricular contraction starts, P<sub>LV</sub> increases. Once it exceeds P<sub>LA</sub>, the <strong>mitral valve closes</strong>, contributing to the first heart sound (<strong>S1</strong>). Yet P<sub>LV</sub> is still <em>below aortic pressure</em>, so the <strong>aortic valve stays closed</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Both valves are closed.</strong> The ventricle develops force, but blood cannot enter or escape. Thus <strong>pressure rises rapidly while ventricular volume remains at EDV</strong>. This is <strong><span data-term=\"isovolumetric\">isovolumetric contraction</span></strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"mechanism-strip\"><span><strong>Mitral</strong> closed</span><span><strong>Aortic</strong> closed</span><span><strong>Pressure</strong> ↑</span><span><strong>Volume</strong> = EDV</span></div>\n<p>Contraction does <em>not</em> necessarily mean ejection: the ventricle must first overcome arterial pressure. With aortic diastolic pressure around <strong>80 mmHg</strong>, for example, left ventricular pressure must rise above that level to open the aortic valve. The lecturer uses approximately <strong>10 mmHg</strong> for pulmonary arterial diastolic pressure as a right-heart comparison; these are illustrative values.</p>\n<div class=\"transition-line\"><strong>What ends this phase?</strong> P<sub>LV</sub> exceeds P<sub>aorta</sub> → the aortic valve opens.</div>\n<h3 id=\"stage-4-how-does-blood-begin-leaving-the-ventricle\">Stage 4 — How does blood begin leaving the ventricle?</h3>\n<p>Once P<sub>LV</sub> exceeds aortic pressure, the <strong>aortic valve opens</strong>. Blood now enters the aorta, so <strong>ventricular volume decreases</strong>.</p>\n<p>Initially, a high rate of outflow produces <strong>rapid ejection</strong>. Later, outflow decreases as ventricular force and the pressure conditions change; this is <strong>reduced ejection</strong>. Ventricular pressure may continue <em>rising at first</em> after the valve opens, even though ventricular volume is already falling; the two measurements need not move in the same direction.</p>\n<p>The lecturer describes ejection as initially slow then rapid; the conventional physiological subdivision is <strong>rapid ejection followed by reduced ejection</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>During ejection:</strong> mitral valve closed; aortic valve open; blood leaves and ventricular volume falls. The blood remaining at the end is <strong><span data-term=\"esv\">end-systolic volume (ESV)</span></strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"transition-line\"><strong>What happens next?</strong> Ventricular force declines; ventricular pressure begins to fall.</div>\n<h3 id=\"stage-5-why-does-the-aortic-valve-close\">Stage 5 — Why does the aortic valve close?</h3>\n<p>As the ventricle begins to relax, its pressure falls. Blood may still be flowing forward late in ejection, but eventually <strong>aortic pressure exceeds ventricular pressure</strong>. The pressure gradient reverses and the <strong>aortic valve closes</strong>, contributing to <strong>S2</strong> (the second heart sound).</p>\n<p>The lecturer calls the brief period of falling ventricular pressure before semilunar valve closure <strong>protodiastole</strong>. It precedes the following isovolumetric relaxation phase.</p>\n<div class=\"transition-line\"><strong>What happens next?</strong> The aortic valve has closed, but the mitral valve is not yet open.</div>\n<h3 id=\"stage-6-why-doesnt-filling-restart-immediately\">Stage 6 — Why doesn't filling restart immediately?</h3>\n<p>Although the aortic valve is now closed, P<sub>LV</sub> is still above P<sub>LA</sub>, keeping the <strong>mitral valve closed</strong>. The ventricle relaxes further, so pressure drops quickly, but neither valve is open and <strong>volume remains at ESV</strong>. This is <strong>isovolumetric relaxation</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"mechanism-strip\"><span><strong>Mitral</strong> closed</span><span><strong>Aortic</strong> closed</span><span><strong>Pressure</strong> ↓</span><span><strong>Volume</strong> = ESV</span></div>\n<p>When P<sub>LV</sub> finally falls below P<sub>LA</sub>, the mitral valve <strong>reopens</strong>. Blood enters and a <strong>new cardiac cycle begins</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"compare-the-two-isovolumetric-phases\">Compare the two isovolumetric phases</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Isovolumetric contraction</th><th>Isovolumetric relaxation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Muscle</td><td>Developing force</td><td>Relaxing</td></tr><tr><td>Mitral and aortic valves</td><td>Both closed</td><td>Both closed</td></tr><tr><td>Ventricular pressure</td><td>Increasing ↑</td><td>Decreasing ↓</td></tr><tr><td>Ventricular volume</td><td>Constant at <strong>EDV</strong></td><td>Constant at <strong>ESV</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Ends when</td><td>Aortic valve opens</td><td>Mitral valve opens</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Why is volume constant in both?</strong> Neither valve is open. <strong>Why does pressure change in opposite directions?</strong> The myocardium is generating force in one phase and relaxing in the other.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-the-cycle-on-one-compact-map\">3. The cycle on one compact map</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>What changes?</th><th>Immediate consequence</th><th>Next phase</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure</td><td>Mitral valve opens</td><td>Filling</td></tr><tr><td>Atria depolarise</td><td>Atrial contraction adds final blood volume</td><td>Atrial systole</td></tr><tr><td>Ventricular pressure exceeds atrial pressure</td><td>Mitral valve closes (S1)</td><td>Isovolumetric contraction</td></tr><tr><td>Ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure</td><td>Aortic valve opens</td><td>Ejection</td></tr><tr><td>Aortic pressure exceeds ventricular pressure</td><td>Aortic valve closes (S2)</td><td>Isovolumetric relaxation</td></tr><tr><td>Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure</td><td>Mitral valve reopens</td><td>Next filling phase</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>This map is for <strong>revision after</strong> the explanation: its purpose is to reconstruct each transition, not to replace the reasoning behind it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-how-much-blood-does-the-ventricle-pump\">4. How much blood does the ventricle pump?</h2>\n<p>A ventricle does not empty completely during systole. <strong>EDV</strong> is the blood it contains immediately before contraction; <strong>ESV</strong> is what remains after ejection. Their difference is the <strong>stroke volume (SV)</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Stroke volume</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mi>SV</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>EDV</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ESV</mi></math><span class=\"equation-example\">Example: 130 mL − 50 mL = <strong>80 mL</strong></span></div>\n<p>The <strong><span data-term=\"ejection-fraction\">ejection fraction (EF)</span></strong> is the <em>fraction of EDV</em> ejected, expressed as a percentage.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Ejection fraction</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mi>EF</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>SV</mi><mi>EDV</mi></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></math><span class=\"equation-example\">Example: (80 ÷ 130) × 100 = <strong>61.5%</strong></span></div>\n<p>The lecturer uses EDV ≈130 mL, SV ≈70–90 mL and EF ≈65% as illustrative values. <strong>EF is not the fraction of all blood in the body ejected per beat</strong>—it is the fraction of end-diastolic blood within that ventricle.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-reading-the-wiggers-diagram-without-memorising-the-curves\">5. Reading the Wiggers diagram without memorising the curves</h2>\n<p>A <strong><span data-term=\"wiggers\">Wiggers diagram</span></strong> aligns the ECG, atrial pressure, ventricular pressure, aortic pressure, ventricular volume and heart sounds along one time axis.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs04-wiggers\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f4/Wiggers_Diagram.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f4/Wiggers_Diagram.svg\" alt=\"Wiggers diagram showing ECG, atrial, ventricular and aortic pressure traces, ventricular volume and heart sounds across the cardiac cycle.\" width=\"1098\" height=\"841\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 01. The Wiggers diagram.</strong> First follow ventricular pressure, ventricular volume and valve transitions; then connect them to the ECG and heart sounds. Tap to enlarge. <span class=\"figure-credit\">DanielChangMD (revised from DestinyQx); SVG redrawn by xavax · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wiggers_Diagram.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Source</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 2.5</a> · unmodified.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h3 id=\"read-it-in-four-passes\">Read it in four passes</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Find QRS and mitral closure.</strong> QRS represents ventricular depolarisation. Pressure then rises, the mitral valve closes and volume stays at EDV: <strong>isovolumetric contraction</strong>.</li><li><strong>Find aortic opening.</strong> Left ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure. The volume curve now <strong>falls</strong> as ejection begins.</li><li><strong>Find aortic closure.</strong> Pressure falls below aortic pressure, producing valve closure and <strong>S2</strong>. A small <strong>dicrotic notch</strong> appears on the aortic pressure trace around this event. Ventricular volume then stays at ESV during isovolumetric relaxation.</li><li><strong>Find mitral reopening.</strong> Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure; the mitral valve opens, and the volume curve <strong>rises</strong> as filling resumes.</li></ol>\n<p>The <strong>P wave</strong> represents atrial depolarisation, followed by atrial contraction near the end of diastole. The <strong>T wave</strong> represents ventricular repolarisation during late systole; it does not mean all contraction stops instantly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-are-there-a-c-and-v-waves-in-atrial-pressure\">Why are there a, c and v waves in atrial pressure?</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Wave</th><th>Cause</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>a wave</strong></td><td>Atrial contraction increases atrial pressure.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>c wave</strong></td><td>Early ventricular contraction pushes the closed AV valve towards the atrium.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>v wave</strong></td><td>Venous blood continues entering the atrium while the AV valve is closed.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <strong>v wave</strong> does <em>not</em> require atrial contraction. Blood continues returning to the atrium even while the mitral valve remains closed. S1 accompanies AV valve closure; S2 accompanies semilunar valve closure. Heart-sound details follow in Chapter 05.</p>\n<h3 id=\"watch-the-mechanism\">Watch the mechanism</h3>\n<div class=\"media-resource animation-resource\" data-animation-src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/Cardiac-Cycle-Animated.gif\" data-media-alt=\"Animated heart with valve motion and cardiac cycle pressure curves.\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">SHORT ANIMATION · OPTIONAL</span><h3>Cardiac cycle and Wiggers traces</h3><p>Observe when each valve closes, when volume stops changing and when ejection starts.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-animation\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Play animation</button><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">DrJanaOfficial; diagram derived from Xavax · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cardiac-Cycle-Animated.gif\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>. Loaded only when selected.</p></div>\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"IS9TD9fHFv0\" data-media-title=\"The Cardiac Cycle — Alila Medical Media\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO</span><h3>The Cardiac Cycle — Alila Medical Media</h3><p>Identify the four valve transitions and compare the two isovolumetric phases.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Watch video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IS9TD9fHFv0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not rehosted.</p></div>\n<h2 id=\"6-what-about-the-right-heart-and-faster-heart-rates\">6. What about the right heart and faster heart rates?</h2>\n<p>The <strong>right heart follows the same valve-pressure logic</strong> at lower pressures: tricuspid opening depends on right atrial versus right ventricular pressure; pulmonary valve opening depends on right ventricular versus pulmonary arterial pressure.</p>\n<p>Because pulmonary arterial pressure is much lower than aortic pressure, <strong>right ventricular ejection may begin slightly earlier</strong> even though both ventricles are activated close together. Differences in valve closure timing during respiration are developed in Chapter 05.</p>\n<p>When heart rate increases, the cardiac cycle becomes shorter. <strong>Diastole shortens proportionately more than systole.</strong> This reduces the time available for ventricular filling and much of left ventricular coronary perfusion. At moderately faster rates, increased contractility and quicker relaxation help preserve pumping; with <strong>excessive tachycardia</strong>, EDV and stroke volume may fall. A higher heart rate therefore does <strong>not necessarily</strong> increase cardiac output.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Left ventricular pressure exceeds left atrial pressure but remains below aortic pressure. Which valves are open, and what happens to ventricular volume?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Both the mitral and aortic valves are closed.</strong> Ventricular pressure has reversed the mitral gradient, closing that valve, but has not yet exceeded aortic pressure to open the aortic valve. The ventricle is in <strong>isovolumetric contraction</strong>: pressure increases while blood volume remains at approximately EDV.</p></details>\n<p><strong>2. During early ventricular ejection, ventricular volume decreases while ventricular pressure may still rise. How can both occur simultaneously?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Volume and pressure measure different things.</strong> Once left ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure, the aortic valve opens and blood leaves, so volume falls. Meanwhile, myocardial contraction may continue generating greater force early in ejection, allowing ventricular pressure to continue rising despite the decreasing blood volume. Ejection does not require pressure to be falling.</p></details>\n<p><strong>3. Ventricular repolarisation has begun, but the aortic valve remains open and blood is still being ejected. Is this physiologically possible? Explain.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Yes.</strong> Ventricular repolarisation (the T wave) occurs during late systole, but electrical recovery does not instantly end mechanical force or forward blood flow. The aortic valve can remain open while ventricular pressure and flow conditions still favour ejection. It closes when aortic pressure exceeds left ventricular pressure and the pressure gradient reverses.</p></details>\n<p><strong>4. Why does the mitral valve remain closed just after the aortic valve closes?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Left ventricular pressure is still above left atrial pressure.</strong> The aortic valve closes when aortic pressure becomes greater than ventricular pressure. That event does not automatically open the mitral valve: ventricular pressure must fall further, below atrial pressure. While both are closed, the ventricle undergoes <em>isovolumetric relaxation</em>.</p></details>\n<p><strong>5. The atrium is not contracting, yet its pressure rises during ventricular systole. Which wave explains this, and why?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>The v wave.</strong> Pulmonary venous blood continues entering the left atrium while the mitral valve is closed during ventricular systole. Blood accumulates, increasing atrial pressure without requiring atrial contraction. When the mitral valve reopens, the accumulated blood can flow into the ventricle.</p></details>\n<p><strong>6. Ventricular pressure falls rapidly while volume remains fixed at its minimum value. Identify the phase and valve positions.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Isovolumetric relaxation.</strong> The aortic valve has closed after ventricular pressure fell below aortic pressure; the mitral valve has not yet opened because ventricular pressure remains above atrial pressure. With both valves closed, ventricular pressure can decrease while volume stays at <strong>ESV</strong>.</p></details>\n<p><strong>7. End-diastolic volume is 140 mL and end-systolic volume is 60 mL. Calculate stroke volume and ejection fraction.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>SV = EDV − ESV = 140 − 60 = 80 mL.</strong> <strong>EF = (SV ÷ EDV) × 100 = (80 ÷ 140) × 100 ≈ 57.1%.</strong> Stroke volume is the actual volume ejected; ejection fraction expresses it as a percentage of the blood present at end-diastole.</p></details>\n<p><strong>8. Why may right ventricular ejection begin before left ventricular ejection?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>The right ventricle ejects against a much lower arterial pressure.</strong> Pulmonary arterial diastolic pressure is normally far below aortic diastolic pressure. Even with closely timed ventricular activation, right ventricular pressure may surpass the pulmonary arterial pressure threshold first, opening the pulmonary valve before the aortic valve.</p></details>\n<p><strong>9. Why might extreme tachycardia reduce stroke volume even though ventricular contractions occur more frequently?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Diastole shortens disproportionately as heart rate rises.</strong> At very high rates, the ventricles may not have enough time to fill, reducing EDV and the blood available for each ejection. Therefore <strong>stroke volume may decrease</strong>; whether overall cardiac output increases or falls depends on the product CO = HR × SV.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Pressure gradients drive valve movements:</strong> the mitral valve depends on LA–LV pressure; the aortic valve depends on LV–aortic pressure.</li><li><strong>Filling:</strong> Mitral open, aortic closed; ventricular volume rises, largely without atrial contraction.</li><li><strong>Atrial systole:</strong> Adds the final volume and creates the <strong>a wave</strong> of atrial pressure.</li><li><strong>Isovolumetric contraction:</strong> Both valves closed; pressure rises at constant <strong>EDV</strong>; mitral closure contributes to <strong>S1</strong>.</li><li><strong>Ejection:</strong> Aortic valve opens when LV pressure exceeds aortic pressure; ventricular volume falls.</li><li><strong>Isovolumetric relaxation:</strong> Both valves closed; pressure falls at constant <strong>ESV</strong>; aortic closure contributes to <strong>S2</strong>.</li><li><strong>Stroke volume:</strong> SV = EDV − ESV.</li><li><strong>Ejection fraction:</strong> EF = SV/EDV × 100%.</li><li><strong>Wiggers diagram:</strong> Match valve transitions to simultaneous pressure, volume, ECG and heart-sound traces.</li><li><strong>Tachycardia:</strong> Diastole shortens disproportionately; extreme rates can impair filling and coronary perfusion.</li></ol>","plain":"The Cardiac Cycle Lecture: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 11–21. Supplementary clarification: pressure-gradient reasoning, Wiggers interpretation and standard ejection-phase terminology. 1. What actually drives the cardiac cycle? The cardiac cycle is one complete sequence of ventricular filling, contraction, ejection and relaxation. To understand it, follow what happens to pressure , the valves and the amount of blood in the ventricle . Start with the left heart: left atrium → mitral valve → left ventricle → aortic valve → aorta . Blood flows through an open valve when pressure upstream is higher than pressure downstream. Valves open and close passively as these pressures change; they do not receive individual nerve impulses telling them when to move. Mitral valve opens P LA P LV Blood enters the ventricle Aortic valve opens P LV P aorta Blood leaves the ventricle Here P means pressure, LA is left atrium and LV is left ventricle. When either pressure difference reverses, the corresponding valve closes. The two valves respond to different pressure differences, which is why both may be closed at once . 2. Follow one complete heartbeat We begin with the ventricle relaxed and about to receive blood. Each phase ends only when a change in electrical activity or pressure creates the conditions for the next event. Stage 1 — Why does blood enter the ventricle? During ventricular diastole , the myocardium relaxes and ventricular pressure falls. Once P LV falls below P LA , the mitral valve opens . Pulmonary venous blood has continued entering the left atrium while the mitral valve was closed, so blood is available to flow into the ventricle. At first, filling is rapid : blood moves down the atrial-to-ventricular pressure gradient, and the relaxed, compliant ventricle can accept an increasing volume without much rise in pressure. As filling progresses, the pressure difference narrows and the flow rate slows. This later, slower period is diastasis . During filling: mitral valve open; aortic valve closed; ventricular volume rises , while pressure remains relatively low. What happens next? Atrial depolarisation occurs and the atria contract, adding the final portion of ventricular filling. Stage 2 — Why contract the atria if filling is already occurring? Most filling normally occurs passively before atrial contraction. Near the end of diastole, atrial systole pushes an additional volume into the ventricle—the atrial kick . It matters especially when a ventricle is stiff or relaxes poorly. The lecturer illustrates approximately 70% passive filling and 30% atrial contribution . These are teaching estimates, not constant percentages: atrial contraction often contributes roughly 10–30% at rest, depending on age, rate and ventricular properties. The P wave is atrial depolarisation; atrial contraction follows and produces the a wave in the atrial pressure tracing. After filling ends, the ventricle contains its end-diastolic volume (EDV) —approximately 130 mL in the lecturer's example. What happens next? Ventricular depolarisation (QRS complex) initiates contraction and raises ventricular pressure. Stage 3 — Why doesn't ventricular contraction immediately eject blood? As ventricular contraction starts, P LV increases. Once it exceeds P LA , the mitral valve closes , contributing to the first heart sound ( S1 ). Yet P LV is still below aortic pressure , so the aortic valve stays closed . Both valves are closed. The ventricle develops force, but blood cannot enter or escape. Thus pressure rises rapidly while ventricular volume remains at EDV . This is isovolumetric contraction . Mitral closed Aortic closed Pressure ↑ Volume = EDV Contraction does not necessarily mean ejection: the ventricle must first overcome arterial pressure. With aortic diastolic pressure around 80 mmHg , for example, left ventricular pressure must rise above that level to open the aortic valve. The lecturer uses approximately 10 mmHg for pulmonary arterial diastolic pressure as a right-heart comparison; these are illustrative values. What ends this phase? P LV exceeds P aorta → the aortic valve opens. Stage 4 — How does blood begin leaving the ventricle? Once P LV exceeds aortic pressure, the aortic valve opens . Blood now enters the aorta, so ventricular volume decreases . Initially, a high rate of outflow produces rapid ejection . Later, outflow decreases as ventricular force and the pressure conditions change; this is reduced ejection . Ventricular pressure may continue rising at first after the valve opens, even though ventricular volume is already falling; the two measurements need not move in the same direction. The lecturer describes ejection as initially slow then rapid; the conventional physiological subdivision is rapid ejection followed by reduced ejection . During ejection: mitral valve closed; aortic valve open; blood leaves and ventricular volume falls. The blood remaining at the end is end-systolic volume (ESV) . What happens next? Ventricular force declines; ventricular pressure begins to fall. Stage 5 — Why does the aortic valve close? As the ventricle begins to relax, its pressure falls. Blood may still be flowing forward late in ejection, but eventually aortic pressure exceeds ventricular pressure . The pressure gradient reverses and the aortic valve closes , contributing to S2 (the second heart sound). The lecturer calls the brief period of falling ventricular pressure before semilunar valve closure protodiastole . It precedes the following isovolumetric relaxation phase. What happens next? The aortic valve has closed, but the mitral valve is not yet open. Stage 6 — Why doesn't filling restart immediately? Although the aortic valve is now closed, P LV is still above P LA , keeping the mitral valve closed . The ventricle relaxes further, so pressure drops quickly, but neither valve is open and volume remains at ESV . This is isovolumetric relaxation . Mitral closed Aortic closed Pressure ↓ Volume = ESV When P LV finally falls below P LA , the mitral valve reopens . Blood enters and a new cardiac cycle begins . Compare the two isovolumetric phases Isovolumetric contraction Isovolumetric relaxation Muscle Developing force Relaxing Mitral and aortic valves Both closed Both closed Ventricular pressure Increasing ↑ Decreasing ↓ Ventricular volume Constant at EDV Constant at ESV Ends when Aortic valve opens Mitral valve opens Why is volume constant in both? Neither valve is open. Why does pressure change in opposite directions? The myocardium is generating force in one phase and relaxing in the other. 3. The cycle on one compact map What changes? Immediate consequence Next phase Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure Mitral valve opens Filling Atria depolarise Atrial contraction adds final blood volume Atrial systole Ventricular pressure exceeds atrial pressure Mitral valve closes (S1) Isovolumetric contraction Ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure Aortic valve opens Ejection Aortic pressure exceeds ventricular pressure Aortic valve closes (S2) Isovolumetric relaxation Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure Mitral valve reopens Next filling phase This map is for revision after the explanation: its purpose is to reconstruct each transition, not to replace the reasoning behind it. 4. How much blood does the ventricle pump? A ventricle does not empty completely during systole. EDV is the blood it contains immediately before contraction; ESV is what remains after ejection. Their difference is the stroke volume (SV) . Stroke volume SV = EDV − ESV Example: 130 mL − 50 mL = 80 mL The ejection fraction (EF) is the fraction of EDV ejected, expressed as a percentage. Ejection fraction EF = SV EDV × 100 % Example: (80 ÷ 130) × 100 = 61.5% The lecturer uses EDV ≈130 mL, SV ≈70–90 mL and EF ≈65% as illustrative values. EF is not the fraction of all blood in the body ejected per beat —it is the fraction of end-diastolic blood within that ventricle. 5. Reading the Wiggers diagram without memorising the curves A Wiggers diagram aligns the ECG, atrial pressure, ventricular pressure, aortic pressure, ventricular volume and heart sounds along one time axis. Figure 01. The Wiggers diagram. First follow ventricular pressure, ventricular volume and valve transitions; then connect them to the ECG and heart sounds. Tap to enlarge. DanielChangMD (revised from DestinyQx); SVG redrawn by xavax · Source · CC BY-SA 2.5 · unmodified. Read it in four passes Find QRS and mitral closure. QRS represents ventricular depolarisation. Pressure then rises, the mitral valve closes and volume stays at EDV: isovolumetric contraction . Find aortic opening. Left ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure. The volume curve now falls as ejection begins. Find aortic closure. Pressure falls below aortic pressure, producing valve closure and S2 . A small dicrotic notch appears on the aortic pressure trace around this event. Ventricular volume then stays at ESV during isovolumetric relaxation. Find mitral reopening. Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure; the mitral valve opens, and the volume curve rises as filling resumes. The P wave represents atrial depolarisation, followed by atrial contraction near the end of diastole. The T wave represents ventricular repolarisation during late systole; it does not mean all contraction stops instantly. Why are there a, c and v waves in atrial pressure? Wave Cause a wave Atrial contraction increases atrial pressure. c wave Early ventricular contraction pushes the closed AV valve towards the atrium. v wave Venous blood continues entering the atrium while the AV valve is closed. The v wave does not require atrial contraction. Blood continues returning to the atrium even while the mitral valve remains closed. S1 accompanies AV valve closure; S2 accompanies semilunar valve closure. Heart-sound details follow in Chapter 05. Watch the mechanism SHORT ANIMATION · OPTIONAL Cardiac cycle and Wiggers traces Observe when each valve closes, when volume stops changing and when ejection starts. Play animation DrJanaOfficial; diagram derived from Xavax · Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 4.0 . Loaded only when selected. OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO The Cardiac Cycle — Alila Medical Media Identify the four valve transitions and compare the two isovolumetric phases. Watch video Open on YouTube © Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not rehosted. 6. What about the right heart and faster heart rates? The right heart follows the same valve-pressure logic at lower pressures: tricuspid opening depends on right atrial versus right ventricular pressure; pulmonary valve opening depends on right ventricular versus pulmonary arterial pressure. Because pulmonary arterial pressure is much lower than aortic pressure, right ventricular ejection may begin slightly earlier even though both ventricles are activated close together. Differences in valve closure timing during respiration are developed in Chapter 05. When heart rate increases, the cardiac cycle becomes shorter. Diastole shortens proportionately more than systole. This reduces the time available for ventricular filling and much of left ventricular coronary perfusion. At moderately faster rates, increased contractility and quicker relaxation help preserve pumping; with excessive tachycardia , EDV and stroke volume may fall. A higher heart rate therefore does not necessarily increase cardiac output. Understanding Check 1. Left ventricular pressure exceeds left atrial pressure but remains below aortic pressure. Which valves are open, and what happens to ventricular volume? Reveal explanation Both the mitral and aortic valves are closed. Ventricular pressure has reversed the mitral gradient, closing that valve, but has not yet exceeded aortic pressure to open the aortic valve. The ventricle is in isovolumetric contraction : pressure increases while blood volume remains at approximately EDV. 2. During early ventricular ejection, ventricular volume decreases while ventricular pressure may still rise. How can both occur simultaneously? Reveal explanation Volume and pressure measure different things. Once left ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure, the aortic valve opens and blood leaves, so volume falls. Meanwhile, myocardial contraction may continue generating greater force early in ejection, allowing ventricular pressure to continue rising despite the decreasing blood volume. Ejection does not require pressure to be falling. 3. Ventricular repolarisation has begun, but the aortic valve remains open and blood is still being ejected. Is this physiologically possible? Explain. Reveal explanation Yes. Ventricular repolarisation (the T wave) occurs during late systole, but electrical recovery does not instantly end mechanical force or forward blood flow. The aortic valve can remain open while ventricular pressure and flow conditions still favour ejection. It closes when aortic pressure exceeds left ventricular pressure and the pressure gradient reverses. 4. Why does the mitral valve remain closed just after the aortic valve closes? Reveal explanation Left ventricular pressure is still above left atrial pressure. The aortic valve closes when aortic pressure becomes greater than ventricular pressure. That event does not automatically open the mitral valve: ventricular pressure must fall further, below atrial pressure. While both are closed, the ventricle undergoes isovolumetric relaxation . 5. The atrium is not contracting, yet its pressure rises during ventricular systole. Which wave explains this, and why? Reveal explanation The v wave. Pulmonary venous blood continues entering the left atrium while the mitral valve is closed during ventricular systole. Blood accumulates, increasing atrial pressure without requiring atrial contraction. When the mitral valve reopens, the accumulated blood can flow into the ventricle. 6. Ventricular pressure falls rapidly while volume remains fixed at its minimum value. Identify the phase and valve positions. Reveal explanation Isovolumetric relaxation. The aortic valve has closed after ventricular pressure fell below aortic pressure; the mitral valve has not yet opened because ventricular pressure remains above atrial pressure. With both valves closed, ventricular pressure can decrease while volume stays at ESV . 7. End-diastolic volume is 140 mL and end-systolic volume is 60 mL. Calculate stroke volume and ejection fraction. Reveal explanation SV = EDV − ESV = 140 − 60 = 80 mL. EF = (SV ÷ EDV) × 100 = (80 ÷ 140) × 100 ≈ 57.1%. Stroke volume is the actual volume ejected; ejection fraction expresses it as a percentage of the blood present at end-diastole. 8. Why may right ventricular ejection begin before left ventricular ejection? Reveal explanation The right ventricle ejects against a much lower arterial pressure. Pulmonary arterial diastolic pressure is normally far below aortic diastolic pressure. Even with closely timed ventricular activation, right ventricular pressure may surpass the pulmonary arterial pressure threshold first, opening the pulmonary valve before the aortic valve. 9. Why might extreme tachycardia reduce stroke volume even though ventricular contractions occur more frequently? Reveal explanation Diastole shortens disproportionately as heart rate rises. At very high rates, the ventricles may not have enough time to fill, reducing EDV and the blood available for each ejection. Therefore stroke volume may decrease ; whether overall cardiac output increases or falls depends on the product CO = HR × SV. Chapter Essentials Pressure gradients drive valve movements: the mitral valve depends on LA–LV pressure; the aortic valve depends on LV–aortic pressure. Filling: Mitral open, aortic closed; ventricular volume rises, largely without atrial contraction. Atrial systole: Adds the final volume and creates the a wave of atrial pressure. Isovolumetric contraction: Both valves closed; pressure rises at constant EDV ; mitral closure contributes to S1 . Ejection: Aortic valve opens when LV pressure exceeds aortic pressure; ventricular volume falls. Isovolumetric relaxation: Both valves closed; pressure falls at constant ESV ; aortic closure contributes to S2 . Stroke volume: SV = EDV − ESV. Ejection fraction: EF = SV/EDV × 100%. Wiggers diagram: Match valve transitions to simultaneous pressure, volume, ECG and heart-sound traces. Tachycardia: Diastole shortens disproportionately; extreme rates can impair filling and coronary perfusion."},{"slug":"heart-sounds","title":"Heart Sounds and Murmurs","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"18-19 and 27-34","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-does-the-heart-produce-sounds","title":"1. Why does the heart produce sounds?"},{"id":"2-why-does-s1-occur-before-s2","title":"2. Why does S1 occur before S2?"},{"id":"3-why-does-s2-sometimes-split-with-inspiration","title":"3. Why does S2 sometimes split with inspiration?"},{"id":"4-what-causes-s3-and-s4","title":"4. What causes S3 and S4?"},{"id":"5-why-do-heart-sounds-change-in-intensity","title":"5. Why do heart sounds change in intensity?"},{"id":"6-what-makes-a-murmur","title":"6. What makes a murmur?"},{"id":"7-predict-a-murmurs-timing-without-memorising-a-list","title":"7. Predict a murmur's timing without memorising a list"},{"id":"8-where-do-we-listen-to-each-heart-valve","title":"8. Where do we listen to each heart valve?"},{"id":"9-why-can-bruits-or-murmurs-arise-elsewhere","title":"9. Why can bruits or murmurs arise elsewhere?"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1 id=\"heart-sounds-and-murmurs\">Heart Sounds and Murmurs</h1>\n<p><strong>Lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 27–34, with related concepts from slides 18–19. <strong>Supplementary explanation:</strong> mechanisms of S3 and S4, auscultation landmarks, and applying valve physiology to murmur timing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-the-heart-produce-sounds\">1. Why does the heart produce sounds?</h2>\n<p>During the cardiac cycle, pressure changes make heart valves close, and blood accelerates or decelerates inside the heart. Sudden changes in blood motion set the valve apparatus, surrounding tissues and blood into <strong>vibration</strong>. Some of these vibrations travel through the chest wall and can be heard with a stethoscope.</p>\n<p>Heart sounds are therefore <strong>not simply leaflets hitting one another</strong>. The two principal sounds normally heard are <strong>S1</strong> and <strong>S2</strong>.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Sound</th><th>Main valve event</th><th>When it occurs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>S1 — “lub”</strong></td><td>Mitral and tricuspid closure</td><td>Beginning of ventricular systole</td></tr><tr><td><strong>S2 — “dub”</strong></td><td>Aortic and pulmonary closure</td><td>Beginning of ventricular diastole</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"heart-sound-line\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Heart sound timeline\"><span><strong>S1</strong><small>AV valves close</small></span><span class=\"timeline-phase\"><strong>Systole</strong><small>Ventricular pressure and ejection</small></span><span><strong>S2</strong><small>Semilunar valves close</small></span><span class=\"timeline-phase\"><strong>Diastole</strong><small>Relaxation and filling</small></span></div>\n<h3 id=\"listen-to-normal-s1-and-s2\">Listen to normal S1 and S2</h3>\n<div class=\"media-resource audio-resource\" data-audio-src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/72/HROgg.ogg\" data-audio-mp3=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/7/72/HROgg.ogg/HROgg.ogg.mp3\" data-media-title=\"Normal heart sounds at approximately 70 beats per minute\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">REAL HEART-SOUND RECORDING · 19 SECONDS</span><h3>Normal S1–S2 sequence</h3><p>Listen for two sounds per beat. The shorter S1-to-S2 interval is systole; the usually longer S2-to-next-S1 interval is diastole at a normal resting rate.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-audio\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Listen</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HROgg.ogg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Source / alternative playback</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">James Heilman, MD · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC0 public-domain dedication</a>. Audio is loaded only when selected; the explanation above works without it.</p></div>\n<h2 id=\"2-why-does-s1-occur-before-s2\">2. Why does S1 occur before S2?</h2>\n<h3 id=\"s1-the-start-of-ventricular-systole\">S1: the start of ventricular systole</h3>\n<p>Ventricular depolarisation (QRS) is followed by myocardial contraction, raising ventricular pressure. Once ventricular pressure exceeds atrial pressure, the <strong>AV valves close</strong>. Their closure and the associated sudden changes in blood motion produce <strong>S1</strong>, around the onset of <span data-term=\"isovolumetric\">isovolumetric contraction</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"s2-the-end-of-ventricular-ejection\">S2: the end of ventricular ejection</h3>\n<p>Later, ventricular force and pressure decline. When aortic and pulmonary arterial pressures exceed the corresponding ventricular pressures, the <strong>semilunar valves close</strong>. This produces <strong>S2</strong>, around the beginning of isovolumetric relaxation.</p>\n<p>The important pressure logic is: <strong>S1 follows reversal of the atrial-to-ventricular pressure gradient; S2 follows reversal of the ventricular-to-arterial pressure gradient.</strong> Closure generates audible vibrations; a valve does not make a sound simply because it is open.</p>\n<p>The lecturer gives illustrative durations of approximately <strong>0.15 s for S1</strong> and <strong>0.12 s for S2</strong>. Sound duration and intensity can vary.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-does-s2-sometimes-split-with-inspiration\">3. Why does S2 sometimes split with inspiration?</h2>\n<p>S2 has two components: <strong>A2</strong> (aortic valve closure) and <strong>P2</strong> (pulmonary valve closure). Usually <strong>A2 precedes P2</strong> by a short interval.</p>\n<p>During inspiration, more negative intrathoracic pressure promotes <strong>systemic venous return to the right heart</strong>. Right ventricular filling rises, and right ventricular ejection may last slightly longer. This delays <strong>P2 relative to A2</strong>, making the two components more clearly separated. Respiratory changes in pulmonary venous return and left ventricular timing can contribute as well.</p>\n<div class=\"sound-split\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Aortic and pulmonary valve closure timing\"><div><strong>Expiration</strong><span>A2 <b>·</b> P2</span><small>Short separation; may sound single</small></div><div><strong>Inspiration</strong><span>A2 <b>····</b> P2</span><small>Longer separation: physiological splitting</small></div></div>\n<p><strong>The important point is widening of the A2–P2 interval during inspiration</strong>, not that the two valves necessarily close at precisely the same time during expiration. More complex patterns of splitting are advanced clinical material beyond this lecturer's primary scope.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-what-causes-s3-and-s4\">4. What causes S3 and S4?</h2>\n<p>Both are additional low-frequency sounds in <strong>diastole</strong>, but they correspond to <em>different phases of filling</em>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"s3-early-diastole-rapid-ventricular-filling\">S3 — Early diastole: rapid ventricular filling</h3>\n<p><strong>S3 occurs shortly after S2</strong>, when blood enters the ventricle rapidly. Rapid filling and sudden deceleration of blood can set ventricular structures vibrating. S3 can be physiological in children and younger adults, and during pregnancy. In an older or symptomatic adult, it can indicate volume overload or ventricular dysfunction, including heart failure.</p>\n<h3 id=\"s4-late-diastole-atrial-contraction-against-resistance\">S4 — Late diastole: atrial contraction against resistance</h3>\n<p><strong>S4 occurs shortly before S1</strong>, during atrial contraction. If the ventricle is relatively stiff and noncompliant, the additional blood delivered by atrial systole encounters greater resistance to filling, generating low-frequency vibrations.</p>\n<p>S4 is usually abnormal in adults. A typical atrial S4 is <strong>absent in atrial fibrillation</strong> because there is no effective coordinated atrial contraction to produce it.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th><strong>S3</strong></th><th><strong>S4</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Early diastole, just after S2</td><td>Late diastole, just before S1</td></tr><tr><td>Trigger</td><td>Rapid ventricular filling</td><td>Atrial contraction</td></tr><tr><td>Interpretation</td><td>Sometimes normal in younger people; may indicate overload/dysfunction in older adults</td><td>Often suggests a stiff ventricle in adults</td></tr><tr><td>Needs atrial contraction?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Audio interpretation:</strong> A new low-pitched sound just after S2 suggests <strong>S3</strong>; one just before S1 suggests <strong>S4</strong>. Our normal recording above demonstrates S1/S2, <strong>not</strong> a verified S3 or S4 example. Separate recordings should be added only after checking their identity and rights.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-why-do-heart-sounds-change-in-intensity\">5. Why do heart sounds change in intensity?</h2>\n<p>Sound intensity depends on valve motion, pressure changes, the rate at which blood flow is stopped, and how well the vibrations travel to the stethoscope.</p>\n<p>For example, <strong>A2 may be accentuated in systemic hypertension</strong>, and <strong>P2 in pulmonary hypertension</strong>, because higher arterial pressure can produce more energetic closing events. However, severe valve immobility may make a closure sound softer despite disease.</p>\n<p>Thus, <strong>a loud or soft sound alone cannot reliably establish disease severity</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-what-makes-a-murmur\">6. What makes a murmur?</h2>\n<p>A <strong>heart murmur</strong> is a relatively sustained sound produced when blood flow and cardiac structures generate audible vibrations, often in association with disturbed or turbulent flow.</p>\n<p>Examples of conditions that change the flow include:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Stenosis:</strong> A valve narrows, so blood has difficulty passing <em>forward</em> through it.</li><li><strong>Regurgitation (incompetence):</strong> A valve fails to close properly and allows blood to leak <em>backward</em>.</li><li><strong>High-flow states:</strong> For example, anaemia can increase flow or reduce blood viscosity enough to produce a murmur despite structurally normal valves.</li></ul>\n<p>To predict when a murmur occurs, first identify <strong>the direction of abnormal flow</strong> and <strong>whether that flow happens during ventricular systole or diastole</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-predict-a-murmurs-timing-without-memorising-a-list\">7. Predict a murmur's timing without memorising a list</h2>\n<p>Remember the normal valve pattern:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>During</th><th>AV valves (mitral/tricuspid)</th><th>Semilunar valves (aortic/pulmonary)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Ventricular systole</strong></td><td>Should be <strong>closed</strong></td><td>Should be <strong>open</strong> for ejection</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ventricular diastole</strong></td><td>Should be <strong>open</strong> for filling</td><td>Should be <strong>closed</strong></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"systolic-murmurs\">Systolic murmurs</h3>\n<p><strong>Aortic or pulmonary stenosis:</strong> These valves should be open during ventricular ejection. A narrowed opening obstructs <strong>forward systolic flow</strong>, so turbulent high-velocity flow can produce a systolic ejection murmur.</p>\n<p><strong>Mitral or tricuspid regurgitation:</strong> These AV valves should be closed during ventricular contraction. If they leak, blood moves <strong>backward from ventricle to atrium during systole</strong>, creating a systolic regurgitant murmur.</p>\n<h3 id=\"diastolic-murmurs\">Diastolic murmurs</h3>\n<p><strong>Mitral or tricuspid stenosis:</strong> AV valves should open during ventricular filling. A narrowed opening obstructs <strong>forward flow from atrium to ventricle during diastole</strong>, causing a diastolic murmur.</p>\n<p><strong>Aortic or pulmonary regurgitation:</strong> Semilunar valves should remain closed during diastole. If they leak, arterial pressure drives <strong>backward flow into the ventricle during diastole</strong>, causing a diastolic murmur.</p>\n<h3 id=\"murmur-timing-at-a-glance\">Murmur timing at a glance</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Valve group</th><th>Stenosis</th><th>Regurgitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Aortic / pulmonary</strong></td><td><strong>Systolic</strong> (forward outflow obstruction)</td><td><strong>Diastolic</strong> (backflow into ventricle)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mitral / tricuspid</strong></td><td><strong>Diastolic</strong> (forward filling obstruction)</td><td><strong>Systolic</strong> (backflow into atrium)</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>To solve a new question, ask <strong>which valve should be open or shut during the abnormal flow?</strong> This explains the timing. Murmur loudness alone is not a dependable measure of lesion severity; location, character, radiation and other signs also matter.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-where-do-we-listen-to-each-heart-valve\">8. Where do we listen to each heart valve?</h2>\n<p>The best listening points on the chest are <strong>not necessarily directly over the anatomical valve openings</strong>. Vibrations are transmitted through blood and cardiac tissues.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Auscultation area</th><th>Surface landmark</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Aortic</strong></td><td>Second <strong>right</strong> intercostal space, near sternum</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pulmonary</strong></td><td>Second <strong>left</strong> intercostal space, near sternum</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tricuspid</strong></td><td>Lower <strong>left</strong> sternal border</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mitral</strong></td><td><strong>Apex</strong>, commonly fifth left intercostal space near midclavicular line</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration teaching-illustration-portrait\" id=\"cvs05-auscultation\">\n<a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heart_sounds_auscultation_areas.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/44/Heart_sounds_auscultation_areas.svg\" alt=\"Anterior chest surface showing colour-coded cardiac auscultation points: navy aortic, yellow pulmonary, red tricuspid, green mitral, light blue Erb's point.\" width=\"436\" height=\"600\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 01. Cardiac auscultation areas.</strong> Navy = aortic; yellow = pulmonary; red = tricuspid; green = mitral; light blue = Erb's point (supplementary). <span class=\"figure-credit\">Vinne2, after Ickle and Gray's Anatomy · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heart_sounds_auscultation_areas.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · public domain.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p>A stethoscope's <strong>diaphragm</strong> transmits many higher-frequency sounds well (e.g. S1, S2 and many murmurs). Its <strong>bell</strong>, applied lightly, can help detect low-frequency S3, S4 and some diastolic murmurs. This is a supplementary clinical technique.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-why-can-bruits-or-murmurs-arise-elsewhere\">9. Why can bruits or murmurs arise elsewhere?</h2>\n<p>A <strong><span data-term=\"bruit\">bruit</span></strong> is an abnormal vascular sound heard over a vessel when flow generates audible vibrations.</p>\n<p>The lecture also names conditions that can produce abnormal cardiovascular sounds:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Mechanism</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)</strong></td><td>Aorta-to-pulmonary artery flow may continue during both systole and diastole, producing a <em>continuous</em> murmur.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ventricular septal defect (VSD)</strong></td><td>Abnormal flow between ventricles commonly produces a systolic murmur.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Arteriovenous fistula</strong></td><td>High-velocity artery-to-vein flow can create a bruit.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Anaemia</strong></td><td>Reduced viscosity and increased flow can cause a functional murmur without valve stenosis.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Vascular aneurysm</strong></td><td>Altered vessel geometry may disturb flow; not every aneurysm produces a bruit.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hypervascular goitre</strong></td><td>Increased thyroid blood flow can cause a bruit over the gland.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The common mechanism is <strong>flow-induced vibration</strong>, but its timing depends on the relevant pressure gradient and anatomy.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. A sound is heard at the beginning of ventricular systole. Which valves are mainly responsible, and what pressure change closes them?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>S1, mainly from mitral and tricuspid valve closure.</strong> Early ventricular contraction raises ventricular pressure above atrial pressure, reversing the gradient across the AV valves. Their closure abruptly alters blood motion and generates vibrations heard as S1.</p></details>\n<p><strong>2. Why does physiological splitting of S2 increase with inspiration?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Inspiration increases venous return to the right heart. Greater right ventricular filling can prolong ejection and <strong>delay pulmonary valve closure (P2)</strong> relative to aortic closure (A2). The A2–P2 interval widens. Expiration usually narrows the separation; the valves are not required to close at exactly the same instant.</p></details>\n<p><strong>3. An extra sound occurs shortly after S2 during rapid ventricular filling. Identify it and explain when it may be physiological.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>S3.</strong> Rapid early-diastolic filling and deceleration produce vibrations in ventricular structures. An S3 may be normal in children or young adults, but in an older symptomatic patient it can suggest ventricular overload or dysfunction. Timing and clinical context are essential.</p></details>\n<p><strong>4. A low-frequency sound just before S1 disappears when a patient develops atrial fibrillation. Explain the likely sound and why it disappears.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>S4.</strong> It occurs in late diastole when effective atrial contraction forces blood into a relatively stiff ventricle, producing vibrations. In atrial fibrillation, coordinated atrial contraction is lost; without the atrial kick, the usual mechanism producing an atrial S4 is absent.</p></details>\n<p><strong>5. Why can mitral stenosis cause a diastolic murmur but mitral regurgitation cause a systolic murmur?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Stenosis obstructs normal forward filling:</strong> the mitral valve should be open in diastole for left atrium-to-ventricle flow, so narrowing generates a diastolic murmur. <strong>Regurgitation permits backward flow:</strong> the valve should be closed during ventricular systole, but leakage sends blood into the left atrium and produces a systolic murmur.</p></details>\n<p><strong>6. A valve leaks blood from the aorta back into the left ventricle. Name the lesion and predict the murmur's timing.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Aortic regurgitation; diastolic murmur.</strong> During ventricular relaxation, aortic pressure is greater than left ventricular pressure and the aortic valve should be closed. If it is incompetent, blood leaks backward into the ventricle during diastole and generates an audible murmur.</p></details>\n<p><strong>7. Why might an anaemic patient have a systolic flow murmur even when all four valves are structurally normal?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Anaemia reduces blood viscosity and often increases cardiac output. Faster flow through otherwise normal valves may generate audible flow-related vibrations, often as an ejection-type systolic murmur. A murmur therefore does not automatically mean a damaged or narrowed valve.</p></details>\n<p><strong>8. A stethoscope is placed over the second right intercostal space. Which valve area is being examined, and why is this not necessarily the valve's anatomical position?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>The aortic auscultation area.</strong> Valve vibrations travel with blood and through surrounding tissues, so the point at which they are best transmitted to the chest wall is not necessarily directly over the anatomical aortic valve.</p></details>\n<p><strong>9. Listen to the normal heart recording. Which interval corresponds to ventricular systole, and why?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>The S1-to-S2 interval is ventricular systole.</strong> S1 follows AV valve closure as ventricular pressure rises; S2 follows semilunar valve closure when ventricular ejection ends. The S2-to-next-S1 interval is ventricular diastole. The recording illustrates normal S1 and S2, not a proven S3 or S4.</p></details>\n<p><strong>10. A continuous murmur is heard in a patient with a patent ductus arteriosus. Why does the abnormal flow persist across both parts of the cardiac cycle?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>A patent ductus joins the aorta to the pulmonary artery. Because aortic pressure is typically greater than pulmonary arterial pressure during both systole and diastole, blood may continue to cross the connection throughout the cycle. Thus the sound can be <strong>continuous</strong>, unlike a murmur confined to one phase.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>S1:</strong> Mostly due to mitral and tricuspid closure at the start of ventricular systole.</li><li><strong>S2:</strong> Mostly due to aortic and pulmonary closure at the start of ventricular diastole.</li><li><strong>S2 splitting:</strong> Inspiration normally widens the A2–P2 interval, largely through relative delay of P2.</li><li><strong>S3:</strong> Early-diastolic rapid-filling sound; may be physiological in younger people.</li><li><strong>S4:</strong> Late-diastolic sound involving atrial contraction into a relatively stiff ventricle; typically absent in atrial fibrillation.</li><li><strong>Murmurs:</strong> Audible flow-induced vibrations; assess timing, direction and affected valve.</li><li><strong>Semilunar stenosis / AV regurgitation:</strong> Usually <strong>systolic</strong> murmurs.</li><li><strong>AV stenosis / semilunar regurgitation:</strong> Usually <strong>diastolic</strong> murmurs.</li><li><strong>Auscultation:</strong> Aortic—second right; pulmonary—second left; tricuspid—lower left sternal border; mitral—apex.</li><li><strong>Auscultation areas are listening points</strong>, not the exact anatomical valve locations.</li><li><strong>Bruits and nonvalvular murmurs:</strong> Can accompany abnormal vascular connections, turbulent flow or high-flow states.</li><li><strong>Continuous PDA murmur:</strong> Flow can persist because the aorta-to-pulmonary artery pressure difference remains present through much of the cardiac cycle.</li></ol>","plain":"Heart Sounds and Murmurs Lecture: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 27–34, with related concepts from slides 18–19. Supplementary explanation: mechanisms of S3 and S4, auscultation landmarks, and applying valve physiology to murmur timing. 1. Why does the heart produce sounds? During the cardiac cycle, pressure changes make heart valves close, and blood accelerates or decelerates inside the heart. Sudden changes in blood motion set the valve apparatus, surrounding tissues and blood into vibration . Some of these vibrations travel through the chest wall and can be heard with a stethoscope. Heart sounds are therefore not simply leaflets hitting one another . The two principal sounds normally heard are S1 and S2 . Sound Main valve event When it occurs S1 — “lub” Mitral and tricuspid closure Beginning of ventricular systole S2 — “dub” Aortic and pulmonary closure Beginning of ventricular diastole S1 AV valves close Systole Ventricular pressure and ejection S2 Semilunar valves close Diastole Relaxation and filling Listen to normal S1 and S2 REAL HEART-SOUND RECORDING · 19 SECONDS Normal S1–S2 sequence Listen for two sounds per beat. The shorter S1-to-S2 interval is systole; the usually longer S2-to-next-S1 interval is diastole at a normal resting rate. Listen Source / alternative playback James Heilman, MD · CC0 public-domain dedication . Audio is loaded only when selected; the explanation above works without it. 2. Why does S1 occur before S2? S1: the start of ventricular systole Ventricular depolarisation (QRS) is followed by myocardial contraction, raising ventricular pressure. Once ventricular pressure exceeds atrial pressure, the AV valves close . Their closure and the associated sudden changes in blood motion produce S1 , around the onset of isovolumetric contraction . S2: the end of ventricular ejection Later, ventricular force and pressure decline. When aortic and pulmonary arterial pressures exceed the corresponding ventricular pressures, the semilunar valves close . This produces S2 , around the beginning of isovolumetric relaxation. The important pressure logic is: S1 follows reversal of the atrial-to-ventricular pressure gradient; S2 follows reversal of the ventricular-to-arterial pressure gradient. Closure generates audible vibrations; a valve does not make a sound simply because it is open. The lecturer gives illustrative durations of approximately 0.15 s for S1 and 0.12 s for S2 . Sound duration and intensity can vary. 3. Why does S2 sometimes split with inspiration? S2 has two components: A2 (aortic valve closure) and P2 (pulmonary valve closure). Usually A2 precedes P2 by a short interval. During inspiration, more negative intrathoracic pressure promotes systemic venous return to the right heart . Right ventricular filling rises, and right ventricular ejection may last slightly longer. This delays P2 relative to A2 , making the two components more clearly separated. Respiratory changes in pulmonary venous return and left ventricular timing can contribute as well. Expiration A2 · P2 Short separation; may sound single Inspiration A2 ···· P2 Longer separation: physiological splitting The important point is widening of the A2–P2 interval during inspiration , not that the two valves necessarily close at precisely the same time during expiration. More complex patterns of splitting are advanced clinical material beyond this lecturer's primary scope. 4. What causes S3 and S4? Both are additional low-frequency sounds in diastole , but they correspond to different phases of filling . S3 — Early diastole: rapid ventricular filling S3 occurs shortly after S2 , when blood enters the ventricle rapidly. Rapid filling and sudden deceleration of blood can set ventricular structures vibrating. S3 can be physiological in children and younger adults, and during pregnancy. In an older or symptomatic adult, it can indicate volume overload or ventricular dysfunction, including heart failure. S4 — Late diastole: atrial contraction against resistance S4 occurs shortly before S1 , during atrial contraction. If the ventricle is relatively stiff and noncompliant, the additional blood delivered by atrial systole encounters greater resistance to filling, generating low-frequency vibrations. S4 is usually abnormal in adults. A typical atrial S4 is absent in atrial fibrillation because there is no effective coordinated atrial contraction to produce it. Feature S3 S4 Timing Early diastole, just after S2 Late diastole, just before S1 Trigger Rapid ventricular filling Atrial contraction Interpretation Sometimes normal in younger people; may indicate overload/dysfunction in older adults Often suggests a stiff ventricle in adults Needs atrial contraction? No Yes Audio interpretation: A new low-pitched sound just after S2 suggests S3 ; one just before S1 suggests S4 . Our normal recording above demonstrates S1/S2, not a verified S3 or S4 example. Separate recordings should be added only after checking their identity and rights. 5. Why do heart sounds change in intensity? Sound intensity depends on valve motion, pressure changes, the rate at which blood flow is stopped, and how well the vibrations travel to the stethoscope. For example, A2 may be accentuated in systemic hypertension , and P2 in pulmonary hypertension , because higher arterial pressure can produce more energetic closing events. However, severe valve immobility may make a closure sound softer despite disease. Thus, a loud or soft sound alone cannot reliably establish disease severity . 6. What makes a murmur? A heart murmur is a relatively sustained sound produced when blood flow and cardiac structures generate audible vibrations, often in association with disturbed or turbulent flow. Examples of conditions that change the flow include: Stenosis: A valve narrows, so blood has difficulty passing forward through it. Regurgitation (incompetence): A valve fails to close properly and allows blood to leak backward . High-flow states: For example, anaemia can increase flow or reduce blood viscosity enough to produce a murmur despite structurally normal valves. To predict when a murmur occurs, first identify the direction of abnormal flow and whether that flow happens during ventricular systole or diastole . 7. Predict a murmur's timing without memorising a list Remember the normal valve pattern: During AV valves (mitral/tricuspid) Semilunar valves (aortic/pulmonary) Ventricular systole Should be closed Should be open for ejection Ventricular diastole Should be open for filling Should be closed Systolic murmurs Aortic or pulmonary stenosis: These valves should be open during ventricular ejection. A narrowed opening obstructs forward systolic flow , so turbulent high-velocity flow can produce a systolic ejection murmur. Mitral or tricuspid regurgitation: These AV valves should be closed during ventricular contraction. If they leak, blood moves backward from ventricle to atrium during systole , creating a systolic regurgitant murmur. Diastolic murmurs Mitral or tricuspid stenosis: AV valves should open during ventricular filling. A narrowed opening obstructs forward flow from atrium to ventricle during diastole , causing a diastolic murmur. Aortic or pulmonary regurgitation: Semilunar valves should remain closed during diastole. If they leak, arterial pressure drives backward flow into the ventricle during diastole , causing a diastolic murmur. Murmur timing at a glance Valve group Stenosis Regurgitation Aortic / pulmonary Systolic (forward outflow obstruction) Diastolic (backflow into ventricle) Mitral / tricuspid Diastolic (forward filling obstruction) Systolic (backflow into atrium) To solve a new question, ask which valve should be open or shut during the abnormal flow? This explains the timing. Murmur loudness alone is not a dependable measure of lesion severity; location, character, radiation and other signs also matter. 8. Where do we listen to each heart valve? The best listening points on the chest are not necessarily directly over the anatomical valve openings . Vibrations are transmitted through blood and cardiac tissues. Auscultation area Surface landmark Aortic Second right intercostal space, near sternum Pulmonary Second left intercostal space, near sternum Tricuspid Lower left sternal border Mitral Apex , commonly fifth left intercostal space near midclavicular line Figure 01. Cardiac auscultation areas. Navy = aortic; yellow = pulmonary; red = tricuspid; green = mitral; light blue = Erb's point (supplementary). Vinne2, after Ickle and Gray's Anatomy · Wikimedia Commons · public domain. A stethoscope's diaphragm transmits many higher-frequency sounds well (e.g. S1, S2 and many murmurs). Its bell , applied lightly, can help detect low-frequency S3, S4 and some diastolic murmurs. This is a supplementary clinical technique. 9. Why can bruits or murmurs arise elsewhere? A bruit is an abnormal vascular sound heard over a vessel when flow generates audible vibrations. The lecture also names conditions that can produce abnormal cardiovascular sounds: Condition Mechanism Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) Aorta-to-pulmonary artery flow may continue during both systole and diastole, producing a continuous murmur. Ventricular septal defect (VSD) Abnormal flow between ventricles commonly produces a systolic murmur. Arteriovenous fistula High-velocity artery-to-vein flow can create a bruit. Anaemia Reduced viscosity and increased flow can cause a functional murmur without valve stenosis. Vascular aneurysm Altered vessel geometry may disturb flow; not every aneurysm produces a bruit. Hypervascular goitre Increased thyroid blood flow can cause a bruit over the gland. The common mechanism is flow-induced vibration , but its timing depends on the relevant pressure gradient and anatomy. Understanding Check 1. A sound is heard at the beginning of ventricular systole. Which valves are mainly responsible, and what pressure change closes them? Reveal explanation S1, mainly from mitral and tricuspid valve closure. Early ventricular contraction raises ventricular pressure above atrial pressure, reversing the gradient across the AV valves. Their closure abruptly alters blood motion and generates vibrations heard as S1. 2. Why does physiological splitting of S2 increase with inspiration? Reveal explanation Inspiration increases venous return to the right heart. Greater right ventricular filling can prolong ejection and delay pulmonary valve closure (P2) relative to aortic closure (A2). The A2–P2 interval widens. Expiration usually narrows the separation; the valves are not required to close at exactly the same instant. 3. An extra sound occurs shortly after S2 during rapid ventricular filling. Identify it and explain when it may be physiological. Reveal explanation S3. Rapid early-diastolic filling and deceleration produce vibrations in ventricular structures. An S3 may be normal in children or young adults, but in an older symptomatic patient it can suggest ventricular overload or dysfunction. Timing and clinical context are essential. 4. A low-frequency sound just before S1 disappears when a patient develops atrial fibrillation. Explain the likely sound and why it disappears. Reveal explanation S4. It occurs in late diastole when effective atrial contraction forces blood into a relatively stiff ventricle, producing vibrations. In atrial fibrillation, coordinated atrial contraction is lost; without the atrial kick, the usual mechanism producing an atrial S4 is absent. 5. Why can mitral stenosis cause a diastolic murmur but mitral regurgitation cause a systolic murmur? Reveal explanation Stenosis obstructs normal forward filling: the mitral valve should be open in diastole for left atrium-to-ventricle flow, so narrowing generates a diastolic murmur. Regurgitation permits backward flow: the valve should be closed during ventricular systole, but leakage sends blood into the left atrium and produces a systolic murmur. 6. A valve leaks blood from the aorta back into the left ventricle. Name the lesion and predict the murmur's timing. Reveal explanation Aortic regurgitation; diastolic murmur. During ventricular relaxation, aortic pressure is greater than left ventricular pressure and the aortic valve should be closed. If it is incompetent, blood leaks backward into the ventricle during diastole and generates an audible murmur. 7. Why might an anaemic patient have a systolic flow murmur even when all four valves are structurally normal? Reveal explanation Anaemia reduces blood viscosity and often increases cardiac output. Faster flow through otherwise normal valves may generate audible flow-related vibrations, often as an ejection-type systolic murmur. A murmur therefore does not automatically mean a damaged or narrowed valve. 8. A stethoscope is placed over the second right intercostal space. Which valve area is being examined, and why is this not necessarily the valve's anatomical position? Reveal explanation The aortic auscultation area. Valve vibrations travel with blood and through surrounding tissues, so the point at which they are best transmitted to the chest wall is not necessarily directly over the anatomical aortic valve. 9. Listen to the normal heart recording. Which interval corresponds to ventricular systole, and why? Reveal explanation The S1-to-S2 interval is ventricular systole. S1 follows AV valve closure as ventricular pressure rises; S2 follows semilunar valve closure when ventricular ejection ends. The S2-to-next-S1 interval is ventricular diastole. The recording illustrates normal S1 and S2, not a proven S3 or S4. 10. A continuous murmur is heard in a patient with a patent ductus arteriosus. Why does the abnormal flow persist across both parts of the cardiac cycle? Reveal explanation A patent ductus joins the aorta to the pulmonary artery. Because aortic pressure is typically greater than pulmonary arterial pressure during both systole and diastole, blood may continue to cross the connection throughout the cycle. Thus the sound can be continuous , unlike a murmur confined to one phase. Chapter Essentials S1: Mostly due to mitral and tricuspid closure at the start of ventricular systole. S2: Mostly due to aortic and pulmonary closure at the start of ventricular diastole. S2 splitting: Inspiration normally widens the A2–P2 interval, largely through relative delay of P2. S3: Early-diastolic rapid-filling sound; may be physiological in younger people. S4: Late-diastolic sound involving atrial contraction into a relatively stiff ventricle; typically absent in atrial fibrillation. Murmurs: Audible flow-induced vibrations; assess timing, direction and affected valve. Semilunar stenosis / AV regurgitation: Usually systolic murmurs. AV stenosis / semilunar regurgitation: Usually diastolic murmurs. Auscultation: Aortic—second right; pulmonary—second left; tricuspid—lower left sternal border; mitral—apex. Auscultation areas are listening points , not the exact anatomical valve locations. Bruits and nonvalvular murmurs: Can accompany abnormal vascular connections, turbulent flow or high-flow states. Continuous PDA murmur: Flow can persist because the aorta-to-pulmonary artery pressure difference remains present through much of the cardiac cycle."},{"slug":"ecg","title":"Electrocardiography and Basic Rhythms","group":"Heart","source":"Lecture 7 ECG 2.pdf","pages":"1-14; extra diagrams Circulation 3 35-38","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-does-an-ecg-actually-record","title":"1. What does an ECG actually record?"},{"id":"2-why-can-the-tracing-move-upwards-or-downwards","title":"2. Why can the tracing move upwards or downwards?"},{"id":"3-why-can-ten-electrodes-produce-twelve-ecg-leads","title":"3. Why can ten electrodes produce twelve ECG leads?"},{"id":"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference","title":"4. Waves, segments and intervals: what is the difference?"},{"id":"5-connect-the-ecg-to-mechanical-events","title":"5. Connect the ECG to mechanical events"},{"id":"6-how-do-ecg-paper-squares-represent-time-and-voltage","title":"6. How do ECG paper squares represent time and voltage?"},{"id":"7-calculate-heart-rate-from-the-tracing","title":"7. Calculate heart rate from the tracing"},{"id":"8-basic-rhythm-assessment-supplementary","title":"8. Basic rhythm assessment (supplementary)"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1 id=\"electrocardiography-and-basic-rhythms\">Electrocardiography and Basic Rhythms</h1>\n<p><strong>Primary lecture:</strong> Lecture 7 ECG 2.pdf, slides 1–14. <strong>Supplementary foundations:</strong> vector reasoning, accurate lead positions, basic rhythm assessment and AV block recognition.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-does-an-ecg-actually-record\">1. What does an ECG actually record?</h2>\n<p>Every heartbeat is preceded by cardiac <strong>electrical activation</strong>. As myocardial cells depolarise and repolarise, electrical potential differences are produced and conducted through body tissues. Electrodes on the skin detect these differences. The <strong><span data-term=\"ecg\">electrocardiogram (ECG, or EKG)</span></strong> is a recording of this electrical activity over time.</p>\n<p>An ECG <strong>does not directly measure</strong> contraction strength, pressure, stroke volume or cardiac output. For example, a QRS complex signifies <em>ventricular depolarisation</em>, not the actual volume of blood being ejected. Electrical activity can be present without an effective pulse.</p>\n<div class=\"ecg-concept-line\" aria-label=\"Electrical and mechanical sequence\"><span>Electrical activation</span><b>→</b><span>Myocardial contraction</span><b>→</b><span>Pressure changes</span><b>→</b><span>Blood movement</span></div>\n<p><strong>Why does this distinction matter?</strong> An ECG can show organised electrical complexes even if ventricular contraction produces little or no effective circulation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-why-can-the-tracing-move-upwards-or-downwards\">2. Why can the tracing move upwards or downwards?</h2>\n<p>An <strong>electrode</strong> is a physical contact on the patient's skin. A <strong>lead</strong> is a particular electrical view calculated from electrode potentials. Different leads view the same heart from different directions, so the same heartbeat can produce different shapes across leads.</p>\n<p>As activation spreads through the myocardium, we can represent its combined direction and magnitude by a changing electrical <strong><span data-term=\"ecg-vector\">vector</span></strong>. The ECG lead detects the part of that vector directed along its positive recording direction.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>A depolarisation wave travels…</th><th>Recorded tendency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Towards</strong> a lead's positive direction</td><td>Positive (upward) deflection</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Away from</strong> its positive direction</td><td>Negative (downward) deflection</td></tr><tr><td>Roughly <strong>perpendicular</strong></td><td>Small or biphasic deflection</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>For example, much of normal ventricular depolarisation is directed inferiorly and towards the patient's left. Since Lead II views the heart in that general direction, its QRS complex is often predominantly upright.</p>\n<p><strong>Important:</strong> These rules describe <strong>depolarisation</strong>. Repolarisation has a different electrical polarity: a repolarisation wave travelling towards a positive electrode tends to produce a negative deflection. The normal ventricular T wave can therefore be upright even though it represents repolarisation, because the spread and polarity of repolarisation differ from depolarisation.</p>\n<p>An upward deflection is <strong>not</strong> a drawing of the heart moving upwards—it is the electrical signal's projection onto that lead.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-can-ten-electrodes-produce-twelve-ecg-leads\">3. Why can ten electrodes produce twelve ECG leads?</h2>\n<p>The standard <strong>12-lead ECG</strong> uses <strong>10 physical electrodes</strong>: four on the limbs and six on the chest. Their potentials are combined to produce twelve electrical views.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Lead group</th><th>Views</th><th>Names</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bipolar limb</td><td>3</td><td>I, II, III</td></tr><tr><td>Augmented limb</td><td>3</td><td>aVR, aVL, aVF</td></tr><tr><td>Precordial (chest)</td><td>6</td><td>V1–V6</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <strong>right-leg (RL)</strong> electrode mainly helps suppress electrical interference; it is not a thirteenth lead. The six limb leads observe the heart in the <strong>frontal plane</strong>, while the six chest leads add views in the <strong>horizontal plane</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-three-bipolar-limb-leads\">The three bipolar limb leads</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Lead</th><th>Negative electrode</th><th>Positive electrode</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>I</strong></td><td>Right arm (RA)</td><td>Left arm (LA)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>II</strong></td><td>Right arm (RA)</td><td>Left leg (LL)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>III</strong></td><td>Left arm (LA)</td><td>Left leg (LL)</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>These directions form <strong>Einthoven's triangle</strong> (lecturer slide 3). Their simultaneous voltages obey <strong>Einthoven's law</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Einthoven's law</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>II</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>III</mi></msub></math></div>\n<p>For instance, if Lead I measures +0.5 mV and Lead III measures +0.3 mV at the same instant, Lead II should measure <strong>+0.8 mV</strong> (ignoring recording error).</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration ecg-figure-small\" id=\"ecg-einthoven\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/ECG-Einthoven-triangle.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/ECG-Einthoven-triangle.svg\" alt=\"Diagram of Einthoven triangle connecting arm and leg lead positions with bipolar Leads I, II and III.\" width=\"992\" height=\"744\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 01. Bipolar limb-lead directions.</strong> Relate the positive and negative recording directions to the three limb leads. <span class=\"figure-credit\">Kychot · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ECG-Einthoven-triangle.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h3 id=\"the-augmented-limb-leads\">The augmented limb leads</h3>\n<p><strong>aVR</strong> views towards the right arm, <strong>aVL</strong> towards the left arm and <strong>aVF</strong> towards the left leg/foot (lecturer slide 4). Each uses one positive limb electrode and a reference derived from the other limb electrodes. The normal mean ventricular depolarisation vector usually moves away from aVR, so its QRS complex is commonly predominantly negative.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-six-chest-leads\">The six chest leads</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Electrode</th><th>Standard position</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>V1</strong></td><td>Fourth right intercostal space, right sternal border</td></tr><tr><td><strong>V2</strong></td><td>Fourth left intercostal space, left sternal border</td></tr><tr><td><strong>V3</strong></td><td>Midway between V2 and V4</td></tr><tr><td><strong>V4</strong></td><td>Fifth left intercostal space, midclavicular line</td></tr><tr><td><strong>V5</strong></td><td>Anterior axillary line, horizontal with V4</td></tr><tr><td><strong>V6</strong></td><td>Midaxillary line, horizontal with V4</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>For accurate placement, locate <strong>V4 before V3</strong>, then put V3 midway between V2 and V4. Incorrect placement can alter the tracing and create misleading findings.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration ecg-figure-small\" id=\"ecg-precordial\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c4/Precordial_Leads_3.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c4/Precordial_Leads_3.svg\" alt=\"Anterior torso and rib cage with six labelled ECG chest electrodes V1 to V6 in their correct anatomical positions.\" width=\"287\" height=\"252\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 02. V1–V6 chest electrode positions.</strong> The diagram shows where electrodes go; the table adds intercostal-space landmarks. <span class=\"figure-credit\">Jmarchn · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Precordial_Leads_3.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2 id=\"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference\">4. Waves, segments and intervals: what is the difference?</h2>\n<p>The ECG tracing is made up of <strong>waves</strong>, <strong>segments</strong> and <strong>intervals</strong>. These words are not interchangeable:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Wave:</strong> A deflection associated with a change in the heart's summed electrical activity (e.g. P or T).</li><li><strong>Segment:</strong> The part <strong>between</strong> specified waves (e.g. PR segment or ST segment).</li><li><strong>Interval:</strong> A measured span including <strong>one or more waves</strong> and often a segment (e.g. PR or QT interval).</li></ul>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration ecg-figure-signal\" id=\"ecg-normal-wave\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/SinusRhythmLabels.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/SinusRhythmLabels.svg\" alt=\"Labeled normal ECG with P wave, QRS complex, T wave, PR segment and interval, ST segment and QT interval.\" width=\"512\" height=\"512\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 03. Normal ECG waves and intervals.</strong> Compare the <em>PR segment</em> (end of P to start of QRS) with the <em>PR interval</em> (start of P to start of QRS). Open to enlarge. <span class=\"figure-credit\">Agateller (Anthony Atkielski), SVG conversion by atom · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SinusRhythmLabels.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · public domain.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h3 id=\"p-wave-atrial-depolarisation\">P wave — Atrial depolarisation</h3>\n<p>The SA node initiates an impulse, which spreads through atrial myocardium. The <strong>P wave represents atrial myocardial depolarisation</strong>, not merely the firing of the tiny SA node. Atrial contraction follows. The lecturer gives an illustrative normal P-wave duration of <strong>0.08–0.10 seconds</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pr-interval-time-from-atrial-to-ventricular-depolarisation\">PR interval — Time from atrial to ventricular depolarisation</h3>\n<p>The <strong>PR interval</strong> starts at the <strong>beginning of the P wave</strong> and ends at the <strong>beginning of QRS</strong>. It is normally about <strong>0.12–0.20 seconds</strong>. It includes atrial depolarisation and conduction through the AV node and His–Purkinje system up to the start of ventricular activation. <strong>The whole PR interval is not simply the AV nodal delay.</strong></p>\n<p>In contrast, the <strong>PR segment</strong> runs from the <strong>end</strong> of P to the <strong>start</strong> of QRS. Keep the starting points distinct when answering exam questions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"qrs-complex-ventricular-depolarisation\">QRS complex — Ventricular depolarisation</h3>\n<p>The <strong>QRS complex</strong> represents ventricular depolarisation. Ventricular muscle mass is much greater than atrial mass, so its summed signal is typically larger than the P wave. The lecturer quotes a QRS duration of approximately <strong>0.06–0.10 seconds</strong>; a duration <strong>under 0.12 seconds</strong> is a common broad normal clinical criterion.</p>\n<p><strong>Q</strong> is the first negative deflection before a positive R; <strong>R</strong> is a positive deflection; <strong>S</strong> is a negative deflection after an R. A particular lead need not show all three letters.</p>\n<p><strong>Why don't we see atrial repolarisation separately?</strong> It usually overlaps with ventricular depolarisation, and its smaller signal is masked by QRS—not absent.</p>\n<h3 id=\"st-segment-a-nearly-flat-line-during-continued-ventricular-electrical-activity\">ST segment — A nearly flat line during continued ventricular electrical activity</h3>\n<p>The <strong>ST segment</strong> extends from the <strong>end of QRS (J point)</strong> to the <strong>beginning of the T wave</strong>. During much of this interval, ventricular myocardial cells are in relatively similar depolarised states, so the lead often detects little net voltage difference.</p>\n<p><strong>Flat does not mean inactive.</strong> The ST segment roughly corresponds to the ventricular action-potential plateau, not to a period when the cells have returned to rest. ST elevation and depression can have clinical importance, including myocardial ischaemia, but require appropriate context.</p>\n<h3 id=\"t-wave-ventricular-repolarisation\">T wave — Ventricular repolarisation</h3>\n<p>The <strong>T wave</strong> reflects the spread of ventricular repolarisation. Repolarisation is not instantaneous and may give a broader waveform than QRS. The normal T-wave direction reflects both the spatial sequence of recovery and the polarity of repolarisation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"u-wave-sometimes-present-origin-uncertain\">U wave — Sometimes present, origin uncertain</h3>\n<p>A small <strong>U wave</strong> may follow the T wave. The lecture calls it the final remnants of ventricular repolarisation; this is a traditional teaching description. <strong>Its precise source is still debated</strong>, so we should not present a specific proposed mechanism as settled fact.</p>\n<h3 id=\"qt-interval-ventricular-activation-through-recovery\">QT interval — Ventricular activation through recovery</h3>\n<p>The <strong>QT interval</strong> extends from the <strong>start of QRS to the end of the T wave</strong>. It includes ventricular depolarisation and repolarisation. The lecturer gives an illustrative range of <strong>0.2–0.4 seconds</strong>, noting that QT varies with heart rate. Its duration shortens or lengthens with heart rate, so a single fixed normal QT value is misleading.</p>\n<p><strong>Supplementary: heart-rate correction.</strong> Bazett's formula is a commonly taught approximation, although it is less reliable at unusually low or high heart rates.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Bazett's QT correction</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>QT</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>QT</mi><msqrt><mi>RR</mi></msqrt></mfrac></math><span class=\"equation-example\">Use QT and RR measurements in seconds; interpret the result in clinical context.</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"5-connect-the-ecg-to-mechanical-events\">5. Connect the ECG to mechanical events</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>ECG feature</th><th>Electrical event</th><th>Mechanical connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P wave</td><td>Atrial depolarisation</td><td>Atrial contraction follows</td></tr><tr><td>PR interval</td><td>Atrial-to-ventricular activation time</td><td>Includes the AV delay that supports final filling</td></tr><tr><td>QRS complex</td><td>Ventricular depolarisation</td><td>Ventricular contraction follows</td></tr><tr><td>ST segment</td><td>Much ventricular myocardium remains depolarised</td><td>Sustained ventricular contraction/ejection</td></tr><tr><td>T wave</td><td>Ventricular repolarisation</td><td>Mechanical relaxation develops, but not instantaneously</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>This connects directly with Chapter 04's Wiggers diagram: <strong>the ECG measures the electrical changes; the Wiggers diagram also shows pressure, volume and valve events</strong>. The electrical and mechanical curves are connected but are not interchangeable.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-how-do-ecg-paper-squares-represent-time-and-voltage\">6. How do ECG paper squares represent time and voltage?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer's standard settings are <strong>25 mm/s</strong> (paper speed) and <strong>10 mm/mV</strong> (voltage gain).</p>\n<div class=\"ecg-calibration-mini\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"ECG paper calibration\"><div class=\"ecg-paper-sample\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></div><div><strong>At 25 mm/s</strong><p>1 small 1-mm horizontal square = <strong>0.04 s</strong><br>1 large 5-mm horizontal square = <strong>0.20 s</strong></p><p><strong>At 10 mm/mV:</strong> 1 small vertical square = <strong>0.1 mV</strong>; 1 large vertical square = <strong>0.5 mV</strong>.</p></div></div>\n<p><strong>Why is this true?</strong> At 25 mm/s, the paper moves 25 mm in one second. Each 1-mm horizontal square therefore spans <strong>1 ÷ 25 = 0.04 s</strong>; five such squares span <strong>0.20 s</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Do not confuse axes:</strong> Horizontal spacing measures time; vertical height measures voltage. A taller QRS does not mean a longer QRS duration.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-calculate-heart-rate-from-the-tracing\">7. Calculate heart rate from the tracing</h2>\n<p>For a <strong>regular rhythm</strong>, measure the R–R interval. Since 60 seconds make one minute:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">General relationship</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>HR</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>60</mn><mrow><mi>RR</mi><mo> </mo><mtext>in seconds</mtext></mrow></mfrac></math></div>\n<p>At 25 mm/s, the common shortcuts follow from 1500 small or 300 large squares passing per minute:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">25 mm/s · regular rhythm</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>HR</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1500</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>small squares</mtext></msub></mfrac></math><span class=\"equation-example\">Or 300 ÷ number of large squares between consecutive R waves.</span></div>\n<h3 id=\"worked-example-the-lecturers-22-small-squares\">Worked example: the lecturer's 22 small squares</h3>\n<p>If an R–R interval spans <strong>22 small squares</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong>RR duration = 22 × 0.04 s = 0.88 s</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Heart rate = 60 ÷ 0.88 ≈ 68 beats/min</strong>, equivalently 1500 ÷ 22 ≈ 68.</p>\n<p><strong>Important observation about slide 14:</strong> Not all its R–R intervals appear equal. The calculation <strong>1500 ÷ 22</strong> gives the rate represented by <em>that particular interval</em>. It should not automatically be called the average rate for the whole irregular tracing.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-changes-if-the-rhythm-is-irregular\">What changes if the rhythm is irregular?</h3>\n<p>When R–R intervals vary, a single pair of R waves is insufficient to estimate the average. One simple method is to count ventricular complexes in a <strong>6-second strip</strong> and multiply by <strong>10</strong> (supplementary).</p>\n<p><em>Example:</em> 8 QRS complexes in 6 seconds → approximate average <strong>80 beats/min</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-if-paper-speed-is-50-mms\">What if paper speed is 50 mm/s?</h3>\n<p>Each small horizontal square then represents <strong>0.02 s</strong>, so the shortcuts become <strong>3000 ÷ small squares</strong> or <strong>600 ÷ large squares</strong>. Always check the paper speed before using a shortcut.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-basic-rhythm-assessment-supplementary\">8. Basic rhythm assessment (supplementary)</h2>\n<p>The lecture's central focus is ECG fundamentals, not a full arrhythmia course. Still, an initial rhythm assessment connects the tracing to its purpose.</p>\n<p>Ask four questions: <strong>Is the ventricular rate appropriate? Are the R–R intervals regular? Are consistent P waves present before QRS? Is conduction through PR and QRS appropriate?</strong></p>\n<p>A normal sinus rhythm generally shows P waves of consistent shape, with an appropriate relationship between each P and the following QRS and a reasonably regular rhythm. For healthy adults at rest, <strong>60–100 beats/min</strong> is a common reference range, though slower rates can be normal in particular circumstances.</p>\n<h3 id=\"recognition-of-av-conduction-disturbance\">Recognition of AV conduction disturbance</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>PR >0.20 s</strong>, every P followed by QRS</td><td>First-degree AV block</td></tr><tr><td>Some P waves <strong>not followed by QRS</strong></td><td>Second-degree AV block</td></tr><tr><td>P waves and QRS complexes follow <strong>independent rhythms</strong></td><td>Complete (third-degree) AV block</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Mechanism:</strong> These patterns concern how the atrial impulse is delayed or fails to reach ventricular myocardium. Their detailed subtypes are supplementary and require additional ECG practice.</p>\n<p>A normal-looking ECG cannot guarantee effective cardiac output: <strong>electrical activity does not prove adequate mechanical pumping</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. How can skin electrodes record activity generated inside the heart?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Cardiac depolarisation and repolarisation produce electrical potential differences that are conducted through body tissues. Electrodes detect differences between skin locations, so the ECG can record the combined electrical activity without touching the myocardium. It records electricity, not the strength of contraction.</p></details>\n<p><strong>2. Why do ten electrodes produce twelve ECG leads?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Electrodes are recording contacts; leads are calculated electrical views.</strong> Four limb electrodes and six precordial electrodes provide three bipolar limb views, three augmented limb views and six chest views. The right-leg electrode helps reduce interference rather than producing another lead.</p></details>\n<p><strong>3. If a depolarisation vector moves away from the positive direction of Lead II, what deflection is expected?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Predominantly negative (downward).</strong> A lead displays the projection of the heart's electrical vector onto its positive recording direction. Depolarisation moving away from that direction generates a negative deflection; moving towards it produces a positive one.</p></details>\n<p><strong>4. Explain what Einthoven's law means if Leads I and III measure +0.5 and +0.3 mV at the same instant.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Lead II = +0.8 mV</strong>, because V<sub>II</sub> = V<sub>I</sub> + V<sub>III</sub>. This relationship follows from how the three bipolar limb leads use the right-arm, left-arm and left-leg electrode potentials. The values must be compared at the same instant.</p></details>\n<p><strong>5. The QRS complex is larger than the P wave. Which electrical event does each represent, and why is the difference expected?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>QRS is ventricular depolarisation; P is atrial depolarisation.</strong> The ventricles have greater muscle mass, so the summed surface electrical signal during ventricular activation is usually much larger. The small atrial repolarisation signal is normally hidden within QRS.</p></details>\n<p><strong>6. A student measures the PR segment and calls it the PR interval. What is missing?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>The P wave itself.</strong> The PR segment starts at the end of P and ends at the beginning of QRS; the PR interval starts at the <em>beginning</em> of P. Therefore the PR interval includes atrial depolarisation plus subsequent conduction time, not just AV nodal delay.</p></details>\n<p><strong>7. Why can the ST segment lie near the baseline while most ventricular cells remain depolarised?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>During much of the ST segment, ventricular regions are in relatively similar depolarised states. The lead therefore detects only a small net electrical potential difference. A flat segment does <strong>not</strong> indicate that the ventricle has become electrically inactive.</p></details>\n<p><strong>8. At 25 mm/s, two R waves are separated by 22 small squares. Calculate the rate.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Each small horizontal square lasts 0.04 s. The RR interval is 22 × 0.04 = <strong>0.88 s</strong>. Rate = 60 ÷ 0.88 ≈ <strong>68 beats/min</strong> (also 1500 ÷ 22). If other R–R intervals differ, this is only the beat-to-beat rate for the chosen interval.</p></details>\n<p><strong>9. Why does the 1500 rule need adjustment at a paper speed of 50 mm/s?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The paper travels twice as far per second, so each small square lasts 0.02 s rather than 0.04 s. There are now 3000 small squares per minute; the corresponding regular-rhythm shortcut is <strong>3000 ÷ small squares</strong> (or 600 ÷ large squares).</p></details>\n<p><strong>10. An irregular ECG has eight QRS complexes in six seconds. Estimate its average ventricular rate.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Approximately 80 beats/min.</strong> Eight beats in six seconds correspond to 8 × (60 ÷ 6) = 80 beats/min. For an irregular rhythm, counting complexes over a longer interval is more representative than applying the 1500 rule to one R–R interval.</p></details>\n<p><strong>11. Each P wave is followed by QRS, but the PR interval is 0.24 seconds. What pattern does this suggest?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>First-degree AV block.</strong> PR is prolonged beyond 0.20 s, yet each atrial impulse still conducts to the ventricles. The finding indicates delayed AV transmission, not dropped beats or complete AV dissociation.</p></details>\n<p><strong>12. Can a regular ECG tracing prove that the ventricles are pumping enough blood?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> An ECG measures electrical activity. Electrical impulses may occur without effective myocardial contraction or sufficient stroke volume, so perfusion requires assessment of the pulse, blood pressure and clinical state.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>ECG:</strong> Records electrical potential differences detected at the body surface, not blood pressure or contraction.</li><li><strong>Twelve leads, ten electrodes:</strong> Three bipolar limb, three augmented limb and six chest leads.</li><li><strong>Vector rule:</strong> Depolarisation towards a positive lead produces a positive deflection; away produces a negative deflection.</li><li><strong>Lead placement:</strong> I, II and III use limb-electrode differences; V1–V6 have specific chest landmarks.</li><li><strong>P wave:</strong> Atrial myocardial depolarisation; <strong>QRS:</strong> ventricular depolarisation; <strong>T wave:</strong> ventricular repolarisation.</li><li><strong>PR interval:</strong> Start of P to start of QRS, normally about 0.12–0.20 s; do not confuse with PR segment or pure AV nodal delay.</li><li><strong>QRS duration:</strong> About 0.06–0.10 s in the lecturer's examples; often less than 0.12 s clinically.</li><li><strong>ST segment:</strong> Ventricular myocardium remains largely depolarised; a flat tracing does not mean inactivity.</li><li><strong>QT interval:</strong> Start of QRS to end of T; varies with heart rate. U-wave origin remains unsettled.</li><li><strong>Calibration at 25 mm/s and 10 mm/mV:</strong> Small square 0.04 s horizontally and 0.1 mV vertically; large square 0.20 s.</li><li><strong>Heart rate:</strong> Regular rhythm HR = 1500 ÷ small squares or 300 ÷ large squares at 25 mm/s; use longer strips to estimate irregular rhythms.</li><li><strong>Basic rhythm assessment:</strong> Rate, regularity, P waves, PR and QRS; ECG electrical activity does not guarantee cardiac output.</li></ol>","plain":"Electrocardiography and Basic Rhythms Primary lecture: Lecture 7 ECG 2.pdf, slides 1–14. Supplementary foundations: vector reasoning, accurate lead positions, basic rhythm assessment and AV block recognition. 1. What does an ECG actually record? Every heartbeat is preceded by cardiac electrical activation . As myocardial cells depolarise and repolarise, electrical potential differences are produced and conducted through body tissues. Electrodes on the skin detect these differences. The electrocardiogram (ECG, or EKG) is a recording of this electrical activity over time. An ECG does not directly measure contraction strength, pressure, stroke volume or cardiac output. For example, a QRS complex signifies ventricular depolarisation , not the actual volume of blood being ejected. Electrical activity can be present without an effective pulse. Electrical activation → Myocardial contraction → Pressure changes → Blood movement Why does this distinction matter? An ECG can show organised electrical complexes even if ventricular contraction produces little or no effective circulation. 2. Why can the tracing move upwards or downwards? An electrode is a physical contact on the patient's skin. A lead is a particular electrical view calculated from electrode potentials. Different leads view the same heart from different directions, so the same heartbeat can produce different shapes across leads. As activation spreads through the myocardium, we can represent its combined direction and magnitude by a changing electrical vector . The ECG lead detects the part of that vector directed along its positive recording direction. A depolarisation wave travels… Recorded tendency Towards a lead's positive direction Positive (upward) deflection Away from its positive direction Negative (downward) deflection Roughly perpendicular Small or biphasic deflection For example, much of normal ventricular depolarisation is directed inferiorly and towards the patient's left. Since Lead II views the heart in that general direction, its QRS complex is often predominantly upright. Important: These rules describe depolarisation . Repolarisation has a different electrical polarity: a repolarisation wave travelling towards a positive electrode tends to produce a negative deflection. The normal ventricular T wave can therefore be upright even though it represents repolarisation, because the spread and polarity of repolarisation differ from depolarisation. An upward deflection is not a drawing of the heart moving upwards—it is the electrical signal's projection onto that lead. 3. Why can ten electrodes produce twelve ECG leads? The standard 12-lead ECG uses 10 physical electrodes : four on the limbs and six on the chest. Their potentials are combined to produce twelve electrical views. Lead group Views Names Bipolar limb 3 I, II, III Augmented limb 3 aVR, aVL, aVF Precordial (chest) 6 V1–V6 The right-leg (RL) electrode mainly helps suppress electrical interference; it is not a thirteenth lead. The six limb leads observe the heart in the frontal plane , while the six chest leads add views in the horizontal plane . The three bipolar limb leads Lead Negative electrode Positive electrode I Right arm (RA) Left arm (LA) II Right arm (RA) Left leg (LL) III Left arm (LA) Left leg (LL) These directions form Einthoven's triangle (lecturer slide 3). Their simultaneous voltages obey Einthoven's law : Einthoven's law V II = V I + V III For instance, if Lead I measures +0.5 mV and Lead III measures +0.3 mV at the same instant, Lead II should measure +0.8 mV (ignoring recording error). Figure 01. Bipolar limb-lead directions. Relate the positive and negative recording directions to the three limb leads. Kychot · Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · unmodified. The augmented limb leads aVR views towards the right arm, aVL towards the left arm and aVF towards the left leg/foot (lecturer slide 4). Each uses one positive limb electrode and a reference derived from the other limb electrodes. The normal mean ventricular depolarisation vector usually moves away from aVR, so its QRS complex is commonly predominantly negative. The six chest leads Electrode Standard position V1 Fourth right intercostal space, right sternal border V2 Fourth left intercostal space, left sternal border V3 Midway between V2 and V4 V4 Fifth left intercostal space, midclavicular line V5 Anterior axillary line, horizontal with V4 V6 Midaxillary line, horizontal with V4 For accurate placement, locate V4 before V3 , then put V3 midway between V2 and V4. Incorrect placement can alter the tracing and create misleading findings. Figure 02. V1–V6 chest electrode positions. The diagram shows where electrodes go; the table adds intercostal-space landmarks. Jmarchn · Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · unmodified. 4. Waves, segments and intervals: what is the difference? The ECG tracing is made up of waves , segments and intervals . These words are not interchangeable: Wave: A deflection associated with a change in the heart's summed electrical activity (e.g. P or T). Segment: The part between specified waves (e.g. PR segment or ST segment). Interval: A measured span including one or more waves and often a segment (e.g. PR or QT interval). Figure 03. Normal ECG waves and intervals. Compare the PR segment (end of P to start of QRS) with the PR interval (start of P to start of QRS). Open to enlarge. Agateller (Anthony Atkielski), SVG conversion by atom · Wikimedia Commons · public domain. P wave — Atrial depolarisation The SA node initiates an impulse, which spreads through atrial myocardium. The P wave represents atrial myocardial depolarisation , not merely the firing of the tiny SA node. Atrial contraction follows. The lecturer gives an illustrative normal P-wave duration of 0.08–0.10 seconds . PR interval — Time from atrial to ventricular depolarisation The PR interval starts at the beginning of the P wave and ends at the beginning of QRS . It is normally about 0.12–0.20 seconds . It includes atrial depolarisation and conduction through the AV node and His–Purkinje system up to the start of ventricular activation. The whole PR interval is not simply the AV nodal delay. In contrast, the PR segment runs from the end of P to the start of QRS. Keep the starting points distinct when answering exam questions. QRS complex — Ventricular depolarisation The QRS complex represents ventricular depolarisation. Ventricular muscle mass is much greater than atrial mass, so its summed signal is typically larger than the P wave. The lecturer quotes a QRS duration of approximately 0.06–0.10 seconds ; a duration under 0.12 seconds is a common broad normal clinical criterion. Q is the first negative deflection before a positive R; R is a positive deflection; S is a negative deflection after an R. A particular lead need not show all three letters. Why don't we see atrial repolarisation separately? It usually overlaps with ventricular depolarisation, and its smaller signal is masked by QRS—not absent. ST segment — A nearly flat line during continued ventricular electrical activity The ST segment extends from the end of QRS (J point) to the beginning of the T wave . During much of this interval, ventricular myocardial cells are in relatively similar depolarised states, so the lead often detects little net voltage difference. Flat does not mean inactive. The ST segment roughly corresponds to the ventricular action-potential plateau, not to a period when the cells have returned to rest. ST elevation and depression can have clinical importance, including myocardial ischaemia, but require appropriate context. T wave — Ventricular repolarisation The T wave reflects the spread of ventricular repolarisation. Repolarisation is not instantaneous and may give a broader waveform than QRS. The normal T-wave direction reflects both the spatial sequence of recovery and the polarity of repolarisation. U wave — Sometimes present, origin uncertain A small U wave may follow the T wave. The lecture calls it the final remnants of ventricular repolarisation; this is a traditional teaching description. Its precise source is still debated , so we should not present a specific proposed mechanism as settled fact. QT interval — Ventricular activation through recovery The QT interval extends from the start of QRS to the end of the T wave . It includes ventricular depolarisation and repolarisation. The lecturer gives an illustrative range of 0.2–0.4 seconds , noting that QT varies with heart rate. Its duration shortens or lengthens with heart rate, so a single fixed normal QT value is misleading. Supplementary: heart-rate correction. Bazett's formula is a commonly taught approximation, although it is less reliable at unusually low or high heart rates. Bazett's QT correction QT c = QT RR Use QT and RR measurements in seconds; interpret the result in clinical context. 5. Connect the ECG to mechanical events ECG feature Electrical event Mechanical connection P wave Atrial depolarisation Atrial contraction follows PR interval Atrial-to-ventricular activation time Includes the AV delay that supports final filling QRS complex Ventricular depolarisation Ventricular contraction follows ST segment Much ventricular myocardium remains depolarised Sustained ventricular contraction/ejection T wave Ventricular repolarisation Mechanical relaxation develops, but not instantaneously This connects directly with Chapter 04's Wiggers diagram: the ECG measures the electrical changes; the Wiggers diagram also shows pressure, volume and valve events . The electrical and mechanical curves are connected but are not interchangeable. 6. How do ECG paper squares represent time and voltage? The lecturer's standard settings are 25 mm/s (paper speed) and 10 mm/mV (voltage gain). At 25 mm/s 1 small 1-mm horizontal square = 0.04 s 1 large 5-mm horizontal square = 0.20 s At 10 mm/mV: 1 small vertical square = 0.1 mV ; 1 large vertical square = 0.5 mV . Why is this true? At 25 mm/s, the paper moves 25 mm in one second. Each 1-mm horizontal square therefore spans 1 ÷ 25 = 0.04 s ; five such squares span 0.20 s . Do not confuse axes: Horizontal spacing measures time; vertical height measures voltage. A taller QRS does not mean a longer QRS duration. 7. Calculate heart rate from the tracing For a regular rhythm , measure the R–R interval. Since 60 seconds make one minute: General relationship HR = 60 RR in seconds At 25 mm/s, the common shortcuts follow from 1500 small or 300 large squares passing per minute: 25 mm/s · regular rhythm HR = 1500 N small squares Or 300 ÷ number of large squares between consecutive R waves. Worked example: the lecturer's 22 small squares If an R–R interval spans 22 small squares : RR duration = 22 × 0.04 s = 0.88 s Heart rate = 60 ÷ 0.88 ≈ 68 beats/min , equivalently 1500 ÷ 22 ≈ 68. Important observation about slide 14: Not all its R–R intervals appear equal. The calculation 1500 ÷ 22 gives the rate represented by that particular interval . It should not automatically be called the average rate for the whole irregular tracing. What changes if the rhythm is irregular? When R–R intervals vary, a single pair of R waves is insufficient to estimate the average. One simple method is to count ventricular complexes in a 6-second strip and multiply by 10 (supplementary). Example: 8 QRS complexes in 6 seconds → approximate average 80 beats/min . What if paper speed is 50 mm/s? Each small horizontal square then represents 0.02 s , so the shortcuts become 3000 ÷ small squares or 600 ÷ large squares . Always check the paper speed before using a shortcut. 8. Basic rhythm assessment (supplementary) The lecture's central focus is ECG fundamentals, not a full arrhythmia course. Still, an initial rhythm assessment connects the tracing to its purpose. Ask four questions: Is the ventricular rate appropriate? Are the R–R intervals regular? Are consistent P waves present before QRS? Is conduction through PR and QRS appropriate? A normal sinus rhythm generally shows P waves of consistent shape, with an appropriate relationship between each P and the following QRS and a reasonably regular rhythm. For healthy adults at rest, 60–100 beats/min is a common reference range, though slower rates can be normal in particular circumstances. Recognition of AV conduction disturbance Pattern Interpretation PR >0.20 s , every P followed by QRS First-degree AV block Some P waves not followed by QRS Second-degree AV block P waves and QRS complexes follow independent rhythms Complete (third-degree) AV block Mechanism: These patterns concern how the atrial impulse is delayed or fails to reach ventricular myocardium. Their detailed subtypes are supplementary and require additional ECG practice. A normal-looking ECG cannot guarantee effective cardiac output: electrical activity does not prove adequate mechanical pumping . Understanding Check 1. How can skin electrodes record activity generated inside the heart? Reveal explanation Cardiac depolarisation and repolarisation produce electrical potential differences that are conducted through body tissues. Electrodes detect differences between skin locations, so the ECG can record the combined electrical activity without touching the myocardium. It records electricity, not the strength of contraction. 2. Why do ten electrodes produce twelve ECG leads? Reveal explanation Electrodes are recording contacts; leads are calculated electrical views. Four limb electrodes and six precordial electrodes provide three bipolar limb views, three augmented limb views and six chest views. The right-leg electrode helps reduce interference rather than producing another lead. 3. If a depolarisation vector moves away from the positive direction of Lead II, what deflection is expected? Reveal explanation Predominantly negative (downward). A lead displays the projection of the heart's electrical vector onto its positive recording direction. Depolarisation moving away from that direction generates a negative deflection; moving towards it produces a positive one. 4. Explain what Einthoven's law means if Leads I and III measure +0.5 and +0.3 mV at the same instant. Reveal explanation Lead II = +0.8 mV , because V II = V I + V III . This relationship follows from how the three bipolar limb leads use the right-arm, left-arm and left-leg electrode potentials. The values must be compared at the same instant. 5. The QRS complex is larger than the P wave. Which electrical event does each represent, and why is the difference expected? Reveal explanation QRS is ventricular depolarisation; P is atrial depolarisation. The ventricles have greater muscle mass, so the summed surface electrical signal during ventricular activation is usually much larger. The small atrial repolarisation signal is normally hidden within QRS. 6. A student measures the PR segment and calls it the PR interval. What is missing? Reveal explanation The P wave itself. The PR segment starts at the end of P and ends at the beginning of QRS; the PR interval starts at the beginning of P. Therefore the PR interval includes atrial depolarisation plus subsequent conduction time, not just AV nodal delay. 7. Why can the ST segment lie near the baseline while most ventricular cells remain depolarised? Reveal explanation During much of the ST segment, ventricular regions are in relatively similar depolarised states. The lead therefore detects only a small net electrical potential difference. A flat segment does not indicate that the ventricle has become electrically inactive. 8. At 25 mm/s, two R waves are separated by 22 small squares. Calculate the rate. Reveal explanation Each small horizontal square lasts 0.04 s. The RR interval is 22 × 0.04 = 0.88 s . Rate = 60 ÷ 0.88 ≈ 68 beats/min (also 1500 ÷ 22). If other R–R intervals differ, this is only the beat-to-beat rate for the chosen interval. 9. Why does the 1500 rule need adjustment at a paper speed of 50 mm/s? Reveal explanation The paper travels twice as far per second, so each small square lasts 0.02 s rather than 0.04 s. There are now 3000 small squares per minute; the corresponding regular-rhythm shortcut is 3000 ÷ small squares (or 600 ÷ large squares). 10. An irregular ECG has eight QRS complexes in six seconds. Estimate its average ventricular rate. Reveal explanation Approximately 80 beats/min. Eight beats in six seconds correspond to 8 × (60 ÷ 6) = 80 beats/min. For an irregular rhythm, counting complexes over a longer interval is more representative than applying the 1500 rule to one R–R interval. 11. Each P wave is followed by QRS, but the PR interval is 0.24 seconds. What pattern does this suggest? Reveal explanation First-degree AV block. PR is prolonged beyond 0.20 s, yet each atrial impulse still conducts to the ventricles. The finding indicates delayed AV transmission, not dropped beats or complete AV dissociation. 12. Can a regular ECG tracing prove that the ventricles are pumping enough blood? Reveal explanation No. An ECG measures electrical activity. Electrical impulses may occur without effective myocardial contraction or sufficient stroke volume, so perfusion requires assessment of the pulse, blood pressure and clinical state. Chapter Essentials ECG: Records electrical potential differences detected at the body surface, not blood pressure or contraction. Twelve leads, ten electrodes: Three bipolar limb, three augmented limb and six chest leads. Vector rule: Depolarisation towards a positive lead produces a positive deflection; away produces a negative deflection. Lead placement: I, II and III use limb-electrode differences; V1–V6 have specific chest landmarks. P wave: Atrial myocardial depolarisation; QRS: ventricular depolarisation; T wave: ventricular repolarisation. PR interval: Start of P to start of QRS, normally about 0.12–0.20 s; do not confuse with PR segment or pure AV nodal delay. QRS duration: About 0.06–0.10 s in the lecturer's examples; often less than 0.12 s clinically. ST segment: Ventricular myocardium remains largely depolarised; a flat tracing does not mean inactivity. QT interval: Start of QRS to end of T; varies with heart rate. U-wave origin remains unsettled. Calibration at 25 mm/s and 10 mm/mV: Small square 0.04 s horizontally and 0.1 mV vertically; large square 0.20 s. Heart rate: Regular rhythm HR = 1500 ÷ small squares or 300 ÷ large squares at 25 mm/s; use longer strips to estimate irregular rhythms. Basic rhythm assessment: Rate, regularity, P waves, PR and QRS; ECG electrical activity does not guarantee cardiac output."},{"slug":"cardiac-output","title":"Cardiac Output and Frank-Starling Law","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"35-51","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-is-cardiac-output-and-why-does-it-matter","title":"1. What is cardiac output, and why does it matter?"},{"id":"2-why-can-changing-heart-rate-alter-cardiac-output","title":"2. Why can changing heart rate alter cardiac output?"},{"id":"3-what-determines-stroke-volume","title":"3. What determines stroke volume?"},{"id":"4-frankstarling-law-why-does-more-filling-produce-more-ejection","title":"4. Frank–Starling law: why does more filling produce more ejection?"},{"id":"5-how-do-we-read-frankstarling-curves","title":"5. How do we read Frank–Starling curves?"},{"id":"6-what-raises-or-lowers-end-diastolic-volume","title":"6. What raises or lowers end-diastolic volume?"},{"id":"7-what-changes-contractility","title":"7. What changes contractility?"},{"id":"8-how-do-preload-afterload-and-inotropy-differ-in-real-situations","title":"8. How do preload, afterload and inotropy differ in real situations?"},{"id":"9-what-happens-to-cardiac-output-during-exercise-and-other-states","title":"9. What happens to cardiac output during exercise and other states?"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1 id=\"cardiac-output-and-the-frankstarling-law\">Cardiac Output and the Frank–Starling Law</h1>\n<p><strong>Primary lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 35–51. <strong>Supplementary clarification:</strong> intrinsic sarcomere mechanisms, precise definitions of loading, interpretation of cardiac-function curves, and clinical examples.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-is-cardiac-output-and-why-does-it-matter\">1. What is cardiac output, and why does it matter?</h2>\n<p><strong>Cardiac output (CO)</strong> is the <strong>volume of blood ejected by one ventricle per unit time</strong>, usually expressed in <strong>litres per minute (L/min)</strong>. It tells us how much blood the heart delivers to a circulation—not how much blood is ejected in a single beat.</p>\n<p>Two quantities determine cardiac output:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Heart rate (HR):</strong> number of beats per minute.</li><li><strong>Stroke volume (SV):</strong> blood ejected from one ventricle <em>per beat</em>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Cardiac output</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>CO</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>HR</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>SV</mi></math><span class=\"equation-example\">Example: 75 beats/min × 70 mL/beat = <strong>5,250 mL/min = 5.25 L/min</strong></span></div>\n<p><strong>Why multiply?</strong> Each beat sends out one stroke volume. If the ventricle pumps 70 mL each time and beats 75 times in one minute, the total volume ejected in that minute is 75 × 70 mL.</p>\n<p>The lecturer illustrates a resting output around <strong>5.5 L/min</strong>. This is a representative value, not a universal constant: cardiac output varies with body size, activity and physiological conditions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-should-right-and-left-cardiac-outputs-be-approximately-equal\">Why should right and left cardiac outputs be approximately equal?</h3>\n<p>The pulmonary and systemic circulations are <strong>connected in series</strong>. Blood ejected by the right ventricle enters the lungs and ultimately returns to the left ventricle. If one side consistently pumped a greater volume per minute than the other, blood would progressively accumulate in one circulation. The two outputs therefore remain approximately equal <strong>over time</strong>, although small moment-to-moment differences can occur.</p>\n<h3 id=\"cardiac-index-allowing-for-body-size\">Cardiac index: allowing for body size</h3>\n<p>A person with a larger body generally needs more total blood flow than a smaller person. <strong>Cardiac index (CI)</strong> expresses cardiac output relative to <strong>body surface area (BSA)</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Cardiac index</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>CI</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>CO</mi><mi>BSA</mi></mfrac></math><span class=\"equation-example\">For CO = 5.25 L/min and BSA = 1.75 m²: <strong>CI = 3.0 L/min/m²</strong>.</span></div>\n<p>Unlike CO, cardiac index is expressed as <strong>L min⁻¹ m⁻²</strong>. It is a size-adjusted measure, not a different kind of blood flow.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-why-can-changing-heart-rate-alter-cardiac-output\">2. Why can changing heart rate alter cardiac output?</h2>\n<p>From <strong>CO = HR × SV</strong>, increasing either HR or SV can raise output <strong>if the other factor remains unchanged</strong>. In a living heart, however, they are connected.</p>\n<p>Sympathetic stimulation generally increases SA nodal firing (positive <strong><span data-term=\"chronotropy\">chronotropy</span></strong>) and makes ventricular contraction stronger (positive <strong><span data-term=\"inotropy\">inotropy</span></strong>). These effects can raise both HR and SV.</p>\n<p>But <strong>a faster heartbeat does not always mean more blood pumped per minute</strong>. At very high rates, diastole becomes so short that ventricular filling decreases. Reduced filling can lower SV enough to offset the rise in HR.</p>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> At 75 beats/min and 70 mL/beat, CO is <strong>5.25 L/min</strong>. At 130 beats/min but only 35 mL/beat, CO is <strong>4.55 L/min</strong>. Even though the heart beats faster, it pumps less blood per minute.</p>\n<div class=\"co-impact-strip\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Two determinants of cardiac output\"><span><strong>HR ↑</strong><small>More beats per minute</small></span><span><strong>SV ↓ markedly</strong><small>Less blood each beat</small></span><span><strong>CO may ↓</strong><small>Depends on their product</small></span></div>\n<p><strong>Reasoning rule:</strong> Never predict cardiac output from heart rate alone unless you know what happens to stroke volume.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-what-determines-stroke-volume\">3. What determines stroke volume?</h2>\n<p>From Chapter 04, <strong>SV = EDV − ESV</strong>: the amount ejected is the amount present at the end of filling minus the amount remaining after contraction.</p>\n<p>Three closely related factors determine how much is ejected:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>The question it answers</th><th>Typical effect on SV, if other conditions are fixed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong><span data-term=\"preload\">Preload</span></strong></td><td>How stretched is the ventricle before contraction?</td><td>More preload → usually higher SV within physiological limits</td></tr><tr><td><strong><span data-term=\"afterload\">Afterload</span></strong></td><td>How difficult is it to eject blood?</td><td>More afterload → usually lower SV acutely</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Contractility (inotropy)</strong></td><td>How strongly can the myocardium contract at the same loading?</td><td>Greater contractility → higher SV</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"preload-the-starting-stretch\">Preload — the starting stretch</h3>\n<p><strong>Preload</strong> refers to ventricular myocardial fibre stretch (and associated passive wall stress) <strong>at the end of diastole</strong>, just before contraction.</p>\n<p>More blood returning to a compliant ventricle usually increases <strong>end-diastolic volume (EDV)</strong> and stretches its fibres. EDV is therefore a useful teaching representation of preload. End-diastolic pressure is another clinical surrogate, but <strong>volume and pressure are not interchangeable</strong>, particularly in a stiff ventricle.</p>\n<p><strong>Analogy:</strong> Gently stretching an elastic band before releasing it can help it recoil more forcefully. It is only an analogy: cardiac muscle is active tissue, and excessive stretch does not produce unlimited pumping.</p>\n<h3 id=\"afterload-the-resistance-or-load-against-ejection\">Afterload — the resistance or load against ejection</h3>\n<p><strong>Afterload</strong> is the mechanical load the ventricle must overcome during ejection. On the left side it is related to <strong>aortic pressure, arterial properties and ventricular wall stress</strong>. Peripheral vascular resistance contributes to the load but is not identical to afterload.</p>\n<p>If aortic pressure rises suddenly while preload and contractility remain unchanged, the ventricle must generate more force before and during ejection; it will generally eject <strong>less blood in that beat</strong>, leaving a higher ESV.</p>\n<h3 id=\"contractility-how-capable-the-muscle-is-at-a-given-load\">Contractility — how capable the muscle is at a given load</h3>\n<p><strong>Contractility</strong> is the intrinsic ability of the myocardium to develop force <strong>when preload and afterload are held comparable</strong>. It depends strongly on intracellular Ca²⁺ cycling and the contractile machinery.</p>\n<p><strong>Important distinction:</strong> A ventricle that contracts more forcefully <em>because it filled more</em> is using the Frank–Starling mechanism; that does <strong>not automatically mean its intrinsic contractility increased</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-frankstarling-law-why-does-more-filling-produce-more-ejection\">4. Frank–Starling law: why does more filling produce more ejection?</h2>\n<p>Imagine more blood returning through the veins. The extra blood enters a ventricle during diastole and stretches its muscle fibres before the next beat.</p>\n<p>Within the normal operating range, increased initial sarcomere length improves force development during the next contraction. This reflects <strong>length-dependent activation</strong>, including improved myofilament sensitivity to Ca²⁺ and more favourable interaction between contractile proteins.</p>\n<div class=\"co-cause-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Frank Starling mechanism\"><span>Venous return ↑</span><b>→</b><span>EDV and fibre stretch ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Force generated ↑</span><b>→</b><span>SV ↑</span></div>\n<p>Thus, the <strong>Frank–Starling law</strong> states that <strong>within physiological limits, the heart tends to eject more blood when its fibres are stretched more during filling</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Why is this useful?</strong> It helps match the amount of blood ejected to the amount returning to the heart. If venous return increases, the ventricle can automatically increase SV without needing a new sympathetic command for every extra millilitre received.</p>\n<h3 id=\"does-greater-preload-always-increase-stroke-volume\">Does greater preload always increase stroke volume?</h3>\n<p><strong>No.</strong> The relationship is not unlimited. At high filling levels the curve becomes flatter, so additional volume yields progressively less extra output. A severely failing ventricle may be particularly unresponsive to extra filling while pressure rises enough to cause congestion.</p>\n<p>Lecture slide 39 includes a <strong>dashed descending limb</strong> at extreme stretch. That portion is a conceptual teaching extension: ordinary intact human hearts do <strong>not normally operate on a descending Frank–Starling limb</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-how-do-we-read-frankstarling-curves\">5. How do we read Frank–Starling curves?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer's slides 39–41 show <strong>ventricular performance</strong> plotted against filling. The usual horizontal axis is EDV or a surrogate of preload; the vertical axis may be <strong>stroke volume</strong> or <strong>cardiac output</strong>, depending on the particular diagram.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure cardiac-output-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Frank–Starling curves — preload versus pumping performance</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 640 295\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Schematic stroke-volume versus end-diastolic-volume graph showing increased, normal and reduced cardiac contractility curves.\"><line x1=\"85\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"85\" y2=\"238\" stroke=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".45\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"85\" y1=\"238\" x2=\"600\" y2=\"238\" stroke=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".45\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M99 229 C168 100 285 57 580 55\" stroke=\"#2863dd\" stroke-width=\"4.5\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M99 232 C188 181 275 148 580 148\" stroke=\"#16a085\" stroke-width=\"4.5\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M99 235 C205 222 320 208 580 207\" stroke=\"#c39431\" stroke-width=\"4.5\" fill=\"none\"/><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"13\"><text x=\"190\" y=\"273\">End-diastolic volume (preload) →</text><text x=\"22\" y=\"130\" transform=\"rotate(-90,22,130)\">Stroke volume</text><text x=\"387\" y=\"40\">Greater contractility</text><text x=\"425\" y=\"138\">Reference</text><text x=\"432\" y=\"196\">Lower contractility</text></g></svg><p><strong>Read across one curve:</strong> changing EDV changes stroke volume by the Frank–Starling mechanism. <strong>Compare curves vertically:</strong> at the same EDV, greater intrinsic contractility gives greater SV (with afterload held constant). Original DOMiNANCE schematic; not patient-specific measurements.</p></figure>\n<h3 id=\"a-moving-along-the-same-curve\">A. Moving <strong>along</strong> the same curve</h3>\n<p>Suppose a person lies down after standing. Venous pooling in the legs decreases, so more blood returns to the heart, EDV increases and the ventricle moves <strong>rightwards and upwards along a curve</strong>.</p>\n<p>This is primarily a change in <strong>preload</strong>, without requiring the intrinsic contractility of the myocardium to change.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-shifting-between-curves\">B. Shifting <strong>between</strong> curves</h3>\n<p>Now imagine two beats with the <strong>same EDV</strong>, but sympathetic stimulation has increased intracellular Ca²⁺ availability during the second beat. The second beat can eject a greater SV <strong>without additional stretch</strong>. This is a change in <strong>contractile state</strong>, represented by an <strong>upward function-curve shift</strong>.</p>\n<p>A fall in contractility, as in significant myocardial dysfunction, tends to produce a <strong>lower curve</strong>. Higher afterload can also lower ventricular performance at a given EDV, so <strong>a shifted curve is not proof of altered contractility unless afterload is accounted for</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-lecturers-unusual-terms-heterometric-and-homometric\">The lecturer's unusual terms: heterometric and homometric</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Typical example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Heterometric regulation</strong></td><td>Adjusting output through changes in the initial fibre length (preload)</td><td>Frank–Starling response to increased venous return</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Homometric regulation</strong></td><td>Adjusting force independently of the initial fibre length</td><td>β₁-mediated increase in contractility at the same EDV</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Exam insight:</strong> <em>Heterometric</em> changes the starting length; <em>homometric</em> changes pumping performance <strong>without needing a length change</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-what-raises-or-lowers-end-diastolic-volume\">6. What raises or lowers end-diastolic volume?</h2>\n<p><strong>EDV depends on how much blood returns, the time available to fill, and whether the ventricle can accommodate that blood.</strong> More venous return does not always translate into the same EDV change if ventricular compliance is impaired.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Often increases EDV</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Increased blood volume</td><td>More blood available to return to the heart</td></tr><tr><td>Increased venous tone</td><td>Shifts blood from compliant venous reservoirs towards the heart</td></tr><tr><td>Skeletal-muscle pump</td><td>Contracting muscles compress veins and promote flow through one-way valves</td></tr><tr><td>Respiratory pump</td><td>Breathing-related pressure changes can favour venous return</td></tr><tr><td>Effective atrial contraction</td><td>Completes late ventricular filling</td></tr><tr><td>Longer diastolic filling time</td><td>More time is available for blood to enter</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Often reduces EDV or limits effective filling</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Haemorrhage / hypovolaemia</td><td>Less circulating blood and lower venous return</td></tr><tr><td>Prolonged standing</td><td>Gravity promotes venous pooling in the legs</td></tr><tr><td>Pericardial effusion / tamponade</td><td>External pericardial pressure limits ventricular expansion</td></tr><tr><td>Reduced ventricular compliance</td><td>A stiff ventricle fills less readily for a given pressure</td></tr><tr><td>Severe tachycardia</td><td>Shortened diastole limits filling time</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The lecturer also mentions <strong>myocardial infarction and infiltrative disease</strong>, including endomyocardial fibrosis (EMF). These can impair relaxation, compliance and/or pumping; their effect on EDV is <strong>not universally a simple decrease</strong>. The precise direction depends on the disease and stage.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-what-changes-contractility\">7. What changes contractility?</h2>\n<p>Sympathetic cardiac nerves release <strong>noradrenaline (norepinephrine)</strong>, while the adrenal medulla releases circulating <strong>adrenaline (epinephrine)</strong> and some noradrenaline. These catecholamines stimulate <strong>β₁-adrenergic receptors</strong> in the heart.</p>\n<div class=\"co-cause-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Beta adrenergic inotropic pathway\"><span>β₁ receptor</span><b>→</b><span>Gs / adenylyl cyclase</span><b>→</b><span>cAMP / PKA ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Ca²⁺ cycling ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Stronger contraction</span></div>\n<p>PKA signalling increases L-type Ca²⁺ entry and enhances Ca²⁺ release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, allowing greater actin–myosin interaction. This produces <strong>positive inotropy</strong>. The same signalling can speed Ca²⁺ reuptake, producing <strong>positive <span data-term=\"lusitropy\">lusitropy</span></strong> (faster relaxation).</p>\n<p>The lecture cites <strong>caffeine and theophylline</strong> (xanthines). By inhibiting phosphodiesterase activity at sufficiently high concentrations, such agents can reduce cAMP breakdown, but their physiological and clinical effects are dose- and context-dependent. Do not assume everyday caffeine intake produces a predictable large positive inotropic response.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-about-the-vagus-nerve\">What about the vagus nerve?</h3>\n<p>The lecturer lists vagal stimulation as negative inotropic. <strong>Clarification:</strong> vagal effects are strong for <strong>heart rate</strong> and AV nodal conduction, but the <strong>direct parasympathetic effect on ventricular contractility is relatively small</strong>. Any fall in cardiac output may occur largely through changes in HR and filling rather than a large direct weakening of ventricular myocardium.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-can-depress-inotropy\">What can depress inotropy?</h3>\n<p>Lecture slide 48 lists <strong>hypercapnia, hypoxia, acidosis, selected drugs and cardiac failure</strong>. Severe myocardial hypoxia and acidosis can interfere with Ca²⁺ handling and cellular energy availability, reducing force generation; myocardial injury or failure may also reduce contractility. Responses to individual drugs depend on their specific mechanism.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-how-do-preload-afterload-and-inotropy-differ-in-real-situations\">8. How do preload, afterload and inotropy differ in real situations?</h2>\n<div class=\"co-compare-grid\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Three ways stroke volume changes\"><div><strong>More venous return</strong><span>Preload ↑</span><small>Often moves <em>along</em> the curve → SV ↑</small></div><div><strong>Higher aortic pressure</strong><span>Afterload ↑</span><small>Ejection becomes harder → SV may ↓</small></div><div><strong>β₁ stimulation</strong><span>Contractility ↑</span><small>More force at same EDV → SV ↑</small></div></div>\n<p><strong>Why are these comparisons useful?</strong> In a patient with blood loss, HR may rise through the sympathetic reflex, but low preload can still reduce SV and CO. In a patient with aortic stenosis, even a strong contraction faces a substantial outflow load. In a failing heart, giving more filling volume may only modestly increase SV if the ventricular function curve is flat.</p>\n<p>These examples show why no single determinant should be interpreted in isolation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-what-happens-to-cardiac-output-during-exercise-and-other-states\">9. What happens to cardiac output during exercise and other states?</h2>\n<p>During <strong>dynamic exercise</strong>, several processes usually occur together: sympathetic stimulation increases HR and inotropy; the skeletal-muscle and respiratory pumps increase <strong>venous return</strong>; and arterioles supplying active muscle dilate. CO can therefore increase substantially by changes in <strong>both</strong> HR and SV.</p>\n<p><strong>Lecture clarification:</strong> Slide 49 contains an ambiguous downward-arrow description of venous return during exercise. For ordinary dynamic exercise, the expected effect of muscle and respiratory pumping is generally <strong>increased</strong>, not decreased, venous return. The lecturer's diagrams on slides 50–51 also show increased venous return associated with the pumps.</p>\n<p>The lecture additionally mentions conditions associated with increased CO or cardiac demands: <strong>eating, high environmental temperature, pregnancy, anxiety/excitement, catecholamines and thyrotoxicosis</strong>. These conditions differ in mechanisms and magnitude; they are not interchangeable.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical connection — shock:</strong> During major haemorrhage, venous return and preload decrease. Sympathetic tachycardia and vasoconstriction can help maintain arterial pressure temporarily, but this does not guarantee normal CO or adequate tissue perfusion.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. A person has a heart rate of 75 beats/min and stroke volume of 70 mL. Calculate CO and state the correct units.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>CO = HR × SV = 75 × 70 = 5250 mL/min = 5.25 L/min.</strong> Heart rate counts beats per minute, while stroke volume gives the blood ejected per beat; multiplying cancels 'beats' and leaves volume per minute.</p></details>\n<p><strong>2. Why must right and left ventricular outputs be approximately equal over time, even though they pump against different pressures?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The systemic and pulmonary circuits are arranged <strong>in series</strong>: what leaves the right heart eventually returns to the left heart. A persistent unequal flow would cause blood to accumulate in one circuit and deplete the other. The left ventricle produces higher pressure, but not a greater long-term flow rate.</p></details>\n<p><strong>3. Heart rate rises from 75 to 130 beats/min, but SV falls from 70 to 35 mL. What happens to CO?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Initially, CO = 75 × 70 = <strong>5.25 L/min</strong>. Afterwards CO = 130 × 35 = <strong>4.55 L/min</strong>. Thus CO <strong>decreases</strong> despite tachycardia. Severe shortening of filling time can reduce SV enough to outweigh the increase in HR.</p></details>\n<p><strong>4. Two ventricular beats have the same EDV, but the second ejects a larger SV following sympathetic stimulation. Has preload necessarily increased?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> Same EDV implies comparable filling volume, while β₁ stimulation can increase Ca²⁺ entry and SR release, raising intrinsic contractility. At similar preload and afterload, this produces greater force and a larger SV, reflecting a <strong>higher ventricular function curve</strong> rather than merely movement along the original curve.</p></details>\n<p><strong>5. A patient lies down after standing for a long time. Explain the expected Frank–Starling response.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Standing promotes venous pooling. Lying down reduces the gravitational gradient, usually improving venous return and EDV. Extra ventricular filling stretches fibres, increasing force through <strong>length-dependent activation</strong> and generally increasing SV. This is primarily movement <strong>along</strong> a Frank–Starling curve.</p></details>\n<p><strong>6. Why is EDV not an exact measure of preload in a very stiff ventricle?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>True preload concerns end-diastolic myocardial stretch and wall stress; EDV is only a surrogate. A stiff ventricle may develop high filling pressure with relatively little increase in volume or fibre stretch. Therefore identical EDVs or pressures need not imply identical myocardial preload across different ventricular properties.</p></details>\n<p><strong>7. On a stroke-volume-versus-EDV graph, what distinguishes moving along one curve from shifting to a higher curve?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Along one curve:</strong> EDV changes, and the Frank–Starling length-dependent mechanism changes SV at the same functional state. <strong>Higher curve:</strong> at the same EDV, the ventricle ejects more blood, often because intrinsic contractility increased; reduced afterload can also improve performance, so loading conditions must be considered.</p></details>\n<p><strong>8. An acute increase in aortic pressure occurs without an immediate change in preload or contractility. Predict the first-beat effect on SV.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>SV usually decreases.</strong> Higher aortic pressure increases the load opposing ventricular ejection. With initially unchanged preload and inotropy, the ventricle ejects less blood, leaving more residual ESV. Later compensatory changes can alter the result, but they are not part of the immediate first-beat prediction.</p></details>\n<p><strong>9. Why might a large intravenous fluid load fail to appreciably raise SV in a severely failing ventricle?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>In marked systolic dysfunction the Frank–Starling curve may be relatively <strong>flat</strong> at high filling levels. Additional filling can markedly increase pressures and congestion while producing only a small increase in force and SV. More preload therefore does not guarantee proportionately greater output.</p></details>\n<p><strong>10. How can β₁ stimulation increase contraction strength and accelerate myocardial relaxation?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>β₁ receptors activate Gs → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → PKA. Greater L-type Ca²⁺ influx and SR Ca²⁺ release strengthen contraction (<strong>positive inotropy</strong>), while enhanced SERCA-mediated Ca²⁺ reuptake accelerates relaxation (<strong>positive lusitropy</strong>). The two effects arise from different parts of the same signalling pathway.</p></details>\n<p><strong>11. Why does dynamic exercise usually increase venous return rather than reduce it?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Contracting skeletal muscles compress veins, and one-way venous valves favour flow towards the heart. Respiratory pressure changes also support return. Combined with sympathetic venoconstriction, these mechanisms tend to move more blood back to the heart, increasing preload and helping sustain the higher CO required during exercise.</p></details>\n<p><strong>12. A patient loses a large volume of blood, becomes tachycardic, yet develops poor tissue perfusion. Explain using CO, preload and compensation.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Haemorrhage reduces blood volume and venous return, causing <strong>lower preload and EDV</strong>. The reduction in SV can markedly reduce CO. Reflex sympathetic stimulation increases HR and vascular resistance, which may support arterial pressure temporarily; however, CO = HR × SV means a sufficiently large SV fall can overwhelm tachycardic compensation, leaving tissue perfusion inadequate.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Cardiac output:</strong> CO = HR × SV, measured in L/min; right and left outputs are approximately equal over time.</li><li><strong>Cardiac index:</strong> CI = CO/BSA, measured in L/min/m².</li><li><strong>Stroke volume:</strong> SV = EDV − ESV; altered HR alone does not predict CO.</li><li><strong>Preload:</strong> End-diastolic myocardial fibre stretch; EDV and filling pressure are imperfect surrogates.</li><li><strong>Afterload:</strong> Mechanical load opposing ejection; acute increase generally lowers SV at unchanged preload/inotropy.</li><li><strong>Contractility:</strong> Ability to develop force at comparable loading; β₁ stimulation increases inotropy through cAMP/PKA/Ca²⁺.</li><li><strong>Frank–Starling law:</strong> Within physiological limits, increased filling and fibre length increase force and SV.</li><li><strong>Curve interpretation:</strong> Changing preload moves <em>along</em> a curve; changing contractile state shifts performance between curves.</li><li><strong>Heterometric regulation:</strong> Change in fibre starting length; <strong>homometric regulation:</strong> change in force without change in starting length.</li><li><strong>Venous return and EDV:</strong> Supported by blood volume, venous tone, muscle pump and respiratory pump; restricted by pooling, tamponade and stiff ventricles.</li><li><strong>Vagal influence:</strong> Dominant direct effects on HR and AV conduction; direct ventricular negative inotropy is small.</li><li><strong>Severe dysfunction:</strong> A flat Frank–Starling curve means more filling may raise congestion more than SV.</li><li><strong>Exercise and haemorrhage:</strong> CO must be reasoned from changes in both HR and SV, not heart rate alone.</li></ol>","plain":"Cardiac Output and the Frank–Starling Law Primary lecture: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 35–51. Supplementary clarification: intrinsic sarcomere mechanisms, precise definitions of loading, interpretation of cardiac-function curves, and clinical examples. 1. What is cardiac output, and why does it matter? Cardiac output (CO) is the volume of blood ejected by one ventricle per unit time , usually expressed in litres per minute (L/min) . It tells us how much blood the heart delivers to a circulation—not how much blood is ejected in a single beat. Two quantities determine cardiac output: Heart rate (HR): number of beats per minute. Stroke volume (SV): blood ejected from one ventricle per beat . Cardiac output CO = HR × SV Example: 75 beats/min × 70 mL/beat = 5,250 mL/min = 5.25 L/min Why multiply? Each beat sends out one stroke volume. If the ventricle pumps 70 mL each time and beats 75 times in one minute, the total volume ejected in that minute is 75 × 70 mL. The lecturer illustrates a resting output around 5.5 L/min . This is a representative value, not a universal constant: cardiac output varies with body size, activity and physiological conditions. Why should right and left cardiac outputs be approximately equal? The pulmonary and systemic circulations are connected in series . Blood ejected by the right ventricle enters the lungs and ultimately returns to the left ventricle. If one side consistently pumped a greater volume per minute than the other, blood would progressively accumulate in one circulation. The two outputs therefore remain approximately equal over time , although small moment-to-moment differences can occur. Cardiac index: allowing for body size A person with a larger body generally needs more total blood flow than a smaller person. Cardiac index (CI) expresses cardiac output relative to body surface area (BSA) . Cardiac index CI = CO BSA For CO = 5.25 L/min and BSA = 1.75 m²: CI = 3.0 L/min/m² . Unlike CO, cardiac index is expressed as L min⁻¹ m⁻² . It is a size-adjusted measure, not a different kind of blood flow. 2. Why can changing heart rate alter cardiac output? From CO = HR × SV , increasing either HR or SV can raise output if the other factor remains unchanged . In a living heart, however, they are connected. Sympathetic stimulation generally increases SA nodal firing (positive chronotropy ) and makes ventricular contraction stronger (positive inotropy ). These effects can raise both HR and SV. But a faster heartbeat does not always mean more blood pumped per minute . At very high rates, diastole becomes so short that ventricular filling decreases. Reduced filling can lower SV enough to offset the rise in HR. Example: At 75 beats/min and 70 mL/beat, CO is 5.25 L/min . At 130 beats/min but only 35 mL/beat, CO is 4.55 L/min . Even though the heart beats faster, it pumps less blood per minute. HR ↑ More beats per minute SV ↓ markedly Less blood each beat CO may ↓ Depends on their product Reasoning rule: Never predict cardiac output from heart rate alone unless you know what happens to stroke volume. 3. What determines stroke volume? From Chapter 04, SV = EDV − ESV : the amount ejected is the amount present at the end of filling minus the amount remaining after contraction. Three closely related factors determine how much is ejected: Factor The question it answers Typical effect on SV, if other conditions are fixed Preload How stretched is the ventricle before contraction? More preload → usually higher SV within physiological limits Afterload How difficult is it to eject blood? More afterload → usually lower SV acutely Contractility (inotropy) How strongly can the myocardium contract at the same loading? Greater contractility → higher SV Preload — the starting stretch Preload refers to ventricular myocardial fibre stretch (and associated passive wall stress) at the end of diastole , just before contraction. More blood returning to a compliant ventricle usually increases end-diastolic volume (EDV) and stretches its fibres. EDV is therefore a useful teaching representation of preload. End-diastolic pressure is another clinical surrogate, but volume and pressure are not interchangeable , particularly in a stiff ventricle. Analogy: Gently stretching an elastic band before releasing it can help it recoil more forcefully. It is only an analogy: cardiac muscle is active tissue, and excessive stretch does not produce unlimited pumping. Afterload — the resistance or load against ejection Afterload is the mechanical load the ventricle must overcome during ejection. On the left side it is related to aortic pressure, arterial properties and ventricular wall stress . Peripheral vascular resistance contributes to the load but is not identical to afterload. If aortic pressure rises suddenly while preload and contractility remain unchanged, the ventricle must generate more force before and during ejection; it will generally eject less blood in that beat , leaving a higher ESV. Contractility — how capable the muscle is at a given load Contractility is the intrinsic ability of the myocardium to develop force when preload and afterload are held comparable . It depends strongly on intracellular Ca²⁺ cycling and the contractile machinery. Important distinction: A ventricle that contracts more forcefully because it filled more is using the Frank–Starling mechanism; that does not automatically mean its intrinsic contractility increased . 4. Frank–Starling law: why does more filling produce more ejection? Imagine more blood returning through the veins. The extra blood enters a ventricle during diastole and stretches its muscle fibres before the next beat. Within the normal operating range, increased initial sarcomere length improves force development during the next contraction. This reflects length-dependent activation , including improved myofilament sensitivity to Ca²⁺ and more favourable interaction between contractile proteins. Venous return ↑ → EDV and fibre stretch ↑ → Force generated ↑ → SV ↑ Thus, the Frank–Starling law states that within physiological limits, the heart tends to eject more blood when its fibres are stretched more during filling . Why is this useful? It helps match the amount of blood ejected to the amount returning to the heart. If venous return increases, the ventricle can automatically increase SV without needing a new sympathetic command for every extra millilitre received. Does greater preload always increase stroke volume? No. The relationship is not unlimited. At high filling levels the curve becomes flatter, so additional volume yields progressively less extra output. A severely failing ventricle may be particularly unresponsive to extra filling while pressure rises enough to cause congestion. Lecture slide 39 includes a dashed descending limb at extreme stretch. That portion is a conceptual teaching extension: ordinary intact human hearts do not normally operate on a descending Frank–Starling limb . 5. How do we read Frank–Starling curves? The lecturer's slides 39–41 show ventricular performance plotted against filling. The usual horizontal axis is EDV or a surrogate of preload; the vertical axis may be stroke volume or cardiac output , depending on the particular diagram. Figure 01. Frank–Starling curves — preload versus pumping performance End-diastolic volume (preload) → Stroke volume Greater contractility Reference Lower contractility Read across one curve: changing EDV changes stroke volume by the Frank–Starling mechanism. Compare curves vertically: at the same EDV, greater intrinsic contractility gives greater SV (with afterload held constant). Original DOMiNANCE schematic; not patient-specific measurements. A. Moving along the same curve Suppose a person lies down after standing. Venous pooling in the legs decreases, so more blood returns to the heart, EDV increases and the ventricle moves rightwards and upwards along a curve . This is primarily a change in preload , without requiring the intrinsic contractility of the myocardium to change. B. Shifting between curves Now imagine two beats with the same EDV , but sympathetic stimulation has increased intracellular Ca²⁺ availability during the second beat. The second beat can eject a greater SV without additional stretch . This is a change in contractile state , represented by an upward function-curve shift . A fall in contractility, as in significant myocardial dysfunction, tends to produce a lower curve . Higher afterload can also lower ventricular performance at a given EDV, so a shifted curve is not proof of altered contractility unless afterload is accounted for . The lecturer's unusual terms: heterometric and homometric Term Meaning Typical example Heterometric regulation Adjusting output through changes in the initial fibre length (preload) Frank–Starling response to increased venous return Homometric regulation Adjusting force independently of the initial fibre length β₁-mediated increase in contractility at the same EDV Exam insight: Heterometric changes the starting length; homometric changes pumping performance without needing a length change . 6. What raises or lowers end-diastolic volume? EDV depends on how much blood returns, the time available to fill, and whether the ventricle can accommodate that blood. More venous return does not always translate into the same EDV change if ventricular compliance is impaired. Often increases EDV Why Increased blood volume More blood available to return to the heart Increased venous tone Shifts blood from compliant venous reservoirs towards the heart Skeletal-muscle pump Contracting muscles compress veins and promote flow through one-way valves Respiratory pump Breathing-related pressure changes can favour venous return Effective atrial contraction Completes late ventricular filling Longer diastolic filling time More time is available for blood to enter Often reduces EDV or limits effective filling Why Haemorrhage / hypovolaemia Less circulating blood and lower venous return Prolonged standing Gravity promotes venous pooling in the legs Pericardial effusion / tamponade External pericardial pressure limits ventricular expansion Reduced ventricular compliance A stiff ventricle fills less readily for a given pressure Severe tachycardia Shortened diastole limits filling time The lecturer also mentions myocardial infarction and infiltrative disease , including endomyocardial fibrosis (EMF). These can impair relaxation, compliance and/or pumping; their effect on EDV is not universally a simple decrease . The precise direction depends on the disease and stage. 7. What changes contractility? Sympathetic cardiac nerves release noradrenaline (norepinephrine) , while the adrenal medulla releases circulating adrenaline (epinephrine) and some noradrenaline. These catecholamines stimulate β₁-adrenergic receptors in the heart. β₁ receptor → Gs / adenylyl cyclase → cAMP / PKA ↑ → Ca²⁺ cycling ↑ → Stronger contraction PKA signalling increases L-type Ca²⁺ entry and enhances Ca²⁺ release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, allowing greater actin–myosin interaction. This produces positive inotropy . The same signalling can speed Ca²⁺ reuptake, producing positive lusitropy (faster relaxation). The lecture cites caffeine and theophylline (xanthines). By inhibiting phosphodiesterase activity at sufficiently high concentrations, such agents can reduce cAMP breakdown, but their physiological and clinical effects are dose- and context-dependent. Do not assume everyday caffeine intake produces a predictable large positive inotropic response. What about the vagus nerve? The lecturer lists vagal stimulation as negative inotropic. Clarification: vagal effects are strong for heart rate and AV nodal conduction, but the direct parasympathetic effect on ventricular contractility is relatively small . Any fall in cardiac output may occur largely through changes in HR and filling rather than a large direct weakening of ventricular myocardium. What can depress inotropy? Lecture slide 48 lists hypercapnia, hypoxia, acidosis, selected drugs and cardiac failure . Severe myocardial hypoxia and acidosis can interfere with Ca²⁺ handling and cellular energy availability, reducing force generation; myocardial injury or failure may also reduce contractility. Responses to individual drugs depend on their specific mechanism. 8. How do preload, afterload and inotropy differ in real situations? More venous return Preload ↑ Often moves along the curve → SV ↑ Higher aortic pressure Afterload ↑ Ejection becomes harder → SV may ↓ β₁ stimulation Contractility ↑ More force at same EDV → SV ↑ Why are these comparisons useful? In a patient with blood loss, HR may rise through the sympathetic reflex, but low preload can still reduce SV and CO. In a patient with aortic stenosis, even a strong contraction faces a substantial outflow load. In a failing heart, giving more filling volume may only modestly increase SV if the ventricular function curve is flat. These examples show why no single determinant should be interpreted in isolation. 9. What happens to cardiac output during exercise and other states? During dynamic exercise , several processes usually occur together: sympathetic stimulation increases HR and inotropy; the skeletal-muscle and respiratory pumps increase venous return ; and arterioles supplying active muscle dilate. CO can therefore increase substantially by changes in both HR and SV. Lecture clarification: Slide 49 contains an ambiguous downward-arrow description of venous return during exercise. For ordinary dynamic exercise, the expected effect of muscle and respiratory pumping is generally increased , not decreased, venous return. The lecturer's diagrams on slides 50–51 also show increased venous return associated with the pumps. The lecture additionally mentions conditions associated with increased CO or cardiac demands: eating, high environmental temperature, pregnancy, anxiety/excitement, catecholamines and thyrotoxicosis . These conditions differ in mechanisms and magnitude; they are not interchangeable. Clinical connection — shock: During major haemorrhage, venous return and preload decrease. Sympathetic tachycardia and vasoconstriction can help maintain arterial pressure temporarily, but this does not guarantee normal CO or adequate tissue perfusion. Understanding Check 1. A person has a heart rate of 75 beats/min and stroke volume of 70 mL. Calculate CO and state the correct units. Reveal explanation CO = HR × SV = 75 × 70 = 5250 mL/min = 5.25 L/min. Heart rate counts beats per minute, while stroke volume gives the blood ejected per beat; multiplying cancels 'beats' and leaves volume per minute. 2. Why must right and left ventricular outputs be approximately equal over time, even though they pump against different pressures? Reveal explanation The systemic and pulmonary circuits are arranged in series : what leaves the right heart eventually returns to the left heart. A persistent unequal flow would cause blood to accumulate in one circuit and deplete the other. The left ventricle produces higher pressure, but not a greater long-term flow rate. 3. Heart rate rises from 75 to 130 beats/min, but SV falls from 70 to 35 mL. What happens to CO? Reveal explanation Initially, CO = 75 × 70 = 5.25 L/min . Afterwards CO = 130 × 35 = 4.55 L/min . Thus CO decreases despite tachycardia. Severe shortening of filling time can reduce SV enough to outweigh the increase in HR. 4. Two ventricular beats have the same EDV, but the second ejects a larger SV following sympathetic stimulation. Has preload necessarily increased? Reveal explanation No. Same EDV implies comparable filling volume, while β₁ stimulation can increase Ca²⁺ entry and SR release, raising intrinsic contractility. At similar preload and afterload, this produces greater force and a larger SV, reflecting a higher ventricular function curve rather than merely movement along the original curve. 5. A patient lies down after standing for a long time. Explain the expected Frank–Starling response. Reveal explanation Standing promotes venous pooling. Lying down reduces the gravitational gradient, usually improving venous return and EDV. Extra ventricular filling stretches fibres, increasing force through length-dependent activation and generally increasing SV. This is primarily movement along a Frank–Starling curve. 6. Why is EDV not an exact measure of preload in a very stiff ventricle? Reveal explanation True preload concerns end-diastolic myocardial stretch and wall stress; EDV is only a surrogate. A stiff ventricle may develop high filling pressure with relatively little increase in volume or fibre stretch. Therefore identical EDVs or pressures need not imply identical myocardial preload across different ventricular properties. 7. On a stroke-volume-versus-EDV graph, what distinguishes moving along one curve from shifting to a higher curve? Reveal explanation Along one curve: EDV changes, and the Frank–Starling length-dependent mechanism changes SV at the same functional state. Higher curve: at the same EDV, the ventricle ejects more blood, often because intrinsic contractility increased; reduced afterload can also improve performance, so loading conditions must be considered. 8. An acute increase in aortic pressure occurs without an immediate change in preload or contractility. Predict the first-beat effect on SV. Reveal explanation SV usually decreases. Higher aortic pressure increases the load opposing ventricular ejection. With initially unchanged preload and inotropy, the ventricle ejects less blood, leaving more residual ESV. Later compensatory changes can alter the result, but they are not part of the immediate first-beat prediction. 9. Why might a large intravenous fluid load fail to appreciably raise SV in a severely failing ventricle? Reveal explanation In marked systolic dysfunction the Frank–Starling curve may be relatively flat at high filling levels. Additional filling can markedly increase pressures and congestion while producing only a small increase in force and SV. More preload therefore does not guarantee proportionately greater output. 10. How can β₁ stimulation increase contraction strength and accelerate myocardial relaxation? Reveal explanation β₁ receptors activate Gs → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → PKA. Greater L-type Ca²⁺ influx and SR Ca²⁺ release strengthen contraction ( positive inotropy ), while enhanced SERCA-mediated Ca²⁺ reuptake accelerates relaxation ( positive lusitropy ). The two effects arise from different parts of the same signalling pathway. 11. Why does dynamic exercise usually increase venous return rather than reduce it? Reveal explanation Contracting skeletal muscles compress veins, and one-way venous valves favour flow towards the heart. Respiratory pressure changes also support return. Combined with sympathetic venoconstriction, these mechanisms tend to move more blood back to the heart, increasing preload and helping sustain the higher CO required during exercise. 12. A patient loses a large volume of blood, becomes tachycardic, yet develops poor tissue perfusion. Explain using CO, preload and compensation. Reveal explanation Haemorrhage reduces blood volume and venous return, causing lower preload and EDV . The reduction in SV can markedly reduce CO. Reflex sympathetic stimulation increases HR and vascular resistance, which may support arterial pressure temporarily; however, CO = HR × SV means a sufficiently large SV fall can overwhelm tachycardic compensation, leaving tissue perfusion inadequate. Chapter Essentials Cardiac output: CO = HR × SV, measured in L/min; right and left outputs are approximately equal over time. Cardiac index: CI = CO/BSA, measured in L/min/m². Stroke volume: SV = EDV − ESV; altered HR alone does not predict CO. Preload: End-diastolic myocardial fibre stretch; EDV and filling pressure are imperfect surrogates. Afterload: Mechanical load opposing ejection; acute increase generally lowers SV at unchanged preload/inotropy. Contractility: Ability to develop force at comparable loading; β₁ stimulation increases inotropy through cAMP/PKA/Ca²⁺. Frank–Starling law: Within physiological limits, increased filling and fibre length increase force and SV. Curve interpretation: Changing preload moves along a curve; changing contractile state shifts performance between curves. Heterometric regulation: Change in fibre starting length; homometric regulation: change in force without change in starting length. Venous return and EDV: Supported by blood volume, venous tone, muscle pump and respiratory pump; restricted by pooling, tamponade and stiff ventricles. Vagal influence: Dominant direct effects on HR and AV conduction; direct ventricular negative inotropy is small. Severe dysfunction: A flat Frank–Starling curve means more filling may raise congestion more than SV. Exercise and haemorrhage: CO must be reasoned from changes in both HR and SV, not heart rate alone."},{"slug":"co-measurement","title":"Cardiac Output Measurement and Work","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"52-54","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"fick-principle","title":"Fick principle"},{"id":"indicator-dilution-and-thermodilution","title":"Indicator dilution and thermodilution"},{"id":"work-performed-by-the-heart","title":"Work performed by the heart"},{"id":"distinguish-output-measurement-from-local-blood-flow-measurement","title":"Distinguish output measurement from local blood-flow measurement"},{"id":"understand-cardiac-work-and-oxygen-demand","title":"Understand cardiac work and oxygen demand"}],"html":"<h1>Measurement of Cardiac Output and Cardiac Work</h1>\n<h2 id=\"fick-principle\">Fick principle</h2>\n<p>The amount of oxygen removed from blood each minute equals blood flow times the arterial–mixed venous oxygen content difference: <strong>CO = VO₂ ÷ (CaO₂ − CvO₂)</strong>. Use consistent units: if oxygen consumption is 250 mL/min and the content difference is 50 mL/L, CO = <strong>5 L/min</strong>.</p>\n<p>The venous sample for systemic Fick cardiac output must approximate <strong>mixed venous blood</strong>, traditionally drawn from the pulmonary artery—not merely any peripheral vein. Whole-body oxygen consumption and paired oxygen content are required.</p>\n<h2 id=\"indicator-dilution-and-thermodilution\">Indicator dilution and thermodilution</h2>\n<p>An indicator injected upstream appears downstream as a concentration–time curve. After correcting for dose and units, <strong>more rapid dilution</strong> generally reflects greater flow. <strong>Thermodilution</strong> uses a temperature change instead of dye and can estimate CO through pulmonary-artery catheter measurements. Measurements have limitations in settings such as major tricuspid regurgitation or shunts.</p>\n<p><strong>Echocardiography</strong> can estimate SV from left ventricular outflow tract cross-sectional area × velocity–time integral; then CO = SV × HR.</p>\n<h2 id=\"work-performed-by-the-heart\">Work performed by the heart</h2>\n<p>Ventricular <strong>stroke work</strong> is approximately the area of the pressure–volume loop (∮P dV). A useful approximation is ejected volume times the pressure against which it ejects, though true work depends on the changing pressure throughout the beat. Myocardial oxygen demand rises with heart rate, contractility and wall stress.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why must the Fick method know oxygen extraction as well as oxygen consumption?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>The same VO₂ could arise from high flow with little oxygen removed per litre or lower flow with more oxygen removed. Their ratio separates the two.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"distinguish-output-measurement-from-local-blood-flow-measurement\">Distinguish output measurement from local blood-flow measurement</h2>\n<p><strong>Fick's principle</strong> is a mass-balance relation: oxygen consumption per unit time equals blood flow × the difference between entering and leaving oxygen content. To estimate whole-body CO, the oxygen content difference must use <strong>systemic arterial and mixed venous</strong> samples. The pulmonary artery conveniently contains mixed systemic venous blood.</p>\n<p>For <strong>indicator dilution</strong>, known indicator quantity injected upstream is related to the integrated downstream concentration–time curve; with constant dose and proper correction, a smaller integrated concentration area corresponds to larger flow. <strong>Thermodilution</strong> substitutes a temperature-change curve, but mixing, valve regurgitation and shunts can introduce error.</p>\n<p>The lecturer also names three methods for <strong>regional blood flow</strong> (Circulation 1, slide 62): an <strong>electromagnetic flowmeter</strong> senses induced voltage when conducting blood moves in a magnetic field; <strong>Doppler ultrasound</strong> estimates velocity from reflected frequency shift; and <strong>plethysmography</strong> detects volume changes in a tissue or limb as an indirect index of blood flow. They are not interchangeable with measuring whole-body cardiac output.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understand-cardiac-work-and-oxygen-demand\">Understand cardiac work and oxygen demand</h2>\n<p>Approximate external <strong>stroke work ≈ mean ejection pressure × stroke volume</strong>, whereas actual LV pressure–volume loop area integrates the varying pressure through contraction/ejection. Raising <strong>afterload</strong> tends to increase pressure work and wall stress; pressure-overload lesions such as aortic stenosis can strongly increase myocardial oxygen demand. Coronary venous blood already has low oxygen content because myocardium extracts a large fraction at rest, so increased oxygen demand is met substantially by <strong>increased coronary flow</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> A patient has the same SV but a much higher aortic pressure. Pressure work and myocardial oxygen requirements generally rise, even though the amount of blood ejected per beat is unchanged.</p>\n","plain":" Measurement of Cardiac Output and Cardiac Work \n Fick principle \n The amount of oxygen removed from blood each minute equals blood flow times the arterial–mixed venous oxygen content difference:  CO = VO₂ ÷ (CaO₂ − CvO₂) . Use consistent units: if oxygen consumption is 250 mL/min and the content difference is 50 mL/L, CO =  5 L/min . \n The venous sample for systemic Fick cardiac output must approximate  mixed venous blood , traditionally drawn from the pulmonary artery—not merely any peripheral vein. Whole-body oxygen consumption and paired oxygen content are required. \n Indicator dilution and thermodilution \n An indicator injected upstream appears downstream as a concentration–time curve. After correcting for dose and units,  more rapid dilution  generally reflects greater flow.  Thermodilution  uses a temperature change instead of dye and can estimate CO through pulmonary-artery catheter measurements. Measurements have limitations in settings such as major tricuspid regurgitation or shunts. \n  Echocardiography  can estimate SV from left ventricular outflow tract cross-sectional area × velocity–time integral; then CO = SV × HR. \n Work performed by the heart \n Ventricular  stroke work  is approximately the area of the pressure–volume loop (∮P dV). A useful approximation is ejected volume times the pressure against which it ejects, though true work depends on the changing pressure throughout the beat. Myocardial oxygen demand rises with heart rate, contractility and wall stress. \n  Check:  Why must the Fick method know oxygen extraction as well as oxygen consumption? \n  Answer The same VO₂ could arise from high flow with little oxygen removed per litre or lower flow with more oxygen removed. Their ratio separates the two. \n\n Distinguish output measurement from local blood-flow measurement \n  Fick's principle  is a mass-balance relation: oxygen consumption per unit time equals blood flow × the difference between entering and leaving oxygen content. To estimate whole-body CO, the oxygen content difference must use  systemic arterial and mixed venous  samples. The pulmonary artery conveniently contains mixed systemic venous blood. \n For  indicator dilution , known indicator quantity injected upstream is related to the integrated downstream concentration–time curve; with constant dose and proper correction, a smaller integrated concentration area corresponds to larger flow.  Thermodilution  substitutes a temperature-change curve, but mixing, valve regurgitation and shunts can introduce error. \n The lecturer also names three methods for  regional blood flow  (Circulation 1, slide 62): an  electromagnetic flowmeter  senses induced voltage when conducting blood moves in a magnetic field;  Doppler ultrasound  estimates velocity from reflected frequency shift; and  plethysmography  detects volume changes in a tissue or limb as an indirect index of blood flow. They are not interchangeable with measuring whole-body cardiac output. \n Understand cardiac work and oxygen demand \n Approximate external  stroke work ≈ mean ejection pressure × stroke volume , whereas actual LV pressure–volume loop area integrates the varying pressure through contraction/ejection. Raising  afterload  tends to increase pressure work and wall stress; pressure-overload lesions such as aortic stenosis can strongly increase myocardial oxygen demand. Coronary venous blood already has low oxygen content because myocardium extracts a large fraction at rest, so increased oxygen demand is met substantially by  increased coronary flow . \n  Practice:  A patient has the same SV but a much higher aortic pressure. Pressure work and myocardial oxygen requirements generally rise, even though the amount of blood ejected per beat is unchanged. \n"},{"slug":"hemodynamics","title":"Blood Flow and Hemodynamics","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"55-74","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"flow-velocity-and-pressure-are-not-synonyms","title":"Flow, velocity and pressure are not synonyms"},{"id":"why-radius-dominates-resistance","title":"Why radius dominates resistance"},{"id":"laminar-versus-turbulent-flow","title":"Laminar versus turbulent flow"},{"id":"compliance-and-blood-storage","title":"Compliance and blood storage"},{"id":"distinguish-velocity-from-volumetric-flow-in-one-vascular-tree","title":"Distinguish velocity from volumetric flow in one vascular tree"},{"id":"where-turbulent-sounds-come-from","title":"Where turbulent sounds come from"},{"id":"blood-is-not-a-simple-fluid","title":"Blood is not a simple fluid"}],"html":"<h1>Blood Flow and Hemodynamics</h1>\n<h2 id=\"flow-velocity-and-pressure-are-not-synonyms\">Flow, velocity and pressure are not synonyms</h2>\n<p><strong>Volume flow (Q)</strong> is volume per time (mL/s); <strong>mean velocity (v)</strong> is distance per time (cm/s). They relate through <strong>Q = v × cross-sectional area</strong>. Since total cross-sectional area is greatest in systemic capillaries, average blood velocity there is low even though the entire capillary network carries the cardiac output.</p>\n<p>For a vascular segment <strong>Q = ΔP/R</strong>. Pressure falls along the circulation as energy is dissipated against resistance; arterioles account for a major pressure drop and are key resistance vessels.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-radius-dominates-resistance\">Why radius dominates resistance</h2>\n<p>For laminar flow of a Newtonian fluid in a rigid cylindrical tube, <strong>Poiseuille's law</strong> gives <strong>R = 8ηL/(πr⁴)</strong>, where η = viscosity, L = length and r = radius. Halving radius increases resistance about <strong>16-fold</strong> under these idealised assumptions. Real blood vessels are compliant and blood is non-Newtonian, so the equation illustrates the strong effect of arteriolar calibre rather than predicting every biological flow precisely.</p>\n<h2 id=\"laminar-versus-turbulent-flow\">Laminar versus turbulent flow</h2>\n<p>Laminar flow has organised layers; axial flow is generally fastest near the centre. Turbulence becomes more likely with increased velocity, increased diameter or decreased viscosity and can generate murmurs or bruits. The <strong>Reynolds number</strong> compares inertial and viscous effects; it is a guide, not a universal cutoff in pulsatile vascular networks.</p>\n<h2 id=\"compliance-and-blood-storage\">Compliance and blood storage</h2>\n<p><strong>Compliance = change in volume/change in pressure</strong>. Veins are highly compliant <strong>capacitance vessels</strong> and hold much of the circulating blood at rest. Large elastic arteries store energy during systole and release it during diastole, smoothing flow (Windkessel effect).</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Two arterioles have the same length and viscosity. One doubles its radius. How does idealised resistance change?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Resistance becomes 1/16 of its original value because R is inversely proportional to r⁴. In living vessels, autoregulation and other factors also act.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"distinguish-velocity-from-volumetric-flow-in-one-vascular-tree\">Distinguish velocity from volumetric flow in one vascular tree</h2>\n<p>In the ideal series circulation, the <strong>same mean volume of blood per minute</strong> passes the aorta and combined systemic capillaries. The capillary network has an enormous <strong>total cross-sectional area</strong>, so its mean velocity is low despite unchanged total Q. Vessel branching does not automatically mean a smaller <em>total</em> flow. A pulsatile elastic aorta stores part of systolic stroke volume and recoils in diastole (<strong>Windkessel effect</strong>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"where-turbulent-sounds-come-from\">Where turbulent sounds come from</h2>\n<p>For idealised tube flow, <strong>Re = ρvD/η</strong>, where ρ is fluid density, v mean velocity, D tube diameter, and η viscosity. A narrowing can make the local velocity rise enough to favour turbulence, and a fall in viscosity in anaemia can also favour turbulence. This is why turbulent <strong>Korotkoff sounds</strong> appear as an external cuff partly compresses a brachial artery. For a particular total flow, the local constricted segment can have high velocity even though its diameter has decreased.</p>\n<p><strong>Hagen–Poiseuille:</strong> <strong>Q = (πr⁴ ΔP)/(8ηL)</strong>, so <strong>R = 8ηL/(πr⁴)</strong> under laminar, rigid, cylindrical assumptions. If radius falls from 2 mm to 1 mm and ΔP is held constant, theoretical flow becomes <strong>one-sixteenth</strong>. Real vascular beds adapt through active tone and parallel branching.</p>\n<h2 id=\"blood-is-not-a-simple-fluid\">Blood is not a simple fluid</h2>\n<p>Apparent blood viscosity depends on <strong>haematocrit</strong>, plasma proteins, red-cell deformability, temperature and vessel size/flow conditions. <strong>Polycythaemia</strong> increases viscosity and may raise circulatory workload; anaemia lowers viscosity but reduces oxygen delivery, often eliciting a higher cardiac output. In small vessels, red-cell axial migration and the Fåhræus–Lindqvist phenomenon modify apparent viscosity. The lecturer includes artery–vein compliance contrasts (slide 74): a much larger increase in <strong>venous volume</strong> can occur with only a small pressure rise, whereas arteries are relatively stiff.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why are capillaries the slow-flow region although each capillary is narrow? Because their <strong>combined cross-sectional area</strong> is much larger than the aortic area.</p>\n","plain":" Blood Flow and Hemodynamics \n Flow, velocity and pressure are not synonyms \n  Volume flow (Q)  is volume per time (mL/s);  mean velocity (v)  is distance per time (cm/s). They relate through  Q = v × cross-sectional area . Since total cross-sectional area is greatest in systemic capillaries, average blood velocity there is low even though the entire capillary network carries the cardiac output. \n For a vascular segment  Q = ΔP/R . Pressure falls along the circulation as energy is dissipated against resistance; arterioles account for a major pressure drop and are key resistance vessels. \n Why radius dominates resistance \n For laminar flow of a Newtonian fluid in a rigid cylindrical tube,  Poiseuille's law  gives  R = 8ηL/(πr⁴) , where η = viscosity, L = length and r = radius. Halving radius increases resistance about  16-fold  under these idealised assumptions. Real blood vessels are compliant and blood is non-Newtonian, so the equation illustrates the strong effect of arteriolar calibre rather than predicting every biological flow precisely. \n Laminar versus turbulent flow \n Laminar flow has organised layers; axial flow is generally fastest near the centre. Turbulence becomes more likely with increased velocity, increased diameter or decreased viscosity and can generate murmurs or bruits. The  Reynolds number  compares inertial and viscous effects; it is a guide, not a universal cutoff in pulsatile vascular networks. \n Compliance and blood storage \n  Compliance = change in volume/change in pressure . Veins are highly compliant  capacitance vessels  and hold much of the circulating blood at rest. Large elastic arteries store energy during systole and release it during diastole, smoothing flow (Windkessel effect). \n  Check:  Two arterioles have the same length and viscosity. One doubles its radius. How does idealised resistance change? \n  Answer Resistance becomes 1/16 of its original value because R is inversely proportional to r⁴. In living vessels, autoregulation and other factors also act. \n\n Distinguish velocity from volumetric flow in one vascular tree \n In the ideal series circulation, the  same mean volume of blood per minute  passes the aorta and combined systemic capillaries. The capillary network has an enormous  total cross-sectional area , so its mean velocity is low despite unchanged total Q. Vessel branching does not automatically mean a smaller  total  flow. A pulsatile elastic aorta stores part of systolic stroke volume and recoils in diastole ( Windkessel effect ). \n Where turbulent sounds come from \n For idealised tube flow,  Re = ρvD/η , where ρ is fluid density, v mean velocity, D tube diameter, and η viscosity. A narrowing can make the local velocity rise enough to favour turbulence, and a fall in viscosity in anaemia can also favour turbulence. This is why turbulent  Korotkoff sounds  appear as an external cuff partly compresses a brachial artery. For a particular total flow, the local constricted segment can have high velocity even though its diameter has decreased. \n  Hagen–Poiseuille:   Q = (πr⁴ ΔP)/(8ηL) , so  R = 8ηL/(πr⁴)  under laminar, rigid, cylindrical assumptions. If radius falls from 2 mm to 1 mm and ΔP is held constant, theoretical flow becomes  one-sixteenth . Real vascular beds adapt through active tone and parallel branching. \n Blood is not a simple fluid \n Apparent blood viscosity depends on  haematocrit , plasma proteins, red-cell deformability, temperature and vessel size/flow conditions.  Polycythaemia  increases viscosity and may raise circulatory workload; anaemia lowers viscosity but reduces oxygen delivery, often eliciting a higher cardiac output. In small vessels, red-cell axial migration and the Fåhræus–Lindqvist phenomenon modify apparent viscosity. The lecturer includes artery–vein compliance contrasts (slide 74): a much larger increase in  venous volume  can occur with only a small pressure rise, whereas arteries are relatively stiff. \n  Practice:  Why are capillaries the slow-flow region although each capillary is narrow? Because their  combined cross-sectional area  is much larger than the aortic area. \n"},{"slug":"blood-pressure","title":"Arterial Blood Pressure","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"76-83","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"what-the-numbers-mean","title":"What the numbers mean"},{"id":"where-pressure-comes-from","title":"Where pressure comes from"},{"id":"measurement-principles","title":"Measurement principles"},{"id":"clinical-reasoning","title":"Clinical reasoning"},{"id":"correct-cuff-technique-and-gravity-effects","title":"Correct cuff technique and gravity effects"},{"id":"interpret-arterial-pressure-through-the-circulation","title":"Interpret arterial pressure through the circulation"}],"html":"<h1>Arterial Blood Pressure</h1>\n<h2 id=\"what-the-numbers-mean\">What the numbers mean</h2>\n<p><strong>Systolic blood pressure (SBP)</strong> is peak arterial pressure during ventricular ejection. <strong>Diastolic blood pressure (DBP)</strong> is the lowest pressure before the next ejection. <strong>Pulse pressure (PP) = SBP − DBP</strong>; for 120/80 mmHg, PP = 40 mmHg.</p>\n<p>Mean arterial pressure (<strong>MAP</strong>) is the cycle-average arterial pressure. At ordinary heart rates <strong>MAP ≈ DBP + ⅓(PP)</strong>, so for 120/80 it is about 93 mmHg. This approximation becomes less accurate with major changes in heart rate or waveform shape.</p>\n<h2 id=\"where-pressure-comes-from\">Where pressure comes from</h2>\n<p>In the systemic circuit <strong>MAP − right atrial pressure ≈ CO × systemic vascular resistance</strong>. Thus BP depends not just on HR, but on SV/CO, vascular resistance, arterial compliance and blood volume. Aortic elasticity maintains pressure between ventricular beats.</p>\n<h2 id=\"measurement-principles\">Measurement principles</h2>\n<p>A cuff is inflated above systolic pressure and then slowly deflated. The first characteristic <strong>Korotkoff sound</strong> is used for systolic pressure and disappearance of sounds (phase V) usually indicates diastolic pressure in adults. Use an appropriately sized cuff, a supported arm at heart level, and proper rest; wrong cuff size and arm position bias the reading.</p>\n<h2 id=\"clinical-reasoning\">Clinical reasoning</h2>\n<p>An increased stroke volume or stiffer large arteries may widen pulse pressure. Strong arteriolar constriction raises resistance, potentially raising MAP even if cardiac output falls.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> If HR rises but SV falls more sharply, what happens to CO and must BP rise?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>CO may fall, and BP need not rise. Systemic arterial pressure depends on both CO and systemic vascular resistance.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"correct-cuff-technique-and-gravity-effects\">Correct cuff technique and gravity effects</h2>\n<p>The reference BP is reported for the cuff <strong>near heart level</strong>. Hydrostatic pressure changes by roughly <strong>0.77 mmHg per vertical centimetre</strong> in a static blood column; keeping the arm much lower than the heart can give a falsely elevated reading. A cuff too <strong>small</strong> tends to overestimate BP, while a cuff too <strong>large</strong> can underestimate it. The patient should rest, avoid talking, support the arm and use the appropriate cuff size.</p>\n<p><strong>Palpation</strong> can estimate systolic pressure from reappearance of the distal pulse while deflating the cuff, but cannot directly establish diastolic pressure. With <strong>auscultation</strong>, the first clear Korotkoff sound estimates systolic, and disappearance (usually phase V in adults) estimates diastolic. Sounds arise when cuff pressure partly compresses the artery and causes disturbed/intermittent flow.</p>\n<h2 id=\"interpret-arterial-pressure-through-the-circulation\">Interpret arterial pressure through the circulation</h2>\n<p>Aortic pressure oscillates because the heart ejects periodically, but <strong>elastic arterial recoil</strong> maintains flow during diastole. MAP falls substantially across resistance vessels, particularly arterioles; the pressure profile in the lecturer's diagram (slide 83) illustrates why capillaries are protected from high arterial pressure. <strong>Pulse pressure</strong> depends strongly on stroke volume and arterial compliance. <strong>MAP</strong> is more strongly related to CO × peripheral resistance (with central venous pressure taken into account).</p>\n<p>If a patient loses blood, the <strong>baroreflex</strong> can temporarily raise resistance enough to maintain MAP even with falling SV. This explains why a near-normal BP cannot exclude compensated shock.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why could two people have the same MAP but different pulse pressures? Their arterial compliance and SV/wave reflection may differ even when the time-averaged pressure is similar.</p>\n","plain":" Arterial Blood Pressure \n What the numbers mean \n  Systolic blood pressure (SBP)  is peak arterial pressure during ventricular ejection.  Diastolic blood pressure (DBP)  is the lowest pressure before the next ejection.  Pulse pressure (PP) = SBP − DBP ; for 120/80 mmHg, PP = 40 mmHg. \n Mean arterial pressure ( MAP ) is the cycle-average arterial pressure. At ordinary heart rates  MAP ≈ DBP + ⅓(PP) , so for 120/80 it is about 93 mmHg. This approximation becomes less accurate with major changes in heart rate or waveform shape. \n Where pressure comes from \n In the systemic circuit  MAP − right atrial pressure ≈ CO × systemic vascular resistance . Thus BP depends not just on HR, but on SV/CO, vascular resistance, arterial compliance and blood volume. Aortic elasticity maintains pressure between ventricular beats. \n Measurement principles \n A cuff is inflated above systolic pressure and then slowly deflated. The first characteristic  Korotkoff sound  is used for systolic pressure and disappearance of sounds (phase V) usually indicates diastolic pressure in adults. Use an appropriately sized cuff, a supported arm at heart level, and proper rest; wrong cuff size and arm position bias the reading. \n Clinical reasoning \n An increased stroke volume or stiffer large arteries may widen pulse pressure. Strong arteriolar constriction raises resistance, potentially raising MAP even if cardiac output falls. \n  Check:  If HR rises but SV falls more sharply, what happens to CO and must BP rise? \n  Answer CO may fall, and BP need not rise. Systemic arterial pressure depends on both CO and systemic vascular resistance. \n\n Correct cuff technique and gravity effects \n The reference BP is reported for the cuff  near heart level . Hydrostatic pressure changes by roughly  0.77 mmHg per vertical centimetre  in a static blood column; keeping the arm much lower than the heart can give a falsely elevated reading. A cuff too  small  tends to overestimate BP, while a cuff too  large  can underestimate it. The patient should rest, avoid talking, support the arm and use the appropriate cuff size. \n  Palpation  can estimate systolic pressure from reappearance of the distal pulse while deflating the cuff, but cannot directly establish diastolic pressure. With  auscultation , the first clear Korotkoff sound estimates systolic, and disappearance (usually phase V in adults) estimates diastolic. Sounds arise when cuff pressure partly compresses the artery and causes disturbed/intermittent flow. \n Interpret arterial pressure through the circulation \n Aortic pressure oscillates because the heart ejects periodically, but  elastic arterial recoil  maintains flow during diastole. MAP falls substantially across resistance vessels, particularly arterioles; the pressure profile in the lecturer's diagram (slide 83) illustrates why capillaries are protected from high arterial pressure.  Pulse pressure  depends strongly on stroke volume and arterial compliance.  MAP  is more strongly related to CO × peripheral resistance (with central venous pressure taken into account). \n If a patient loses blood, the  baroreflex  can temporarily raise resistance enough to maintain MAP even with falling SV. This explains why a near-normal BP cannot exclude compensated shock. \n  Practice:  Why could two people have the same MAP but different pulse pressures? Their arterial compliance and SV/wave reflection may differ even when the time-averaged pressure is similar. \n"},{"slug":"pulses","title":"Arterial Pulse and Jugular Venous Pulse","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"22-26","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"arterial-pulse","title":"Arterial pulse"},{"id":"jugular-venous-pulse-jvp","title":"Jugular venous pulse (JVP)"},{"id":"distinguish-the-arterial-pulse-from-actual-flow","title":"Distinguish the arterial pulse from actual flow"},{"id":"place-jvp-waves-in-the-cardiac-cycle","title":"Place JVP waves in the cardiac cycle"}],"html":"<h1>Arterial Pulse and Jugular Venous Pulse</h1>\n<h2 id=\"arterial-pulse\">Arterial pulse</h2>\n<p>The palpable arterial pulse is a <strong>pressure/distension wave</strong>, not a bolus of blood travelling at the same speed down the artery. Its propagation speed generally rises with arterial stiffness. Assess <strong>rate, rhythm, volume, contour/character, and symmetry</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pulse pressure tends to increase when stroke volume increases or arterial compliance decreases; the relationship is modified by wave reflection and peripheral resistance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"jugular-venous-pulse-jvp\">Jugular venous pulse (JVP)</h2>\n<p>The internal jugular venous pulsation transmits changes in <strong>right atrial pressure</strong>. Unlike a carotid arterial pulse, it is normally <strong>non-palpable</strong>, varies with posture/respiration and has more than one wave per cycle. JVP is clinically estimated from its vertical height above the sternal angle with appropriate patient positioning.</p>\n<p>The major JVP features are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>a wave:</strong> right atrial contraction.</li>\n<li><strong>c wave:</strong> early ventricular systole, including tricuspid bulging/adjacent arterial transmission.</li>\n<li><strong>x descent:</strong> atrial relaxation and downward tricuspid motion.</li>\n<li><strong>v wave:</strong> right atrial filling against a closed tricuspid valve.</li>\n<li><strong>y descent:</strong> opening of the tricuspid valve and rapid ventricular filling.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Large <strong>v waves</strong> may occur with tricuspid regurgitation. <strong>Cannon a waves</strong> can occur when atria contract against a closed tricuspid valve, including AV dissociation. These are not interchangeable.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why can the neck show a venous pressure wave even though venous blood flows toward the heart?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Right atrial pressure fluctuations propagate backward through the compliant venous column; the pulsation is a pressure signal, not necessarily retrograde bulk flow.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"distinguish-the-arterial-pulse-from-actual-flow\">Distinguish the arterial pulse from actual flow</h2>\n<p>Each systolic ejection creates a <strong>pressure/distension wave</strong> that travels much faster than the blood's average forward velocity. Wave transmission increases as arteries stiffen with age; pulse quality may be assessed through rate, regularity, amplitude, contour, symmetry and location. The origin is LV ejection, but the palpable pulse is <strong>not an individual packet of blood</strong> moving from the aorta to the wrist.</p>\n<h2 id=\"place-jvp-waves-in-the-cardiac-cycle\">Place JVP waves in the cardiac cycle</h2>\n<p>The <strong>a wave</strong> follows right atrial contraction; the <strong>c wave</strong> occurs around early ventricular systole; <strong>x descent</strong> includes atrial relaxation and downward tricuspid displacement; the <strong>v wave</strong> reflects filling of the right atrium while the tricuspid valve remains closed; and the <strong>y descent</strong> accompanies tricuspid opening and right ventricular filling. The three positive waves are <strong>a, c and v</strong>; the descents are <strong>x and y</strong>.</p>\n<p>Different abnormalities enlarge different waves: <strong>large v waves</strong> are characteristic of tricuspid regurgitation; <strong>cannon a waves</strong> arise when the atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve, as can occur with AV dissociation. Raised JVP suggests elevated right atrial pressure; compare with a <strong>carotid arterial pulse</strong>, which is palpable, has one dominant systolic upstroke and is less dependent on posture.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why does JVP often fall with inspiration? Negative intrathoracic pressure encourages systemic venous return to the thorax; the normal observed response is downward movement of the venous column (with important pathological exceptions).</p>\n","plain":" Arterial Pulse and Jugular Venous Pulse \n Arterial pulse \n The palpable arterial pulse is a  pressure/distension wave , not a bolus of blood travelling at the same speed down the artery. Its propagation speed generally rises with arterial stiffness. Assess  rate, rhythm, volume, contour/character, and symmetry . \n Pulse pressure tends to increase when stroke volume increases or arterial compliance decreases; the relationship is modified by wave reflection and peripheral resistance. \n Jugular venous pulse (JVP) \n The internal jugular venous pulsation transmits changes in  right atrial pressure . Unlike a carotid arterial pulse, it is normally  non-palpable , varies with posture/respiration and has more than one wave per cycle. JVP is clinically estimated from its vertical height above the sternal angle with appropriate patient positioning. \n The major JVP features are: \n \n  a wave:  right atrial contraction. \n  c wave:  early ventricular systole, including tricuspid bulging/adjacent arterial transmission. \n  x descent:  atrial relaxation and downward tricuspid motion. \n  v wave:  right atrial filling against a closed tricuspid valve. \n  y descent:  opening of the tricuspid valve and rapid ventricular filling. \n \n Large  v waves  may occur with tricuspid regurgitation.  Cannon a waves  can occur when atria contract against a closed tricuspid valve, including AV dissociation. These are not interchangeable. \n  Check:  Why can the neck show a venous pressure wave even though venous blood flows toward the heart? \n  Answer Right atrial pressure fluctuations propagate backward through the compliant venous column; the pulsation is a pressure signal, not necessarily retrograde bulk flow. \n\n Distinguish the arterial pulse from actual flow \n Each systolic ejection creates a  pressure/distension wave  that travels much faster than the blood's average forward velocity. Wave transmission increases as arteries stiffen with age; pulse quality may be assessed through rate, regularity, amplitude, contour, symmetry and location. The origin is LV ejection, but the palpable pulse is  not an individual packet of blood  moving from the aorta to the wrist. \n Place JVP waves in the cardiac cycle \n The  a wave  follows right atrial contraction; the  c wave  occurs around early ventricular systole;  x descent  includes atrial relaxation and downward tricuspid displacement; the  v wave  reflects filling of the right atrium while the tricuspid valve remains closed; and the  y descent  accompanies tricuspid opening and right ventricular filling. The three positive waves are  a, c and v ; the descents are  x and y . \n Different abnormalities enlarge different waves:  large v waves  are characteristic of tricuspid regurgitation;  cannon a waves  arise when the atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve, as can occur with AV dissociation. Raised JVP suggests elevated right atrial pressure; compare with a  carotid arterial pulse , which is palpable, has one dominant systolic upstroke and is less dependent on posture. \n  Practice:  Why does JVP often fall with inspiration? Negative intrathoracic pressure encourages systemic venous return to the thorax; the normal observed response is downward movement of the venous column (with important pathological exceptions). \n"},{"slug":"venous-return","title":"Veins and Venous Return","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"43-44 and 73-75 and 97-98","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"venous-return-determines-how-much-is-available-to-pump","title":"Venous return determines how much is available to pump"},{"id":"pressure-gradients-posture-and-muscle-pumping","title":"Pressure gradients, posture and muscle pumping"},{"id":"pressure-compliance-and-venous-reservoir","title":"Pressure, compliance and venous reservoir"},{"id":"pumps-and-valves","title":"Pumps and valves"}],"html":"<h1>Veins and Venous Return</h1>\n<h2 id=\"venous-return-determines-how-much-is-available-to-pump\">Venous return determines how much is available to pump</h2>\n<p><strong>Venous return (VR)</strong> is blood flowing back to the right atrium per unit time. In a steady state, <strong>venous return equals cardiac output</strong>. When venous return falls abruptly, right ventricular filling drops and—after a delay—left ventricular preload and stroke volume fall.</p>\n<p>Veins hold a major share of blood volume because they are compliant. Increased sympathetic venous tone reduces venous capacitance and mobilizes blood toward the heart. The skeletal-muscle pump, valves and respiratory pressure changes facilitate return from dependent limbs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pressure-gradients-posture-and-muscle-pumping\">Pressure gradients, posture and muscle pumping</h2>\n<p>Blood flows toward the right atrium along a driving pressure gradient. A useful conceptual Guyton relationship is <strong>VR = (mean systemic filling pressure − right atrial pressure)/resistance to venous return</strong>, interpreted with attention to vascular collapse and physiological limitations.</p>\n<p>When standing still, gravity promotes venous pooling in the legs. Walking or subtle calf contractions compress veins; valves promote one-way movement. This explains why prolonged motionless standing can cause light-headedness and why soldiers subtly contract their leg muscles while standing.</p>\n<p>Inspiration usually lowers intrathoracic pressure, facilitating systemic venous return to the right heart; effects on left-heart filling and BP are more complex and may be delayed.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why can a very strong heartbeat fail to maintain output in severe haemorrhage?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Myocardial contractility cannot eject blood that never fills the heart. Low circulating volume causes reduced VR, EDV and ultimately stroke volume.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"pressure-compliance-and-venous-reservoir\">Pressure, compliance and venous reservoir</h2>\n<p>Veins are <strong>capacitance vessels</strong>: they contain a large proportion of resting circulating blood because they can accommodate substantial volume at low pressure. Venoconstriction decreases venous capacity and tends to shift blood toward the central circulation, raising <strong>mean systemic filling pressure</strong> and venous return when other conditions permit.</p>\n<p>Venous return depends on the pressure difference between peripheral veins/mean systemic filling pressure and the right atrium, divided by resistance to venous return. In steady state <strong>venous return = cardiac output</strong> because blood cannot accumulate continuously in either side of a closed system. A normal right atrial/central venous pressure is generally low (often approximately 0–5 mmHg in a resting adult); avoid accepting every old slide's absolute value without the measurement context.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pumps-and-valves\">Pumps and valves</h2>\n<p>Leg venous valves prevent backflow when the <strong>skeletal-muscle pump</strong> compresses deep veins, including during subtle contractions while standing. Inspiration reduces intrathoracic pressure, helping draw blood toward the thorax, although abdominal pressure and mechanical ventilation modify the effect. Arterial pulsation can aid nearby venous movement. Prolonged immobility permits pooling and may impair venous return.</p>\n<p>In the head, collapsible extracranial veins can narrow under low pressure, but <strong>dural venous sinuses</strong> are held open by dural attachments. An injured/open venous sinus may admit air, causing venous air embolism.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why can venous return decrease even if blood volume has not changed? Increased venous pooling or right atrial pressure can reduce the pressure gradient driving return.</p>\n","plain":" Veins and Venous Return \n Venous return determines how much is available to pump \n  Venous return (VR)  is blood flowing back to the right atrium per unit time. In a steady state,  venous return equals cardiac output . When venous return falls abruptly, right ventricular filling drops and—after a delay—left ventricular preload and stroke volume fall. \n Veins hold a major share of blood volume because they are compliant. Increased sympathetic venous tone reduces venous capacitance and mobilizes blood toward the heart. The skeletal-muscle pump, valves and respiratory pressure changes facilitate return from dependent limbs. \n Pressure gradients, posture and muscle pumping \n Blood flows toward the right atrium along a driving pressure gradient. A useful conceptual Guyton relationship is  VR = (mean systemic filling pressure − right atrial pressure)/resistance to venous return , interpreted with attention to vascular collapse and physiological limitations. \n When standing still, gravity promotes venous pooling in the legs. Walking or subtle calf contractions compress veins; valves promote one-way movement. This explains why prolonged motionless standing can cause light-headedness and why soldiers subtly contract their leg muscles while standing. \n Inspiration usually lowers intrathoracic pressure, facilitating systemic venous return to the right heart; effects on left-heart filling and BP are more complex and may be delayed. \n  Check:  Why can a very strong heartbeat fail to maintain output in severe haemorrhage? \n  Answer Myocardial contractility cannot eject blood that never fills the heart. Low circulating volume causes reduced VR, EDV and ultimately stroke volume. \n\n Pressure, compliance and venous reservoir \n Veins are  capacitance vessels : they contain a large proportion of resting circulating blood because they can accommodate substantial volume at low pressure. Venoconstriction decreases venous capacity and tends to shift blood toward the central circulation, raising  mean systemic filling pressure  and venous return when other conditions permit. \n Venous return depends on the pressure difference between peripheral veins/mean systemic filling pressure and the right atrium, divided by resistance to venous return. In steady state  venous return = cardiac output  because blood cannot accumulate continuously in either side of a closed system. A normal right atrial/central venous pressure is generally low (often approximately 0–5 mmHg in a resting adult); avoid accepting every old slide's absolute value without the measurement context. \n Pumps and valves \n Leg venous valves prevent backflow when the  skeletal-muscle pump  compresses deep veins, including during subtle contractions while standing. Inspiration reduces intrathoracic pressure, helping draw blood toward the thorax, although abdominal pressure and mechanical ventilation modify the effect. Arterial pulsation can aid nearby venous movement. Prolonged immobility permits pooling and may impair venous return. \n In the head, collapsible extracranial veins can narrow under low pressure, but  dural venous sinuses  are held open by dural attachments. An injured/open venous sinus may admit air, causing venous air embolism. \n  Practice:  Why can venous return decrease even if blood volume has not changed? Increased venous pooling or right atrial pressure can reduce the pressure gradient driving return. \n"},{"slug":"microcirculation","title":"Microcirculation and Capillary Exchange","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"57-60 and 84-89","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"structure-matches-function","title":"Structure matches function"},{"id":"diffusion-versus-bulk-fluid-movement","title":"Diffusion versus bulk fluid movement"},{"id":"what-changes-exchange","title":"What changes exchange?"},{"id":"capillary-wall-specialisations","title":"Capillary wall specialisations"},{"id":"routes-of-exchange","title":"Routes of exchange"}],"html":"<h1>Microcirculation and Capillary Exchange</h1>\n<h2 id=\"structure-matches-function\">Structure matches function</h2>\n<p>Capillaries consist principally of endothelial cells, a <strong>basal lamina</strong> and associated pericytes, with no typical three-layered arterial wall. Their small diameter and thin exchange surface facilitate diffusion. <strong>Continuous</strong>, <strong>fenestrated</strong> and <strong>sinusoidal/discontinuous</strong> capillaries differ in permeability and location.</p>\n<p>Arterioles regulate incoming resistance and perfused surface area. Capillary recruitment improves exchange area in active tissues. Precapillary sphincter-like regulation varies between vascular beds and should not be assumed identical everywhere.</p>\n<h2 id=\"diffusion-versus-bulk-fluid-movement\">Diffusion versus bulk fluid movement</h2>\n<p><strong>Diffusion</strong> is driven primarily by concentration/partial-pressure gradients and is central for O₂, CO₂ and many solutes. <strong>Bulk filtration</strong> reflects hydrostatic and oncotic pressure differences across vascular barriers. Proteins remaining in plasma contribute to colloid osmotic pressure and oppose outward water movement.</p>\n<p>The classic Starling expression relates net fluid flux to capillary/interstitial hydrostatic and oncotic pressures. Modern refinements recognize the <strong>endothelial glycocalyx</strong> and subglycocalyx protein environment; in many continuous capillary beds there is modest net filtration along much of the vessel, with lymphatics returning interstitial fluid rather than routine large venous-end reabsorption.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-changes-exchange\">What changes exchange?</h2>\n<p>Greater capillary pressure or permeability promotes filtration; lower plasma oncotic pressure also favours tissue-fluid accumulation. Increased capillary surface area can accelerate exchange.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Do capillaries need a thick subendothelial connective-tissue layer to have a basement membrane?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>No. The basal lamina is specialised extracellular matrix closely associated with endothelial cells. A thick subendothelial connective-tissue layer is not required.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"capillary-wall-specialisations\">Capillary wall specialisations</h2>\n<p><strong>Continuous capillaries</strong> have an uninterrupted endothelial lining and comparatively controlled permeability; they are typical in muscle and CNS (the CNS has especially tight junctions). <strong>Fenestrated capillaries</strong> have small pores that favour exchange in tissues such as intestinal villi, endocrine glands and renal glomeruli (glomerular filtration also depends on a specialised basement membrane and podocytes). <strong>Sinusoidal/discontinuous capillaries</strong> have larger, irregular gaps and occur in liver, spleen and bone marrow. All are lined by endothelium supported by a <strong>basal lamina</strong> to varying degrees; capillaries do not have a true muscular tunica media.</p>\n<p>The lecture's diagram describing capillary pores in <strong>millimetres</strong> must not be copied literally: physiological fenestrations are commonly on the <strong>nanometre scale</strong>, not tens of millimetres. <strong>Precapillary sphincters</strong> are described in classical microcirculatory models, but not every microvascular bed has discrete anatomically identical sphincters. Arteriolar tone and local microvascular mechanisms regulate perfused capillary number.</p>\n<h2 id=\"routes-of-exchange\">Routes of exchange</h2>\n<p><strong>Diffusion</strong> carries oxygen, carbon dioxide and many small solutes across short concentration gradients; lipid-soluble gases cross cell membranes readily. <strong>Paracellular pathways/fenestrations</strong> permit varying movement of water and hydrophilic solutes. <strong>Vesicular transport</strong> contributes to selected larger-molecule transfer, depending on tissue. <strong>Bulk fluid filtration</strong> is driven by hydrostatic and oncotic pressures and the endothelial barrier; it is not the same process as individual solute diffusion.</p>\n<p>As a tissue becomes metabolically active, local mediators decrease arteriolar resistance and recruit better-perfused exchange routes, matching delivery to demand. Capillary blood normally travels slowly because the <strong>entire capillary bed has large cross-sectional area</strong>, promoting exchange time.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why are endocrine tissues often supplied with fenestrated capillaries? Their vascular structure supports relatively rapid exchange of circulating hormones and solutes.</p>\n","plain":" Microcirculation and Capillary Exchange \n Structure matches function \n Capillaries consist principally of endothelial cells, a  basal lamina  and associated pericytes, with no typical three-layered arterial wall. Their small diameter and thin exchange surface facilitate diffusion.  Continuous ,  fenestrated  and  sinusoidal/discontinuous  capillaries differ in permeability and location. \n Arterioles regulate incoming resistance and perfused surface area. Capillary recruitment improves exchange area in active tissues. Precapillary sphincter-like regulation varies between vascular beds and should not be assumed identical everywhere. \n Diffusion versus bulk fluid movement \n  Diffusion  is driven primarily by concentration/partial-pressure gradients and is central for O₂, CO₂ and many solutes.  Bulk filtration  reflects hydrostatic and oncotic pressure differences across vascular barriers. Proteins remaining in plasma contribute to colloid osmotic pressure and oppose outward water movement. \n The classic Starling expression relates net fluid flux to capillary/interstitial hydrostatic and oncotic pressures. Modern refinements recognize the  endothelial glycocalyx  and subglycocalyx protein environment; in many continuous capillary beds there is modest net filtration along much of the vessel, with lymphatics returning interstitial fluid rather than routine large venous-end reabsorption. \n What changes exchange? \n Greater capillary pressure or permeability promotes filtration; lower plasma oncotic pressure also favours tissue-fluid accumulation. Increased capillary surface area can accelerate exchange. \n  Check:  Do capillaries need a thick subendothelial connective-tissue layer to have a basement membrane? \n  Answer No. The basal lamina is specialised extracellular matrix closely associated with endothelial cells. A thick subendothelial connective-tissue layer is not required. \n\n Capillary wall specialisations \n  Continuous capillaries  have an uninterrupted endothelial lining and comparatively controlled permeability; they are typical in muscle and CNS (the CNS has especially tight junctions).  Fenestrated capillaries  have small pores that favour exchange in tissues such as intestinal villi, endocrine glands and renal glomeruli (glomerular filtration also depends on a specialised basement membrane and podocytes).  Sinusoidal/discontinuous capillaries  have larger, irregular gaps and occur in liver, spleen and bone marrow. All are lined by endothelium supported by a  basal lamina  to varying degrees; capillaries do not have a true muscular tunica media. \n The lecture's diagram describing capillary pores in  millimetres  must not be copied literally: physiological fenestrations are commonly on the  nanometre scale , not tens of millimetres.  Precapillary sphincters  are described in classical microcirculatory models, but not every microvascular bed has discrete anatomically identical sphincters. Arteriolar tone and local microvascular mechanisms regulate perfused capillary number. \n Routes of exchange \n  Diffusion  carries oxygen, carbon dioxide and many small solutes across short concentration gradients; lipid-soluble gases cross cell membranes readily.  Paracellular pathways/fenestrations  permit varying movement of water and hydrophilic solutes.  Vesicular transport  contributes to selected larger-molecule transfer, depending on tissue.  Bulk fluid filtration  is driven by hydrostatic and oncotic pressures and the endothelial barrier; it is not the same process as individual solute diffusion. \n As a tissue becomes metabolically active, local mediators decrease arteriolar resistance and recruit better-perfused exchange routes, matching delivery to demand. Capillary blood normally travels slowly because the  entire capillary bed has large cross-sectional area , promoting exchange time. \n  Practice:  Why are endocrine tissues often supplied with fenestrated capillaries? Their vascular structure supports relatively rapid exchange of circulating hormones and solutes. \n"},{"slug":"edema","title":"Formation of Oedema","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"89-95","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"oedema-is-excessive-interstitial-fluid","title":"Oedema is excessive interstitial fluid"},{"id":"main-mechanisms","title":"Main mechanisms"},{"id":"why-location-matters","title":"Why location matters"},{"id":"put-starling-s-forces-into-one-equation","title":"Put Starling's forces into one equation"},{"id":"predict-the-distribution-of-oedema","title":"Predict the distribution of oedema"}],"html":"<h1>Formation of Oedema</h1>\n<h2 id=\"oedema-is-excessive-interstitial-fluid\">Oedema is excessive interstitial fluid</h2>\n<p>Interstitial fluid volume depends on fluid leaving the microvessels, its return through lymphatics, and tissue properties. Oedema results when filtration exceeds lymphatic drainage capacity, or when drainage is impaired.</p>\n<h2 id=\"main-mechanisms\">Main mechanisms</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure</strong> — e.g. venous obstruction, prolonged standing, some forms of heart failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Reduced plasma oncotic pressure</strong> — e.g. marked hypoalbuminaemia from liver disease, nephrotic protein loss or severe malnutrition.</li>\n<li><strong>Increased endothelial permeability</strong> — e.g. inflammation, burns, allergic mediator release.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymphatic obstruction/dysfunction</strong> — e.g. lymph node removal, tumour, filariasis.</li>\n<li><strong>Renal sodium/water retention</strong> — expands extracellular fluid and can increase microvascular hydrostatic pressures.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"why-location-matters\">Why location matters</h2>\n<p>Dependent ankle oedema increases after prolonged standing because gravitational hydrostatic pressure rises in leg veins. Pulmonary oedema can occur when elevated pulmonary venous pressure drives excess filtration into lung interstitium and alveoli, disrupting gas exchange.</p>\n<p>Pitting oedema usually reflects free interstitial fluid; established lymphoedema may become less pitting due to fibrosis. The distinction is useful but not absolute.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why does reduced plasma albumin increase the risk of oedema?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Lower plasma colloid osmotic pressure reduces the inward force opposing filtration, favouring accumulation of interstitial fluid, especially when lymphatic return is exceeded.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"put-starling-s-forces-into-one-equation\">Put Starling's forces into one equation</h2>\n<p>A useful traditional expression is <strong>Jv = Kf[(Pc − Pi) − σ(πc − πi)]</strong>, where Jv is net fluid movement, Kf filtration coefficient, Pc/Pi hydrostatic pressures inside capillary/interstitium, πc/πi their colloid osmotic pressures, and σ the protein reflection coefficient. Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure (<strong>venous obstruction or heart failure</strong>), reduced plasma oncotic pressure (<strong>hypoalbuminaemia</strong>), increased barrier permeability (<strong>inflammation</strong>) or obstructed lymphatics can all produce interstitial fluid accumulation.</p>\n<p>The lecturer's <strong>venous-end reabsorption diagram (Circulation 1, slide 87)</strong> represents a classic teaching model. The modern <strong>endothelial glycocalyx</strong> model places the main relevant oncotic gradient across the glycocalyx and recognises that many continuous capillaries have persistent small net filtration at steady state, with filtered fluid returned by <strong>lymphatics</strong>. Transient reabsorption can still occur after a fall in capillary hydrostatic pressure; the old universal '85% reabsorbed at venous end' formulation is <strong>not a universal rule</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"predict-the-distribution-of-oedema\">Predict the distribution of oedema</h2>\n<p>Raised systemic venous pressure causes dependent oedema, often most evident at ankles while upright. Left heart failure can raise <strong>pulmonary venous pressure</strong>, causing pulmonary congestion/oedema. Hypoalbuminaemia lowers the force retaining fluid intravascularly and can produce more widespread oedema. Lymphatic obstruction often produces chronic local swelling with tissue remodelling and may become relatively <strong>non-pitting</strong>, while early lymphoedema can still pit.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> How can prolonged standing cause ankle swelling? Gravity increases dependent venous/capillary hydrostatic pressure, raising filtration; without sufficient muscle/lymph pumping, fluid accumulates.</p>\n","plain":" Formation of Oedema \n Oedema is excessive interstitial fluid \n Interstitial fluid volume depends on fluid leaving the microvessels, its return through lymphatics, and tissue properties. Oedema results when filtration exceeds lymphatic drainage capacity, or when drainage is impaired. \n Main mechanisms \n \n  Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure  — e.g. venous obstruction, prolonged standing, some forms of heart failure. \n  Reduced plasma oncotic pressure  — e.g. marked hypoalbuminaemia from liver disease, nephrotic protein loss or severe malnutrition. \n  Increased endothelial permeability  — e.g. inflammation, burns, allergic mediator release. \n  Lymphatic obstruction/dysfunction  — e.g. lymph node removal, tumour, filariasis. \n  Renal sodium/water retention  — expands extracellular fluid and can increase microvascular hydrostatic pressures. \n \n Why location matters \n Dependent ankle oedema increases after prolonged standing because gravitational hydrostatic pressure rises in leg veins. Pulmonary oedema can occur when elevated pulmonary venous pressure drives excess filtration into lung interstitium and alveoli, disrupting gas exchange. \n Pitting oedema usually reflects free interstitial fluid; established lymphoedema may become less pitting due to fibrosis. The distinction is useful but not absolute. \n  Check:  Why does reduced plasma albumin increase the risk of oedema? \n  Answer Lower plasma colloid osmotic pressure reduces the inward force opposing filtration, favouring accumulation of interstitial fluid, especially when lymphatic return is exceeded. \n\n Put Starling's forces into one equation \n A useful traditional expression is  Jv = Kf[(Pc − Pi) − σ(πc − πi)] , where Jv is net fluid movement, Kf filtration coefficient, Pc/Pi hydrostatic pressures inside capillary/interstitium, πc/πi their colloid osmotic pressures, and σ the protein reflection coefficient. Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure ( venous obstruction or heart failure ), reduced plasma oncotic pressure ( hypoalbuminaemia ), increased barrier permeability ( inflammation ) or obstructed lymphatics can all produce interstitial fluid accumulation. \n The lecturer's  venous-end reabsorption diagram (Circulation 1, slide 87)  represents a classic teaching model. The modern  endothelial glycocalyx  model places the main relevant oncotic gradient across the glycocalyx and recognises that many continuous capillaries have persistent small net filtration at steady state, with filtered fluid returned by  lymphatics . Transient reabsorption can still occur after a fall in capillary hydrostatic pressure; the old universal '85% reabsorbed at venous end' formulation is  not a universal rule . \n Predict the distribution of oedema \n Raised systemic venous pressure causes dependent oedema, often most evident at ankles while upright. Left heart failure can raise  pulmonary venous pressure , causing pulmonary congestion/oedema. Hypoalbuminaemia lowers the force retaining fluid intravascularly and can produce more widespread oedema. Lymphatic obstruction often produces chronic local swelling with tissue remodelling and may become relatively  non-pitting , while early lymphoedema can still pit. \n  Practice:  How can prolonged standing cause ankle swelling? Gravity increases dependent venous/capillary hydrostatic pressure, raising filtration; without sufficient muscle/lymph pumping, fluid accumulates. \n"},{"slug":"lymphatics","title":"Lymphatic Circulation","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"60 and 94-95; Regional Circulation 47-48","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"a-return-route-for-interstitial-fluid-and-proteins","title":"A return route for interstitial fluid and proteins"},{"id":"why-lymphatic-flow-matters-to-starling-balance","title":"Why lymphatic flow matters to Starling balance"},{"id":"map-lymphatic-return-to-venous-blood","title":"Map lymphatic return to venous blood"},{"id":"why-lymphatic-obstruction-causes-swelling","title":"Why lymphatic obstruction causes swelling"}],"html":"<h1>Lymphatic Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"a-return-route-for-interstitial-fluid-and-proteins\">A return route for interstitial fluid and proteins</h2>\n<p>Lymphatic capillaries begin blindly in tissues and collect water, proteins and other materials that cannot simply remain in the interstitium. Collecting lymphatics contain valves and contractile segments; skeletal-muscle activity and external pressure help lymph move toward the venous circulation.</p>\n<p>Most of the body drains via the <strong>thoracic duct</strong> into the left venous angle near the internal jugular–subclavian junction. The <strong>right lymphatic duct</strong> drains a smaller right-upper quadrant territory to the right venous angle. Lymph nodes also support immune surveillance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-lymphatic-flow-matters-to-starling-balance\">Why lymphatic flow matters to Starling balance</h2>\n<p>Microvessels continually filter some fluid into tissue spaces. Lymphatics return excess fluid and leaked proteins to the circulation. If filtration rises sufficiently, lymph flow initially increases; when capacity is overwhelmed, oedema results.</p>\n<p>Blockage after node surgery, malignancy or filarial infection can cause lymphoedema. Lymphatic obstruction can yield protein-rich interstitial fluid, leading over time to tissue inflammation and fibrosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why does lymphatic obstruction cause swelling even with normal cardiac output?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Net microvascular filtration continues, but its main return pathway is impaired; fluid and proteins accumulate in the interstitial space.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"map-lymphatic-return-to-venous-blood\">Map lymphatic return to venous blood</h2>\n<p>Initial lymphatic capillaries are blind-ended channels with overlapping endothelial flaps that admit fluid, proteins and some particles from interstitium. Collecting vessels have <strong>valves</strong> and rhythmic smooth-muscle activity; skeletal-muscle movement, breathing and nearby arterial pulsation help propel lymph toward the venous angles.</p>\n<p>The <strong>thoracic duct</strong> drains most of the body, usually entering the junction of left internal jugular and left subclavian veins. The <strong>right lymphatic duct</strong> drains the right upper quadrant into the corresponding right venous angle; anatomy varies. Lymph passes through <strong>lymph nodes</strong>, where immune surveillance occurs. Specialized intestinal lymphatics called <strong>lacteals</strong> transport absorbed long-chain dietary lipids as <strong>chylomicrons</strong>; they do not directly absorb all lipids by the same route.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-lymphatic-obstruction-causes-swelling\">Why lymphatic obstruction causes swelling</h2>\n<p>When capillaries filter fluid and leak proteins into the interstitium, lymphatics return that material to blood. An obstruction (e.g. <strong>filariasis</strong> or surgery affecting lymph drainage) reduces clearance of interstitial fluid and protein, contributing to chronic swelling and potentially fibrosis. This connects directly to the lecturer's lymphoedema examples (Circulation 1, slide 95) and the lymphatic diagrams in Regional Circulation slides 47–48.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why doesn't filtered capillary fluid normally accumulate progressively in the legs? Lymphatic removal, aided by movement and local mechanisms, returns most surplus filtered fluid to the venous system.</p>\n","plain":" Lymphatic Circulation \n A return route for interstitial fluid and proteins \n Lymphatic capillaries begin blindly in tissues and collect water, proteins and other materials that cannot simply remain in the interstitium. Collecting lymphatics contain valves and contractile segments; skeletal-muscle activity and external pressure help lymph move toward the venous circulation. \n Most of the body drains via the  thoracic duct  into the left venous angle near the internal jugular–subclavian junction. The  right lymphatic duct  drains a smaller right-upper quadrant territory to the right venous angle. Lymph nodes also support immune surveillance. \n Why lymphatic flow matters to Starling balance \n Microvessels continually filter some fluid into tissue spaces. Lymphatics return excess fluid and leaked proteins to the circulation. If filtration rises sufficiently, lymph flow initially increases; when capacity is overwhelmed, oedema results. \n Blockage after node surgery, malignancy or filarial infection can cause lymphoedema. Lymphatic obstruction can yield protein-rich interstitial fluid, leading over time to tissue inflammation and fibrosis. \n  Check:  Why does lymphatic obstruction cause swelling even with normal cardiac output? \n  Answer Net microvascular filtration continues, but its main return pathway is impaired; fluid and proteins accumulate in the interstitial space. \n\n Map lymphatic return to venous blood \n Initial lymphatic capillaries are blind-ended channels with overlapping endothelial flaps that admit fluid, proteins and some particles from interstitium. Collecting vessels have  valves  and rhythmic smooth-muscle activity; skeletal-muscle movement, breathing and nearby arterial pulsation help propel lymph toward the venous angles. \n The  thoracic duct  drains most of the body, usually entering the junction of left internal jugular and left subclavian veins. The  right lymphatic duct  drains the right upper quadrant into the corresponding right venous angle; anatomy varies. Lymph passes through  lymph nodes , where immune surveillance occurs. Specialized intestinal lymphatics called  lacteals  transport absorbed long-chain dietary lipids as  chylomicrons ; they do not directly absorb all lipids by the same route. \n Why lymphatic obstruction causes swelling \n When capillaries filter fluid and leak proteins into the interstitium, lymphatics return that material to blood. An obstruction (e.g.  filariasis  or surgery affecting lymph drainage) reduces clearance of interstitial fluid and protein, contributing to chronic swelling and potentially fibrosis. This connects directly to the lecturer's lymphoedema examples (Circulation 1, slide 95) and the lymphatic diagrams in Regional Circulation slides 47–48. \n  Practice:  Why doesn't filtered capillary fluid normally accumulate progressively in the legs? Lymphatic removal, aided by movement and local mechanisms, returns most surplus filtered fluid to the venous system. \n"},{"slug":"local-regulation","title":"Local Control of Blood Flow","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf","pages":"1-11","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"a-tissue-can-adjust-its-own-supply","title":"A tissue can adjust its own supply"},{"id":"metabolic-mechanism","title":"Metabolic mechanism"},{"id":"myogenic-mechanism","title":"Myogenic mechanism"},{"id":"endothelial-signals","title":"Endothelial signals"},{"id":"compare-metabolic-and-myogenic-autoregulation","title":"Compare metabolic and myogenic autoregulation"},{"id":"do-not-memorise-the-incorrect-laplace-formula","title":"Do not memorise the incorrect Laplace formula"},{"id":"endothelium-active-signalling-organ","title":"Endothelium: active signalling organ"}],"html":"<h1>Local Control of Blood Flow</h1>\n<h2 id=\"a-tissue-can-adjust-its-own-supply\">A tissue can adjust its own supply</h2>\n<p><strong>Autoregulation</strong> is a tissue's ability to keep flow relatively stable despite moderate changes in perfusion pressure. It is especially important in cerebral, renal and coronary circulations. A change in arteriole calibre alters local resistance: <strong>Q = ΔP/R</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"metabolic-mechanism\">Metabolic mechanism</h2>\n<p>When local tissue metabolism rises or perfusion falls, vasodilator signals tend to accumulate. Relevant signals can include increased CO₂/H⁺, K⁺, adenosine, lactate in some contexts, and reduced tissue O₂. Dilation increases flow, improves oxygen delivery and helps wash out signals. Different tissues do <strong>not</strong> use identical mixtures of mediators.</p>\n<p>Increased activity causes <strong>active hyperaemia</strong> (e.g. exercise in skeletal muscle). Transient vessel occlusion followed by a flow overshoot causes <strong>reactive hyperaemia</strong>, reflecting accumulated dilator signals and other mechanisms during reduced flow.</p>\n<h2 id=\"myogenic-mechanism\">Myogenic mechanism</h2>\n<p>An abrupt increase in intraluminal pressure stretches resistance-vessel smooth muscle, which responds by contracting. This increases resistance and helps prevent flow from rising excessively. Reduced stretch can favour relaxation. The myogenic response and metabolic control often act together.</p>\n<p><strong>Laplace's principle:</strong> a larger-radius thin-walled cylindrical vessel requires greater circumferential tension to oppose a given transmural pressure (approximately T = P × r per unit axial length). Different wall geometries use different mathematical forms. This helps explain why vessel radius matters to wall stress, though myogenic autoregulation involves active smooth-muscle signalling, not Laplace's law alone.</p>\n<h2 id=\"endothelial-signals\">Endothelial signals</h2>\n<p><strong>Nitric oxide (NO)</strong> produced by endothelium promotes smooth-muscle relaxation through cGMP. Endothelin is a powerful vasoconstrictor. Local endothelial, neural and metabolic influences collectively determine vessel calibre.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> A resistance arteriole is suddenly exposed to greater perfusion pressure. How can myogenic autoregulation oppose a large rise in flow?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Wall stretch activates smooth muscle contraction, reducing radius, raising resistance and opposing the tendency for pressure-driven flow to increase.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"compare-metabolic-and-myogenic-autoregulation\">Compare metabolic and myogenic autoregulation</h2>\n<p><strong>Metabolic theory:</strong> When local perfusion fails to match tissue activity, oxygen may fall while CO₂, H⁺, adenosine, K⁺ and other local signals accumulate. Arteriolar dilation increases blood delivery and helps wash out metabolites. This predicts <strong>active hyperaemia</strong> during heightened metabolism (for instance exercising skeletal muscle) and <strong>reactive hyperaemia</strong> following temporary arterial occlusion: the longer/deeper the interruption, the greater the transient unmet demand and typical flow overshoot when obstruction is released.</p>\n<p><strong>Myogenic mechanism:</strong> Higher <strong>transmural pressure = intravascular − surrounding pressure</strong> distends arteriolar smooth muscle, activating contraction and raising resistance; decreased pressure tends to reduce stretch-induced tone. This stabilises local flow despite moderate driving-pressure changes and is important, for example, in cerebral and renal vascular beds. Metabolic and myogenic explanations are <strong>complementary</strong>, not mutually exclusive.</p>\n<h2 id=\"do-not-memorise-the-incorrect-laplace-formula\">Do not memorise the incorrect Laplace formula</h2>\n<p>For an ideal <strong>thin-walled cylinder</strong>, circumferential tension per unit axial length is approximately <strong>T = Ptm × r</strong>. If describing average circumferential wall <strong>stress</strong>, <strong>σθ ≈ Ptm × r / wall thickness</strong>. For an ideal sphere, the corresponding membrane tension is <strong>T = Ptm × r / 2</strong>. These are model-dependent, and real arteries have thick, active walls; the inconsistent reciprocal/sum expressions on Regulation slides 9–10 should not be reproduced without fixing the definitions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"endothelium-active-signalling-organ\">Endothelium: active signalling organ</h2>\n<p><strong>Nitric oxide (NO)</strong> activates smooth-muscle soluble guanylyl cyclase → <strong>cGMP</strong> → relaxation. <strong>Endothelin-1</strong> is a potent constrictor. <strong>Histamine and bradykinin</strong> can promote vasodilatation and permeability changes through context-dependent pathways. Cold commonly favours cutaneous vasoconstriction. These responses depend on vascular bed and receptor distribution; do not infer one mediator affects every vessel identically.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> What happens to autoregulatory arteriolar tone if local perfusion pressure suddenly increases? Myogenic constriction tends to counter excessive flow; the metabolic response depends on local tissue needs.</p>\n","plain":" Local Control of Blood Flow \n A tissue can adjust its own supply \n  Autoregulation  is a tissue's ability to keep flow relatively stable despite moderate changes in perfusion pressure. It is especially important in cerebral, renal and coronary circulations. A change in arteriole calibre alters local resistance:  Q = ΔP/R . \n Metabolic mechanism \n When local tissue metabolism rises or perfusion falls, vasodilator signals tend to accumulate. Relevant signals can include increased CO₂/H⁺, K⁺, adenosine, lactate in some contexts, and reduced tissue O₂. Dilation increases flow, improves oxygen delivery and helps wash out signals. Different tissues do  not  use identical mixtures of mediators. \n Increased activity causes  active hyperaemia  (e.g. exercise in skeletal muscle). Transient vessel occlusion followed by a flow overshoot causes  reactive hyperaemia , reflecting accumulated dilator signals and other mechanisms during reduced flow. \n Myogenic mechanism \n An abrupt increase in intraluminal pressure stretches resistance-vessel smooth muscle, which responds by contracting. This increases resistance and helps prevent flow from rising excessively. Reduced stretch can favour relaxation. The myogenic response and metabolic control often act together. \n  Laplace's principle:  a larger-radius thin-walled cylindrical vessel requires greater circumferential tension to oppose a given transmural pressure (approximately T = P × r per unit axial length). Different wall geometries use different mathematical forms. This helps explain why vessel radius matters to wall stress, though myogenic autoregulation involves active smooth-muscle signalling, not Laplace's law alone. \n Endothelial signals \n  Nitric oxide (NO)  produced by endothelium promotes smooth-muscle relaxation through cGMP. Endothelin is a powerful vasoconstrictor. Local endothelial, neural and metabolic influences collectively determine vessel calibre. \n  Check:  A resistance arteriole is suddenly exposed to greater perfusion pressure. How can myogenic autoregulation oppose a large rise in flow? \n  Answer Wall stretch activates smooth muscle contraction, reducing radius, raising resistance and opposing the tendency for pressure-driven flow to increase. \n\n Compare metabolic and myogenic autoregulation \n  Metabolic theory:  When local perfusion fails to match tissue activity, oxygen may fall while CO₂, H⁺, adenosine, K⁺ and other local signals accumulate. Arteriolar dilation increases blood delivery and helps wash out metabolites. This predicts  active hyperaemia  during heightened metabolism (for instance exercising skeletal muscle) and  reactive hyperaemia  following temporary arterial occlusion: the longer/deeper the interruption, the greater the transient unmet demand and typical flow overshoot when obstruction is released. \n  Myogenic mechanism:  Higher  transmural pressure = intravascular − surrounding pressure  distends arteriolar smooth muscle, activating contraction and raising resistance; decreased pressure tends to reduce stretch-induced tone. This stabilises local flow despite moderate driving-pressure changes and is important, for example, in cerebral and renal vascular beds. Metabolic and myogenic explanations are  complementary , not mutually exclusive. \n Do not memorise the incorrect Laplace formula \n For an ideal  thin-walled cylinder , circumferential tension per unit axial length is approximately  T = Ptm × r . If describing average circumferential wall  stress ,  σθ ≈ Ptm × r / wall thickness . For an ideal sphere, the corresponding membrane tension is  T = Ptm × r / 2 . These are model-dependent, and real arteries have thick, active walls; the inconsistent reciprocal/sum expressions on Regulation slides 9–10 should not be reproduced without fixing the definitions. \n Endothelium: active signalling organ \n  Nitric oxide (NO)  activates smooth-muscle soluble guanylyl cyclase →  cGMP  → relaxation.  Endothelin-1  is a potent constrictor.  Histamine and bradykinin  can promote vasodilatation and permeability changes through context-dependent pathways. Cold commonly favours cutaneous vasoconstriction. These responses depend on vascular bed and receptor distribution; do not infer one mediator affects every vessel identically. \n  Practice:  What happens to autoregulatory arteriolar tone if local perfusion pressure suddenly increases? Myogenic constriction tends to counter excessive flow; the metabolic response depends on local tissue needs. \n"},{"slug":"neural-humoral","title":"Neural and Hormonal Regulation","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf","pages":"12-30","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"the-short-term-neural-controller","title":"The short-term neural controller"},{"id":"why-bp-depends-on-two-linked-factors","title":"Why BP depends on two linked factors"},{"id":"humoral-regulation","title":"Humoral regulation"},{"id":"time-scale-is-important","title":"Time scale is important"},{"id":"trace-the-medullary-cardiovascular-network","title":"Trace the medullary cardiovascular network"},{"id":"separate-vascular-tone-from-heart-rate","title":"Separate vascular tone from heart rate"},{"id":"choose-the-correct-time-scale","title":"Choose the correct time scale"}],"html":"<h1>Neural and Hormonal Cardiovascular Regulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"the-short-term-neural-controller\">The short-term neural controller</h2>\n<p>Brainstem cardiovascular networks (including medullary nuclei) coordinate sympathetic and parasympathetic output. Sympathetic nerves release mainly <strong>noradrenaline</strong> from postganglionic endings. <strong>α₁ receptors</strong> on many systemic resistance vessels cause vasoconstriction; sympathetic activation also constricts many veins, reducing capacitance. The heart responds mainly via <strong>β₁ receptors</strong>, increasing heart rate, conduction and contractility.</p>\n<p>Vagal <strong>acetylcholine–M₂</strong> signalling slows SA nodal rate and AV conduction. The vagus normally exerts considerable resting restraint on heart rate, so atropine or vagal withdrawal may raise it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-bp-depends-on-two-linked-factors\">Why BP depends on two linked factors</h2>\n<p>Approximately <strong>MAP ≈ CO × SVR</strong> if right atrial pressure is small. Cardiac sympathetic effects often increase CO; arteriolar α-mediated constriction increases systemic vascular resistance. Venoconstriction can also support preload, allowing output to rise when circulating volume is adequate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"humoral-regulation\">Humoral regulation</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Angiotensin II</strong> strongly constricts vessels and promotes aldosterone release.</li>\n<li><strong>Aldosterone</strong> helps retain sodium and water over hours/days.</li>\n<li><strong>Vasopressin (ADH)</strong> promotes renal water retention; at higher concentrations it can constrict vessels.</li>\n<li><strong>Adrenaline</strong> acts on different α and β receptors depending on dose and vascular bed.</li>\n<li><strong>Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP)</strong> promotes natriuresis and tends to counter volume expansion.</li>\n<li>Endothelium-derived NO, endothelin and local autacoids modify regional tone.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"time-scale-is-important\">Time scale is important</h2>\n<p>Neural regulation can act in seconds. Renal sodium/water balance and RAAS contribute importantly over longer intervals. Both levels interact rather than operating independently.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why can sympathetic activation raise BP through vessels even if HR changes little?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Arteriolar vasoconstriction raises systemic vascular resistance, while venoconstriction may support preload. Both influence arterial pressure independently of a large heart-rate change.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"trace-the-medullary-cardiovascular-network\">Trace the medullary cardiovascular network</h2>\n<p>The lecturer distinguishes medullary <strong>pressor</strong> and <strong>depressor</strong> regions. Modern functional descriptions identify the <strong>nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS)</strong> as a major sensory integrator, the <strong>rostral ventrolateral medulla (RVLM)</strong> as an important source of sympathetic vasomotor drive, and the <strong>caudal ventrolateral medulla (CVLM)</strong> as an inhibitory relay. These networks alter sympathetic and vagal traffic to heart and vessels.</p>\n<p><strong>Tonic sympathetic vasoconstrictor discharge</strong> maintains baseline arteriolar and venous tone. When discharge increases, <strong>α₁ receptors</strong> generally increase vascular smooth-muscle contraction. Decreasing tonic drive allows vasodilatation; it does not require a separate continuously active vasodilator nerve. Some vascular beds have specialised autonomic dilator influences, but the central teaching is widespread sympathetic vasoconstrictor tone and locally regulated perfusion.</p>\n<h2 id=\"separate-vascular-tone-from-heart-rate\">Separate vascular tone from heart rate</h2>\n<p><strong>β₁</strong> stimulation of SA node/ventricular myocardium can increase heart rate and contractility. <strong>M₂</strong> vagal signalling reduces sinus rate and AV conduction. Because <strong>CO = HR × SV</strong>, an increased HR does not guarantee increased CO when reduced filling lowers SV. Because <strong>MAP ≈ CO × SVR</strong> (when right atrial pressure is small), even a falling CO may coexist with temporarily maintained MAP when SVR increases.</p>\n<h2 id=\"choose-the-correct-time-scale\">Choose the correct time scale</h2>\n<p>Neural baroreflex responses act within <strong>seconds</strong>. <strong>Angiotensin II</strong> and catecholamines influence vascular tone over seconds to minutes; <strong>aldosterone</strong>, renal pressure natriuresis and sodium balance influence blood volume over hours to days. <strong>ANP</strong> is released with atrial stretch and promotes renal salt/water excretion. <strong>ADH</strong> supports water retention and at high concentrations can contribute to vasoconstriction.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> A person receives atropine, blocking M₂ receptors. Resting HR often <strong>rises</strong> because tonic vagal restraint of the SA node is reduced.</p>\n","plain":" Neural and Hormonal Cardiovascular Regulation \n The short-term neural controller \n Brainstem cardiovascular networks (including medullary nuclei) coordinate sympathetic and parasympathetic output. Sympathetic nerves release mainly  noradrenaline  from postganglionic endings.  α₁ receptors  on many systemic resistance vessels cause vasoconstriction; sympathetic activation also constricts many veins, reducing capacitance. The heart responds mainly via  β₁ receptors , increasing heart rate, conduction and contractility. \n Vagal  acetylcholine–M₂  signalling slows SA nodal rate and AV conduction. The vagus normally exerts considerable resting restraint on heart rate, so atropine or vagal withdrawal may raise it. \n Why BP depends on two linked factors \n Approximately  MAP ≈ CO × SVR  if right atrial pressure is small. Cardiac sympathetic effects often increase CO; arteriolar α-mediated constriction increases systemic vascular resistance. Venoconstriction can also support preload, allowing output to rise when circulating volume is adequate. \n Humoral regulation \n \n  Angiotensin II  strongly constricts vessels and promotes aldosterone release. \n  Aldosterone  helps retain sodium and water over hours/days. \n  Vasopressin (ADH)  promotes renal water retention; at higher concentrations it can constrict vessels. \n  Adrenaline  acts on different α and β receptors depending on dose and vascular bed. \n  Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP)  promotes natriuresis and tends to counter volume expansion. \n Endothelium-derived NO, endothelin and local autacoids modify regional tone. \n \n Time scale is important \n Neural regulation can act in seconds. Renal sodium/water balance and RAAS contribute importantly over longer intervals. Both levels interact rather than operating independently. \n  Check:  Why can sympathetic activation raise BP through vessels even if HR changes little? \n  Answer Arteriolar vasoconstriction raises systemic vascular resistance, while venoconstriction may support preload. Both influence arterial pressure independently of a large heart-rate change. \n\n Trace the medullary cardiovascular network \n The lecturer distinguishes medullary  pressor  and  depressor  regions. Modern functional descriptions identify the  nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS)  as a major sensory integrator, the  rostral ventrolateral medulla (RVLM)  as an important source of sympathetic vasomotor drive, and the  caudal ventrolateral medulla (CVLM)  as an inhibitory relay. These networks alter sympathetic and vagal traffic to heart and vessels. \n  Tonic sympathetic vasoconstrictor discharge  maintains baseline arteriolar and venous tone. When discharge increases,  α₁ receptors  generally increase vascular smooth-muscle contraction. Decreasing tonic drive allows vasodilatation; it does not require a separate continuously active vasodilator nerve. Some vascular beds have specialised autonomic dilator influences, but the central teaching is widespread sympathetic vasoconstrictor tone and locally regulated perfusion. \n Separate vascular tone from heart rate \n  β₁  stimulation of SA node/ventricular myocardium can increase heart rate and contractility.  M₂  vagal signalling reduces sinus rate and AV conduction. Because  CO = HR × SV , an increased HR does not guarantee increased CO when reduced filling lowers SV. Because  MAP ≈ CO × SVR  (when right atrial pressure is small), even a falling CO may coexist with temporarily maintained MAP when SVR increases. \n Choose the correct time scale \n Neural baroreflex responses act within  seconds .  Angiotensin II  and catecholamines influence vascular tone over seconds to minutes;  aldosterone , renal pressure natriuresis and sodium balance influence blood volume over hours to days.  ANP  is released with atrial stretch and promotes renal salt/water excretion.  ADH  supports water retention and at high concentrations can contribute to vasoconstriction. \n  Practice:  A person receives atropine, blocking M₂ receptors. Resting HR often  rises  because tonic vagal restraint of the SA node is reduced. \n"},{"slug":"reflexes","title":"Cardiovascular Reflexes and Cushing Response","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf","pages":"18-36","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"arterial-baroreflex-a-negative-feedback-loop","title":"Arterial baroreflex: a negative-feedback loop"},{"id":"atrial-stretch-and-volume-reflexes","title":"Atrial stretch and volume reflexes"},{"id":"cushing-response","title":"Cushing response"},{"id":"afferent-nerves-and-feedback-direction","title":"Afferent nerves and feedback direction"},{"id":"atrial-receptors-and-bainbridge-response","title":"Atrial receptors and Bainbridge response"},{"id":"raised-intracranial-pressure-connect-both-reflexes","title":"Raised intracranial pressure: connect both reflexes"}],"html":"<h1>Cardiovascular Reflexes and the Cushing Response</h1>\n<h2 id=\"arterial-baroreflex-a-negative-feedback-loop\">Arterial baroreflex: a negative-feedback loop</h2>\n<p>Stretch-sensitive endings in the <strong>carotid sinus</strong> and <strong>aortic arch</strong> report pressure changes. Carotid afferents travel via <strong>Hering's nerve</strong>, a branch of glossopharyngeal nerve <strong>IX</strong>; aortic afferents largely via vagus <strong>X</strong>. They project to the medullary <strong>nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS)</strong>, which modifies autonomic outflow.</p>\n<p>When BP rises: <strong>more baroreceptor firing → less sympathetic and more vagal cardiac activity → lower HR, contractility and peripheral resistance → pressure tends to fall</strong>. When BP falls, the reflex changes in the opposite direction. It is especially useful for rapid BP buffering, such as standing up.</p>\n<h2 id=\"atrial-stretch-and-volume-reflexes\">Atrial stretch and volume reflexes</h2>\n<p>Low-pressure cardiopulmonary receptors respond to changes in central blood volume. Atrial stretch can contribute to reflex tachycardia (<strong>Bainbridge reflex</strong>) and influence water/sodium regulation, although the actual HR response depends on competing reflexes and physiological context.</p>\n<h2 id=\"cushing-response\">Cushing response</h2>\n<p>Markedly raised <strong>intracranial pressure (ICP)</strong> reduces <strong>cerebral perfusion pressure ≈ MAP − ICP</strong> when venous pressure is not higher. Brainstem ischaemia can trigger intense sympathetic vasoconstriction and systemic hypertension to defend cerebral perfusion. That high arterial pressure then activates baroreceptors, contributing to <strong>reflex bradycardia</strong>. Altered respiration may occur; hypertension, bradycardia and abnormal respiration form the classic <strong>Cushing triad</strong>, a late warning sign of dangerous intracranial pressure elevation.</p>\n<p>The stimulus for hypertension is threatened brain perfusion, <strong>not</strong> the baroreceptor reflex itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why can increased intracranial pressure cause increased BP with a slower pulse?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Reduced brainstem perfusion triggers sympathetic hypertension; the resultant high arterial pressure causes baroreflex-mediated slowing of the heart.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"afferent-nerves-and-feedback-direction\">Afferent nerves and feedback direction</h2>\n<p>Stretch at the <strong>carotid sinus</strong> raises firing through <strong>Hering's nerve → glossopharyngeal nerve IX → NTS</strong>. Stretch at the <strong>aortic arch</strong> sends signals through <strong>vagus X → NTS</strong>. A sudden rise in arterial pressure therefore increases afferent firing, promoting <strong>vagal slowing</strong> of the heart and decreased sympathetic resistance-vessel tone. A pressure fall reduces afferent firing, disinhibiting sympathetic activity and reducing vagal restraint. The baroreceptors sense <strong>arterial wall stretch</strong>, not blood oxygen directly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"atrial-receptors-and-bainbridge-response\">Atrial receptors and Bainbridge response</h2>\n<p>Low-pressure receptors at atrial and pulmonary venous junctions respond to filling. The lecturer describes atrial <strong>type A</strong> discharges around atrial contraction and <strong>type B</strong> discharges during filling. Increased atrial stretch can promote a <strong>Bainbridge reflex</strong> increase in HR, helping the heart handle greater venous return, and engage hormonal responses that limit volume overload. Whether HR rises in an intact person depends on simultaneous baroreflex activity, making simplistic 'atrial stretch always causes tachycardia' unreliable.</p>\n<h2 id=\"raised-intracranial-pressure-connect-both-reflexes\">Raised intracranial pressure: connect both reflexes</h2>\n<p>When increased intracranial pressure compromises cerebral perfusion, medullary ischaemia may drive intense sympathetic vasoconstriction and <strong>hypertension</strong>. Elevated arterial pressure then triggers <strong>baroreflex bradycardia</strong>; respiratory irregularity may emerge from brainstem dysfunction. This is the <strong>Cushing response/triad</strong>. It does <em>not</em> mean raised intracranial pressure always creates high BP/low HR in every case; the triad is an ominous late pattern.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why can BP rise while HR falls? Two linked reflex processes operate: threatened brain perfusion drives sympathetic BP elevation, and arterial baroreceptors then favour bradycardia.</p>\n","plain":" Cardiovascular Reflexes and the Cushing Response \n Arterial baroreflex: a negative-feedback loop \n Stretch-sensitive endings in the  carotid sinus  and  aortic arch  report pressure changes. Carotid afferents travel via  Hering's nerve , a branch of glossopharyngeal nerve  IX ; aortic afferents largely via vagus  X . They project to the medullary  nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) , which modifies autonomic outflow. \n When BP rises:  more baroreceptor firing → less sympathetic and more vagal cardiac activity → lower HR, contractility and peripheral resistance → pressure tends to fall . When BP falls, the reflex changes in the opposite direction. It is especially useful for rapid BP buffering, such as standing up. \n Atrial stretch and volume reflexes \n Low-pressure cardiopulmonary receptors respond to changes in central blood volume. Atrial stretch can contribute to reflex tachycardia ( Bainbridge reflex ) and influence water/sodium regulation, although the actual HR response depends on competing reflexes and physiological context. \n Cushing response \n Markedly raised  intracranial pressure (ICP)  reduces  cerebral perfusion pressure ≈ MAP − ICP  when venous pressure is not higher. Brainstem ischaemia can trigger intense sympathetic vasoconstriction and systemic hypertension to defend cerebral perfusion. That high arterial pressure then activates baroreceptors, contributing to  reflex bradycardia . Altered respiration may occur; hypertension, bradycardia and abnormal respiration form the classic  Cushing triad , a late warning sign of dangerous intracranial pressure elevation. \n The stimulus for hypertension is threatened brain perfusion,  not  the baroreceptor reflex itself. \n  Check:  Why can increased intracranial pressure cause increased BP with a slower pulse? \n  Answer Reduced brainstem perfusion triggers sympathetic hypertension; the resultant high arterial pressure causes baroreflex-mediated slowing of the heart. \n\n Afferent nerves and feedback direction \n Stretch at the  carotid sinus  raises firing through  Hering's nerve → glossopharyngeal nerve IX → NTS . Stretch at the  aortic arch  sends signals through  vagus X → NTS . A sudden rise in arterial pressure therefore increases afferent firing, promoting  vagal slowing  of the heart and decreased sympathetic resistance-vessel tone. A pressure fall reduces afferent firing, disinhibiting sympathetic activity and reducing vagal restraint. The baroreceptors sense  arterial wall stretch , not blood oxygen directly. \n Atrial receptors and Bainbridge response \n Low-pressure receptors at atrial and pulmonary venous junctions respond to filling. The lecturer describes atrial  type A  discharges around atrial contraction and  type B  discharges during filling. Increased atrial stretch can promote a  Bainbridge reflex  increase in HR, helping the heart handle greater venous return, and engage hormonal responses that limit volume overload. Whether HR rises in an intact person depends on simultaneous baroreflex activity, making simplistic 'atrial stretch always causes tachycardia' unreliable. \n Raised intracranial pressure: connect both reflexes \n When increased intracranial pressure compromises cerebral perfusion, medullary ischaemia may drive intense sympathetic vasoconstriction and  hypertension . Elevated arterial pressure then triggers  baroreflex bradycardia ; respiratory irregularity may emerge from brainstem dysfunction. This is the  Cushing response/triad . It does  not  mean raised intracranial pressure always creates high BP/low HR in every case; the triad is an ominous late pattern. \n  Practice:  Why can BP rise while HR falls? Two linked reflex processes operate: threatened brain perfusion drives sympathetic BP elevation, and arterial baroreceptors then favour bradycardia. \n"},{"slug":"gravity","title":"Gravity and Orthostatic Homeostasis","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf","pages":"2-11; duplicate Lecture 5 pp2-11","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"what-changes-when-someone-stands","title":"What changes when someone stands?"},{"id":"the-rapid-compensation","title":"The rapid compensation"},{"id":"why-standing-motionless-can-cause-fainting","title":"Why standing motionless can cause fainting"},{"id":"acceleration-and-g-forces","title":"Acceleration and g-forces"},{"id":"separate-orthostatic-hypotension-and-vasovagal-syncope","title":"Separate orthostatic hypotension and vasovagal syncope"},{"id":"think-in-pressure-columns","title":"Think in pressure columns"}],"html":"<h1>Gravity and Orthostatic Homeostasis</h1>\n<h2 id=\"what-changes-when-someone-stands\">What changes when someone stands?</h2>\n<p>When a person rises from lying to standing, gravity increases hydrostatic pressure in dependent veins. Blood transiently pools in the legs and splanchnic circulation. This reduces central venous volume, venous return, ventricular end-diastolic volume, stroke volume and potentially cerebral perfusion.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-rapid-compensation\">The rapid compensation</h2>\n<p>Reduced carotid sinus/aortic arch stretch decreases baroreceptor firing. Brainstem circuits increase sympathetic activity and reduce vagal activity, producing <strong>tachycardia, venoconstriction and arteriolar constriction</strong>. The skeletal-muscle pump supports venous return when standing or walking. Over longer time periods, renal salt/water regulation contributes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-standing-motionless-can-cause-fainting\">Why standing motionless can cause fainting</h2>\n<p>Without muscle pumping, pooling can continue. If compensatory mechanisms fail, cerebral perfusion falls, causing dizziness, visual dimming or syncope. Lying flat restores the head nearer the heart and reduces the gravitational pressure difference.</p>\n<p><strong>Orthostatic hypotension</strong> is a sustained excessive fall in arterial pressure on standing; autonomic neuropathy, medications and volume depletion are potential causes. Vasovagal syncope has an additional reflex pattern of vasodilatation and often bradycardia, and should not be equated with all postural hypotension.</p>\n<h2 id=\"acceleration-and-g-forces\">Acceleration and g-forces</h2>\n<p>Positive head-to-foot acceleration promotes blood displacement toward the legs and can impair vision and consciousness; anti-g suits counter dependent pooling. Negative acceleration drives blood toward the head and may cause congestion or visual disturbances. Effects depend on direction, magnitude, duration and individual compensation.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why do soldiers subtly contract calf muscles while standing at attention?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Muscle contractions compress deep leg veins. Venous valves direct blood toward the heart, reducing pooling and helping maintain venous return, CO and cerebral perfusion.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"separate-orthostatic-hypotension-and-vasovagal-syncope\">Separate orthostatic hypotension and vasovagal syncope</h2>\n<p><strong>Orthostatic hypotension</strong> is commonly defined as a sustained fall of <strong>at least 20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within three minutes of standing</strong> (standard clinical criterion). Mechanistically, standing pools blood in dependent vessels, reduces preload and challenges cerebral perfusion. An intact baroreflex usually responds with <strong>tachycardia and vasoconstriction</strong>; autonomic failure, certain drugs, dehydration or prolonged pooling can overwhelm compensation. The lecturer's 'postural syncope = reflex bradycardia' should not be taken as the usual response to standing.</p>\n<p><strong>Vasovagal syncope</strong> often involves inappropriate reflex vasodilatation and/or bradycardia after triggers such as pain, heat, emotion or prolonged standing. It can also cause fainting but has a different autonomic pattern. Returning to the horizontal position restores a more favourable head-to-heart hydrostatic relationship.</p>\n<h2 id=\"think-in-pressure-columns\">Think in pressure columns</h2>\n<p>Arterial and venous pressure at the ankle when standing are higher than at heart level because of hydrostatic load. At the head, the opposite tendency occurs. Dependent capillary hydrostatic pressure rises with prolonged standing, encouraging <strong>ankle oedema</strong>. <strong>Leg-muscle isometric contractions</strong> compress deep veins: venous valves channel blood centrally and reduce pooling, an everyday explanation for the soldier-at-attention question.</p>\n<p>Under high positive head-to-foot <strong>+Gz</strong>, blood shifts toward legs and cerebral perfusion can fail, producing <strong>grey-out/blackout</strong>; anti-G suits increase external pressure over legs/abdomen. With negative <strong>−Gz</strong>, head/neck vascular congestion and 'red-out' symptoms may occur. Magnitude, direction and duration are crucial; the slide thresholds are illustrative rather than guarantees.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> A person is dizzy after standing motionless, but feels better while walking. Walking activates the venous muscle pump and helps maintain venous return.</p>\n","plain":" Gravity and Orthostatic Homeostasis \n What changes when someone stands? \n When a person rises from lying to standing, gravity increases hydrostatic pressure in dependent veins. Blood transiently pools in the legs and splanchnic circulation. This reduces central venous volume, venous return, ventricular end-diastolic volume, stroke volume and potentially cerebral perfusion. \n The rapid compensation \n Reduced carotid sinus/aortic arch stretch decreases baroreceptor firing. Brainstem circuits increase sympathetic activity and reduce vagal activity, producing  tachycardia, venoconstriction and arteriolar constriction . The skeletal-muscle pump supports venous return when standing or walking. Over longer time periods, renal salt/water regulation contributes. \n Why standing motionless can cause fainting \n Without muscle pumping, pooling can continue. If compensatory mechanisms fail, cerebral perfusion falls, causing dizziness, visual dimming or syncope. Lying flat restores the head nearer the heart and reduces the gravitational pressure difference. \n  Orthostatic hypotension  is a sustained excessive fall in arterial pressure on standing; autonomic neuropathy, medications and volume depletion are potential causes. Vasovagal syncope has an additional reflex pattern of vasodilatation and often bradycardia, and should not be equated with all postural hypotension. \n Acceleration and g-forces \n Positive head-to-foot acceleration promotes blood displacement toward the legs and can impair vision and consciousness; anti-g suits counter dependent pooling. Negative acceleration drives blood toward the head and may cause congestion or visual disturbances. Effects depend on direction, magnitude, duration and individual compensation. \n  Check:  Why do soldiers subtly contract calf muscles while standing at attention? \n  Answer Muscle contractions compress deep leg veins. Venous valves direct blood toward the heart, reducing pooling and helping maintain venous return, CO and cerebral perfusion. \n\n Separate orthostatic hypotension and vasovagal syncope \n  Orthostatic hypotension  is commonly defined as a sustained fall of  at least 20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within three minutes of standing  (standard clinical criterion). Mechanistically, standing pools blood in dependent vessels, reduces preload and challenges cerebral perfusion. An intact baroreflex usually responds with  tachycardia and vasoconstriction ; autonomic failure, certain drugs, dehydration or prolonged pooling can overwhelm compensation. The lecturer's 'postural syncope = reflex bradycardia' should not be taken as the usual response to standing. \n  Vasovagal syncope  often involves inappropriate reflex vasodilatation and/or bradycardia after triggers such as pain, heat, emotion or prolonged standing. It can also cause fainting but has a different autonomic pattern. Returning to the horizontal position restores a more favourable head-to-heart hydrostatic relationship. \n Think in pressure columns \n Arterial and venous pressure at the ankle when standing are higher than at heart level because of hydrostatic load. At the head, the opposite tendency occurs. Dependent capillary hydrostatic pressure rises with prolonged standing, encouraging  ankle oedema .  Leg-muscle isometric contractions  compress deep veins: venous valves channel blood centrally and reduce pooling, an everyday explanation for the soldier-at-attention question. \n Under high positive head-to-foot  +Gz , blood shifts toward legs and cerebral perfusion can fail, producing  grey-out/blackout ; anti-G suits increase external pressure over legs/abdomen. With negative  −Gz , head/neck vascular congestion and 'red-out' symptoms may occur. Magnitude, direction and duration are crucial; the slide thresholds are illustrative rather than guarantees. \n  Practice:  A person is dizzy after standing motionless, but feels better while walking. Walking activates the venous muscle pump and helps maintain venous return. \n"},{"slug":"shock","title":"Circulatory Shock","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf","pages":"12-30; duplicate Lecture 5 pp12-30","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"what-shock-actually-means","title":"What shock actually means"},{"id":"four-mechanistic-categories","title":"Four mechanistic categories"},{"id":"trace-haemorrhagic-shock-in-sequence","title":"Trace haemorrhagic shock in sequence"},{"id":"why-compensation-eventually-fails","title":"Why compensation eventually fails"},{"id":"the-blood-test-trap","title":"The blood test trap"},{"id":"link-individual-signs-to-compensation-and-perfusion","title":"Link individual signs to compensation and perfusion"},{"id":"explain-fluid-shifts-and-the-haemoglobin-trap-over-time","title":"Explain fluid shifts and the haemoglobin trap over time"},{"id":"classify-causes-by-mechanism-not-only-symptom","title":"Classify causes by mechanism, not only symptom"}],"html":"<h1>Circulatory Shock</h1>\n<h2 id=\"what-shock-actually-means\">What shock actually means</h2>\n<p><strong>Shock is inadequate tissue perfusion and oxygen delivery/utilisation relative to metabolic needs</strong>, producing cellular dysfunction. A low measured BP is common in advanced shock but not required early. Compensatory vasoconstriction can maintain MAP temporarily while tissue perfusion remains inadequate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"four-mechanistic-categories\">Four mechanistic categories</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypovolaemic:</strong> reduced effective intravascular volume—haemorrhage, substantial fluid loss, burns.</li>\n<li><strong>Cardiogenic:</strong> inadequate pump performance—large myocardial infarction, severe myocardial dysfunction or arrhythmia.</li>\n<li><strong>Distributive:</strong> pathologically reduced vascular tone and maldistributed flow—sepsis, anaphylaxis, neurogenic causes.</li>\n<li><strong>Obstructive:</strong> mechanical impediment to filling or flow—cardiac tamponade, tension pneumothorax, massive pulmonary embolism.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"trace-haemorrhagic-shock-in-sequence\">Trace haemorrhagic shock in sequence</h2>\n<p>Blood loss → reduced circulating volume → reduced venous return → reduced preload → reduced stroke volume → reduced CO → inadequate tissue oxygen delivery.</p>\n<p>The baroreflex stimulates tachycardia and arteriolar/venous constriction; RAAS and ADH promote retention of fluid. Skin may become cool/pale due to vasoconstriction; renal perfusion may fall, reducing urine output. Respiratory rate may increase. When oxygen delivery is inadequate, anaerobic metabolism contributes to <strong>lactate accumulation and metabolic acidosis</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-compensation-eventually-fails\">Why compensation eventually fails</h2>\n<p>Persistent ischaemia and acidosis impair myocardial contraction and vascular responsiveness, while endothelial/microcirculatory injury can worsen capillary leakage and cellular injury. This creates reinforcing feedback loops toward progressive shock and multiorgan dysfunction.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-blood-test-trap\">The blood test trap</h2>\n<p>Immediately after whole-blood loss, <strong>Hb concentration and haematocrit can remain near initial values</strong>, because plasma and red cells are lost together. Later interstitial fluid entry or fluid treatment dilutes remaining red cells and measured Hb/Hct decrease.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> A patient is tachycardic with cold extremities but near-normal MAP after bleeding. Can they still be in shock?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Yes. Sympathetic vasoconstriction can preserve MAP while CO and tissue oxygen delivery are inadequate. Organ perfusion and clinical context matter more than BP alone.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"link-individual-signs-to-compensation-and-perfusion\">Link individual signs to compensation and perfusion</h2>\n<p>In <strong>early compensated haemorrhage</strong>, sympathetic discharge increases HR, contractility, arteriolar resistance and venous tone. This supports central BP at the expense of reduced cutaneous and sometimes renal perfusion, producing <strong>cool pale skin</strong>, a fast pulse and reduced urine production. Respiratory drive may increase because of stress, acidosis and chemoreceptor signals. In <strong>septic distributive shock</strong>, however, extremities can initially be warm: avoid treating 'cold skin' as a universal sign of every shock category.</p>\n<p>Low cellular oxygen delivery can increase <strong>anaerobic glycolytic flux</strong>, <strong>lactate</strong> and metabolic acidosis. Prolonged hypoperfusion damages vascular endothelium, impairs myocardial performance and weakens responsiveness to constrictor signals, creating <strong>positive feedback</strong> toward worsening shock. 'Irreversible shock' is a descriptive endpoint when injury has advanced beyond successful rescue; exact timing is not predictable from a single diagram.</p>\n<h2 id=\"explain-fluid-shifts-and-the-haemoglobin-trap-over-time\">Explain fluid shifts and the haemoglobin trap over time</h2>\n<p>When whole blood is lost, <strong>RBCs and plasma are removed together</strong>, so Hb concentration and haematocrit may be little changed <strong>immediately</strong>. Over ensuing hours, movement of interstitial fluid into vessels and IV fluids dilute the remaining red cells, lowering measured Hb/Hct. <strong>RAAS, ADH and thirst</strong> help replace circulating volume, while <strong>erythropoietin-driven erythropoiesis</strong> restores red-cell mass over weeks, not immediately. The lecturer's 12–72 hour volume recovery and several-week red-cell recovery are broad illustrative time frames, not promises for severe untreated bleeding.</p>\n<h2 id=\"classify-causes-by-mechanism-not-only-symptom\">Classify causes by mechanism, not only symptom</h2>\n<p>A large <strong>myocardial infarction</strong> reduces pumping ability (cardiogenic); <strong>tamponade, massive pulmonary embolism or tension pneumothorax</strong> obstruct filling/flow (obstructive); <strong>anaphylaxis and severe sepsis</strong> reduce effective vascular tone and can increase capillary leak (distributive); <strong>haemorrhage, burns and severe gastrointestinal fluid loss</strong> reduce effective volume (hypovolaemic). Multiple categories can overlap in one patient.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why is a normal Hb immediately after bleeding not reassuring? It measures <strong>concentration</strong>, which may stay similar when red cells and plasma are lost together; volume and oxygen delivery may already be dangerously reduced.</p>\n","plain":" Circulatory Shock \n What shock actually means \n  Shock is inadequate tissue perfusion and oxygen delivery/utilisation relative to metabolic needs , producing cellular dysfunction. A low measured BP is common in advanced shock but not required early. Compensatory vasoconstriction can maintain MAP temporarily while tissue perfusion remains inadequate. \n Four mechanistic categories \n \n  Hypovolaemic:  reduced effective intravascular volume—haemorrhage, substantial fluid loss, burns. \n  Cardiogenic:  inadequate pump performance—large myocardial infarction, severe myocardial dysfunction or arrhythmia. \n  Distributive:  pathologically reduced vascular tone and maldistributed flow—sepsis, anaphylaxis, neurogenic causes. \n  Obstructive:  mechanical impediment to filling or flow—cardiac tamponade, tension pneumothorax, massive pulmonary embolism. \n \n Trace haemorrhagic shock in sequence \n Blood loss → reduced circulating volume → reduced venous return → reduced preload → reduced stroke volume → reduced CO → inadequate tissue oxygen delivery. \n The baroreflex stimulates tachycardia and arteriolar/venous constriction; RAAS and ADH promote retention of fluid. Skin may become cool/pale due to vasoconstriction; renal perfusion may fall, reducing urine output. Respiratory rate may increase. When oxygen delivery is inadequate, anaerobic metabolism contributes to  lactate accumulation and metabolic acidosis . \n Why compensation eventually fails \n Persistent ischaemia and acidosis impair myocardial contraction and vascular responsiveness, while endothelial/microcirculatory injury can worsen capillary leakage and cellular injury. This creates reinforcing feedback loops toward progressive shock and multiorgan dysfunction. \n The blood test trap \n Immediately after whole-blood loss,  Hb concentration and haematocrit can remain near initial values , because plasma and red cells are lost together. Later interstitial fluid entry or fluid treatment dilutes remaining red cells and measured Hb/Hct decrease. \n  Check:  A patient is tachycardic with cold extremities but near-normal MAP after bleeding. Can they still be in shock? \n  Answer Yes. Sympathetic vasoconstriction can preserve MAP while CO and tissue oxygen delivery are inadequate. Organ perfusion and clinical context matter more than BP alone. \n\n Link individual signs to compensation and perfusion \n In  early compensated haemorrhage , sympathetic discharge increases HR, contractility, arteriolar resistance and venous tone. This supports central BP at the expense of reduced cutaneous and sometimes renal perfusion, producing  cool pale skin , a fast pulse and reduced urine production. Respiratory drive may increase because of stress, acidosis and chemoreceptor signals. In  septic distributive shock , however, extremities can initially be warm: avoid treating 'cold skin' as a universal sign of every shock category. \n Low cellular oxygen delivery can increase  anaerobic glycolytic flux ,  lactate  and metabolic acidosis. Prolonged hypoperfusion damages vascular endothelium, impairs myocardial performance and weakens responsiveness to constrictor signals, creating  positive feedback  toward worsening shock. 'Irreversible shock' is a descriptive endpoint when injury has advanced beyond successful rescue; exact timing is not predictable from a single diagram. \n Explain fluid shifts and the haemoglobin trap over time \n When whole blood is lost,  RBCs and plasma are removed together , so Hb concentration and haematocrit may be little changed  immediately . Over ensuing hours, movement of interstitial fluid into vessels and IV fluids dilute the remaining red cells, lowering measured Hb/Hct.  RAAS, ADH and thirst  help replace circulating volume, while  erythropoietin-driven erythropoiesis  restores red-cell mass over weeks, not immediately. The lecturer's 12–72 hour volume recovery and several-week red-cell recovery are broad illustrative time frames, not promises for severe untreated bleeding. \n Classify causes by mechanism, not only symptom \n A large  myocardial infarction  reduces pumping ability (cardiogenic);  tamponade, massive pulmonary embolism or tension pneumothorax  obstruct filling/flow (obstructive);  anaphylaxis and severe sepsis  reduce effective vascular tone and can increase capillary leak (distributive);  haemorrhage, burns and severe gastrointestinal fluid loss  reduce effective volume (hypovolaemic). Multiple categories can overlap in one patient. \n  Practice:  Why is a normal Hb immediately after bleeding not reassuring? It measures  concentration , which may stay similar when red cells and plasma are lost together; volume and oxygen delivery may already be dangerously reduced. \n"},{"slug":"heart-failure","title":"Heart Failure Physiology","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf","pages":"31-33; duplicate Lecture 5 pp31-33","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"define-the-pump-problem","title":"Define the pump problem"},{"id":"forward-versus-backward-consequences","title":"Forward versus backward consequences"},{"id":"compensation-can-become-harmful","title":"Compensation can become harmful"},{"id":"systolic-and-diastolic-problems","title":"Systolic and diastolic problems"},{"id":"forward-flow-and-backward-congestion","title":"Forward flow and backward congestion"},{"id":"what-compensates-and-why-compensation-can-harm","title":"What compensates and why compensation can harm"}],"html":"<h1>Heart Failure Physiology</h1>\n<h2 id=\"define-the-pump-problem\">Define the pump problem</h2>\n<p>Heart failure is a clinical syndrome in which cardiac dysfunction produces inadequate forward output and/or elevated intracardiac filling pressures, with characteristic symptoms and signs. Output may be normal at rest in some forms, so it should not be defined only as low cardiac output.</p>\n<h2 id=\"forward-versus-backward-consequences\">Forward versus backward consequences</h2>\n<p><strong>Forward consequences</strong> relate to inadequate organ perfusion (fatigue, poor exercise tolerance, reduced renal blood flow). <strong>Backward consequences</strong> reflect elevated pressures upstream of the failing chamber: left-heart dysfunction can raise pulmonary venous/capillary pressure, causing breathlessness and pulmonary oedema; right-heart dysfunction can raise systemic venous pressure, producing elevated JVP, hepatic congestion, ascites and peripheral oedema.</p>\n<h2 id=\"compensation-can-become-harmful\">Compensation can become harmful</h2>\n<p>Reduced effective arterial perfusion activates sympathetic output, <strong>RAAS</strong> and ADH. These initially defend pressure and perfusion, but chronic tachycardia, vasoconstriction and sodium/water retention can increase afterload and congestion. Ventricular remodelling may further impair efficiency.</p>\n<h2 id=\"systolic-and-diastolic-problems\">Systolic and diastolic problems</h2>\n<p>In <strong>systolic dysfunction</strong>, ventricular ejection is impaired and EF may be reduced. In <strong>diastolic dysfunction</strong>, filling is impaired or requires higher pressure because relaxation/compliance are abnormal; EF may be preserved. Both can cause pulmonary congestion.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why can a patient with heart failure be oedematous even while kidneys behave as though the body lacks volume?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Kidneys may sense reduced effective arterial perfusion and retain sodium/water, despite excess total extracellular fluid and high venous filling pressures. This worsens congestion.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"forward-flow-and-backward-congestion\">Forward flow and backward congestion</h2>\n<p><strong>Forward failure</strong> emphasises insufficient effective cardiac output: fatigue, reduced exercise capacity, renal hypoperfusion and in severe cases reduced cerebral perfusion. <strong>Backward failure</strong> emphasises increased filling pressures and venous congestion: left-sided dysfunction often raises pulmonary venous pressure, causing dyspnoea, orthopnoea and pulmonary oedema; right-sided dysfunction often raises systemic venous pressure, causing raised JVP, dependent oedema, hepatic congestion and ascites.</p>\n<p>These are explanatory perspectives, <strong>not four mutually exclusive diseases</strong>. Left and right failure can coexist, and left-sided disease frequently contributes to right-sided dysfunction. Right-sided failure does not simply imply lungs receive no blood; changes in pulmonary flow and systemic venous pressures depend on the underlying problem.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-compensates-and-why-compensation-can-harm\">What compensates and why compensation can harm</h2>\n<p>Reduced forward output activates <strong>sympathetic nerves, RAAS and ADH</strong>. Initially increased HR, contractility and volume may preserve perfusion, but excessive vasoconstriction raises afterload and retained water/sodium worsen congestion. Frank–Starling can help compensate early, but a failing ventricular function curve gives diminishing benefit as filling pressure rises. Heart failure can occur with reduced ejection fraction or <strong>preserved ejection fraction when filling/relaxation is abnormal</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why might a patient with left-sided heart failure become breathless when lying flat? Recumbency redistributes blood centrally, raising pulmonary venous pressure when the left ventricle cannot accommodate/eject adequately.</p>\n","plain":" Heart Failure Physiology \n Define the pump problem \n Heart failure is a clinical syndrome in which cardiac dysfunction produces inadequate forward output and/or elevated intracardiac filling pressures, with characteristic symptoms and signs. Output may be normal at rest in some forms, so it should not be defined only as low cardiac output. \n Forward versus backward consequences \n  Forward consequences  relate to inadequate organ perfusion (fatigue, poor exercise tolerance, reduced renal blood flow).  Backward consequences  reflect elevated pressures upstream of the failing chamber: left-heart dysfunction can raise pulmonary venous/capillary pressure, causing breathlessness and pulmonary oedema; right-heart dysfunction can raise systemic venous pressure, producing elevated JVP, hepatic congestion, ascites and peripheral oedema. \n Compensation can become harmful \n Reduced effective arterial perfusion activates sympathetic output,  RAAS  and ADH. These initially defend pressure and perfusion, but chronic tachycardia, vasoconstriction and sodium/water retention can increase afterload and congestion. Ventricular remodelling may further impair efficiency. \n Systolic and diastolic problems \n In  systolic dysfunction , ventricular ejection is impaired and EF may be reduced. In  diastolic dysfunction , filling is impaired or requires higher pressure because relaxation/compliance are abnormal; EF may be preserved. Both can cause pulmonary congestion. \n  Check:  Why can a patient with heart failure be oedematous even while kidneys behave as though the body lacks volume? \n  Answer Kidneys may sense reduced effective arterial perfusion and retain sodium/water, despite excess total extracellular fluid and high venous filling pressures. This worsens congestion. \n\n Forward flow and backward congestion \n  Forward failure  emphasises insufficient effective cardiac output: fatigue, reduced exercise capacity, renal hypoperfusion and in severe cases reduced cerebral perfusion.  Backward failure  emphasises increased filling pressures and venous congestion: left-sided dysfunction often raises pulmonary venous pressure, causing dyspnoea, orthopnoea and pulmonary oedema; right-sided dysfunction often raises systemic venous pressure, causing raised JVP, dependent oedema, hepatic congestion and ascites. \n These are explanatory perspectives,  not four mutually exclusive diseases . Left and right failure can coexist, and left-sided disease frequently contributes to right-sided dysfunction. Right-sided failure does not simply imply lungs receive no blood; changes in pulmonary flow and systemic venous pressures depend on the underlying problem. \n What compensates and why compensation can harm \n Reduced forward output activates  sympathetic nerves, RAAS and ADH . Initially increased HR, contractility and volume may preserve perfusion, but excessive vasoconstriction raises afterload and retained water/sodium worsen congestion. Frank–Starling can help compensate early, but a failing ventricular function curve gives diminishing benefit as filling pressure rises. Heart failure can occur with reduced ejection fraction or  preserved ejection fraction when filling/relaxation is abnormal . \n  Practice:  Why might a patient with left-sided heart failure become breathless when lying flat? Recumbency redistributes blood centrally, raising pulmonary venous pressure when the left ventricle cannot accommodate/eject adequately. \n"},{"slug":"cerebral","title":"Cerebral Circulation and CSF","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"2-25; extra diagrams Circulation 3 39-41","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"supply-and-flow-regulation","title":"Supply and flow regulation"},{"id":"intracranial-pressure-and-the-monro-kellie-principle","title":"Intracranial pressure and the Monro–Kellie principle"},{"id":"csf-path-and-functions","title":"CSF path and functions"},{"id":"blood-brain-barrier-bbb","title":"Blood–brain barrier (BBB)"},{"id":"identify-the-arterial-supplies-and-vulnerability","title":"Identify the arterial supplies and vulnerability"},{"id":"meninges-csf-and-pressure","title":"Meninges, CSF and pressure"},{"id":"cerebral-blood-flow-control","title":"Cerebral blood flow control"}],"html":"<h1>Cerebral Circulation, CSF and the Blood–Brain Barrier</h1>\n<h2 id=\"supply-and-flow-regulation\">Supply and flow regulation</h2>\n<p>The <strong>internal carotid</strong> and <strong>vertebrobasilar</strong> systems supply the brain and connect through arteries of the <strong>Circle of Willis</strong>, offering potential collateral routes; the circle is variable and does not guarantee protection from stroke. Cerebral perfusion depends on driving pressure and vascular resistance. <strong>Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) ≈ MAP − ICP</strong> when intracranial pressure exceeds venous outflow pressure.</p>\n<p>Cerebral vessels autoregulate over a range of pressures. Increased arterial CO₂ is a potent cerebral vasodilator; marked hypocapnia constricts cerebral arterioles. Severe hypoxia can also cause vasodilatation. Blood flow is adjusted regionally to neural activity.</p>\n<h2 id=\"intracranial-pressure-and-the-monro-kellie-principle\">Intracranial pressure and the Monro–Kellie principle</h2>\n<p>Within the largely fixed adult skull, brain tissue, blood and CSF share a limited volume. Added volume can initially be offset by shifting CSF/venous blood, but beyond compensation <strong>ICP rises sharply</strong>, potentially reducing CPP and causing the Cushing response.</p>\n<h2 id=\"csf-path-and-functions\">CSF path and functions</h2>\n<p>CSF is produced mainly by <strong>choroid plexus</strong>, flows from lateral ventricles through interventricular foramina (Monro) to third ventricle → cerebral aqueduct → fourth ventricle → median/lateral apertures → subarachnoid space, and returns ultimately to venous/lymphatic drainage pathways. Typical adult CSF volume is about <strong>150 mL</strong>. It provides buoyancy and cushioning and supports CNS waste/solute handling.</p>\n<p><strong>Hydrocephalus</strong> may result from impaired flow or absorption. The subarachnoid space normally contains CSF; the subdural space is a <strong>potential</strong>, not normally CSF-filled, space.</p>\n<h2 id=\"blood-brain-barrier-bbb\">Blood–brain barrier (BBB)</h2>\n<p>Specialised brain capillary <strong>endothelial tight junctions</strong>, transporters and neurovascular unit limit uncontrolled solute passage. Glucose enters through transporters (not simply free diffusion), while many polar compounds require specialised transport. BBB integrity is important for neuronal extracellular stability and drug access.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why can rising ICP cause brain ischaemia even when measured arterial BP appears normal?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>CPP is approximately MAP minus ICP. A rise in ICP reduces the pressure gradient driving cerebral blood flow despite unchanged systemic MAP.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"identify-the-arterial-supplies-and-vulnerability\">Identify the arterial supplies and vulnerability</h2>\n<p>The <strong>anterior, middle and posterior cerebral arteries</strong> supply different territories. The two internal carotids and vertebrobasilar system connect variably in the <strong>Circle of Willis</strong>, potentially supporting collateral supply; the middle cerebral artery is a large terminal branch but <strong>not itself part of the arterial circle</strong>. The actual territories and clinical consequences of occlusion vary; the lecturer includes <strong>ischaemic</strong> (thrombotic/embolic) and <strong>haemorrhagic</strong> stroke as broad categories.</p>\n<h2 id=\"meninges-csf-and-pressure\">Meninges, CSF and pressure</h2>\n<p>From outside inward: <strong>dura mater</strong>, <strong>arachnoid mater</strong>, <strong>subarachnoid space containing CSF</strong>, then <strong>pia mater</strong> adhering to brain/spinal cord. The normal subdural space is only potential. CSF is formed mainly in the ventricular choroid plexuses; normal adult total volume is about <strong>150 mL</strong>, with production several hundred mL/day. Flow proceeds lateral ventricles → <strong>foramina of Monro</strong> → third ventricle → <strong>aqueduct of Sylvius</strong> → fourth ventricle → median (Magendie) and lateral (Luschka) apertures → subarachnoid space. CSF ultimately leaves through arachnoid and lymphatic outflow pathways. <strong>Communicating hydrocephalus</strong> generally has impaired CSF handling beyond the ventricular pathway; <strong>noncommunicating hydrocephalus</strong> has an obstruction within it.</p>\n<p>CSF protects via <strong>buoyancy and cushioning</strong> and supports interstitial solute clearance. <strong>Lumbar puncture</strong> samples subarachnoid CSF below the usual adult spinal cord termination; do not infer puncture is risk-free when raised ICP or mass effect is suspected.</p>\n<h2 id=\"cerebral-blood-flow-control\">Cerebral blood flow control</h2>\n<p><strong>CPP ≈ MAP − ICP</strong> in the common approximation, and high ICP compresses intracranial vessels. Local vessels respond strongly to <strong>CO₂</strong>: rising CO₂ usually dilates and falling CO₂ constricts cerebral arterioles. Brain tissue normally relies heavily on continuous glucose/oxygen delivery, using glucose transport across the BBB and alternative fuels such as <strong>ketone bodies during prolonged starvation</strong>; the slides' suggestion of freely substituting fatty acids is not a general statement of normal brain metabolism.</p>\n<p>The <strong>BBB</strong> depends on continuous nonfenestrated endothelial cells with tight junctions, specialised transporters and associated cells. Glucose uses <strong>GLUT1</strong>, and lipid-soluble gases diffuse comparatively readily. The BBB is <strong>not</strong> the same as the blood–CSF barrier at the choroid plexus.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> A brain tumour increases cranial volume beyond compensatory shifts. ICP rises, CPP can fall, and brain perfusion may worsen even if peripheral BP is unchanged.</p>\n","plain":" Cerebral Circulation, CSF and the Blood–Brain Barrier \n Supply and flow regulation \n The  internal carotid  and  vertebrobasilar  systems supply the brain and connect through arteries of the  Circle of Willis , offering potential collateral routes; the circle is variable and does not guarantee protection from stroke. Cerebral perfusion depends on driving pressure and vascular resistance.  Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) ≈ MAP − ICP  when intracranial pressure exceeds venous outflow pressure. \n Cerebral vessels autoregulate over a range of pressures. Increased arterial CO₂ is a potent cerebral vasodilator; marked hypocapnia constricts cerebral arterioles. Severe hypoxia can also cause vasodilatation. Blood flow is adjusted regionally to neural activity. \n Intracranial pressure and the Monro–Kellie principle \n Within the largely fixed adult skull, brain tissue, blood and CSF share a limited volume. Added volume can initially be offset by shifting CSF/venous blood, but beyond compensation  ICP rises sharply , potentially reducing CPP and causing the Cushing response. \n CSF path and functions \n CSF is produced mainly by  choroid plexus , flows from lateral ventricles through interventricular foramina (Monro) to third ventricle → cerebral aqueduct → fourth ventricle → median/lateral apertures → subarachnoid space, and returns ultimately to venous/lymphatic drainage pathways. Typical adult CSF volume is about  150 mL . It provides buoyancy and cushioning and supports CNS waste/solute handling. \n  Hydrocephalus  may result from impaired flow or absorption. The subarachnoid space normally contains CSF; the subdural space is a  potential , not normally CSF-filled, space. \n Blood–brain barrier (BBB) \n Specialised brain capillary  endothelial tight junctions , transporters and neurovascular unit limit uncontrolled solute passage. Glucose enters through transporters (not simply free diffusion), while many polar compounds require specialised transport. BBB integrity is important for neuronal extracellular stability and drug access. \n  Check:  Why can rising ICP cause brain ischaemia even when measured arterial BP appears normal? \n  Answer CPP is approximately MAP minus ICP. A rise in ICP reduces the pressure gradient driving cerebral blood flow despite unchanged systemic MAP. \n\n Identify the arterial supplies and vulnerability \n The  anterior, middle and posterior cerebral arteries  supply different territories. The two internal carotids and vertebrobasilar system connect variably in the  Circle of Willis , potentially supporting collateral supply; the middle cerebral artery is a large terminal branch but  not itself part of the arterial circle . The actual territories and clinical consequences of occlusion vary; the lecturer includes  ischaemic  (thrombotic/embolic) and  haemorrhagic  stroke as broad categories. \n Meninges, CSF and pressure \n From outside inward:  dura mater ,  arachnoid mater ,  subarachnoid space containing CSF , then  pia mater  adhering to brain/spinal cord. The normal subdural space is only potential. CSF is formed mainly in the ventricular choroid plexuses; normal adult total volume is about  150 mL , with production several hundred mL/day. Flow proceeds lateral ventricles →  foramina of Monro  → third ventricle →  aqueduct of Sylvius  → fourth ventricle → median (Magendie) and lateral (Luschka) apertures → subarachnoid space. CSF ultimately leaves through arachnoid and lymphatic outflow pathways.  Communicating hydrocephalus  generally has impaired CSF handling beyond the ventricular pathway;  noncommunicating hydrocephalus  has an obstruction within it. \n CSF protects via  buoyancy and cushioning  and supports interstitial solute clearance.  Lumbar puncture  samples subarachnoid CSF below the usual adult spinal cord termination; do not infer puncture is risk-free when raised ICP or mass effect is suspected. \n Cerebral blood flow control \n  CPP ≈ MAP − ICP  in the common approximation, and high ICP compresses intracranial vessels. Local vessels respond strongly to  CO₂ : rising CO₂ usually dilates and falling CO₂ constricts cerebral arterioles. Brain tissue normally relies heavily on continuous glucose/oxygen delivery, using glucose transport across the BBB and alternative fuels such as  ketone bodies during prolonged starvation ; the slides' suggestion of freely substituting fatty acids is not a general statement of normal brain metabolism. \n The  BBB  depends on continuous nonfenestrated endothelial cells with tight junctions, specialised transporters and associated cells. Glucose uses  GLUT1 , and lipid-soluble gases diffuse comparatively readily. The BBB is  not  the same as the blood–CSF barrier at the choroid plexus. \n  Practice:  A brain tumour increases cranial volume beyond compensatory shifts. ICP rises, CPP can fall, and brain perfusion may worsen even if peripheral BP is unchanged. \n"},{"slug":"coronary","title":"Coronary Circulation","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"26-29; extra diagrams Circulation 3 42-43","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"the-myocardium-needs-its-own-perfusion","title":"The myocardium needs its own perfusion"},{"id":"why-left-coronary-flow-favours-diastole","title":"Why left coronary flow favours diastole"},{"id":"strong-metabolic-regulation","title":"Strong metabolic regulation"},{"id":"ischaemia-angina-and-infarction","title":"Ischaemia, angina and infarction"},{"id":"routes-and-drainage","title":"Routes and drainage"},{"id":"why-oxygen-delivery-relies-on-more-flow","title":"Why oxygen delivery relies on more flow"}],"html":"<h1>Coronary Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"the-myocardium-needs-its-own-perfusion\">The myocardium needs its own perfusion</h2>\n<p>The <strong>right and left coronary arteries</strong> originate from the aortic root and supply the cardiac muscle. The majority of venous drainage reaches the right atrium through the <strong>coronary sinus</strong>; additional veins may drain directly into chambers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-left-coronary-flow-favours-diastole\">Why left coronary flow favours diastole</h2>\n<p>During systole, high left-ventricular wall tension compresses intramyocardial coronary vessels, especially in the subendocardium. As ventricular pressure falls during <strong>diastole</strong>, coronary resistance decreases and perfusion increases. Therefore markedly shortened diastole in tachycardia may threaten myocardial oxygen supply even as oxygen demand rises.</p>\n<h2 id=\"strong-metabolic-regulation\">Strong metabolic regulation</h2>\n<p>Myocardium already extracts a high fraction of delivered oxygen at rest. When its demand increases, oxygen supply is raised mainly by <strong>increased coronary blood flow</strong>. Local dilators include <strong>adenosine, NO</strong> and metabolites associated with increased activity. Direct sympathetic α vasoconstriction may occur, but metabolic vasodilatation usually dominates during increased cardiac work.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ischaemia-angina-and-infarction\">Ischaemia, angina and infarction</h2>\n<p>A narrowed coronary artery may provide adequate resting flow but insufficient reserve on exercise, producing <strong>angina</strong>. Sustained severe interruption of coronary perfusion can cause <strong>myocardial infarction</strong>, with irreversible myocyte injury. Coronary spasm, thrombosis and plaque complications alter flow.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why might extreme tachycardia simultaneously increase myocardial oxygen demand and reduce supply?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>More rapid/forceful work raises demand, while marked shortening of diastole reduces the time when left coronary perfusion is greatest.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"routes-and-drainage\">Routes and drainage</h2>\n<p>The <strong>left coronary artery</strong> typically divides into <strong>left anterior descending (anterior interventricular)</strong> and <strong>circumflex</strong> branches; the <strong>right coronary artery</strong> supplies right-sided branches and often the posterior descending artery, depending on coronary dominance. Dominance is defined by the origin of the <strong>posterior descending artery</strong>, not simply by which coronary artery looks bigger. Cardiac venous blood returns mainly through the <strong>coronary sinus</strong> to the right atrium, with some anterior and smallest cardiac veins draining separately.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-oxygen-delivery-relies-on-more-flow\">Why oxygen delivery relies on more flow</h2>\n<p>Myocardial oxygen extraction is relatively high even at rest. When heart rate, contractility or wall stress increases oxygen requirements, the principal normal adjustment is <strong>increased coronary blood flow</strong>. Local metabolites including <strong>adenosine</strong>, changes in oxygen tension, potassium and endothelial <strong>NO</strong> help vasodilate coronary resistance vessels. Sympathetic α receptors may constrict directly, while β₁ stimulation of the heart raises metabolism and typically causes <strong>secondary metabolic coronary dilation</strong>; the lecturer's wording that β₁-generated metabolites constrict coronary arteries is inaccurate.</p>\n<p>In the <strong>left ventricle</strong>, intramyocardial vessels are compressed during systole, so much flow occurs in <strong>diastole</strong>. Extreme tachycardia shortens diastole and simultaneously increases oxygen demand. A narrowed coronary artery may supply adequate flow at rest yet fail during exercise (<strong>demand ischaemia/angina</strong>); sustained inadequate oxygen delivery can injure myocytes (<strong>infarction</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why can severe aortic stenosis increase risk of myocardial ischaemia? High ventricular pressure raises wall stress and oxygen demand; altered pressure relations may also compromise subendocardial perfusion.</p>\n","plain":" Coronary Circulation \n The myocardium needs its own perfusion \n The  right and left coronary arteries  originate from the aortic root and supply the cardiac muscle. The majority of venous drainage reaches the right atrium through the  coronary sinus ; additional veins may drain directly into chambers. \n Why left coronary flow favours diastole \n During systole, high left-ventricular wall tension compresses intramyocardial coronary vessels, especially in the subendocardium. As ventricular pressure falls during  diastole , coronary resistance decreases and perfusion increases. Therefore markedly shortened diastole in tachycardia may threaten myocardial oxygen supply even as oxygen demand rises. \n Strong metabolic regulation \n Myocardium already extracts a high fraction of delivered oxygen at rest. When its demand increases, oxygen supply is raised mainly by  increased coronary blood flow . Local dilators include  adenosine, NO  and metabolites associated with increased activity. Direct sympathetic α vasoconstriction may occur, but metabolic vasodilatation usually dominates during increased cardiac work. \n Ischaemia, angina and infarction \n A narrowed coronary artery may provide adequate resting flow but insufficient reserve on exercise, producing  angina . Sustained severe interruption of coronary perfusion can cause  myocardial infarction , with irreversible myocyte injury. Coronary spasm, thrombosis and plaque complications alter flow. \n  Check:  Why might extreme tachycardia simultaneously increase myocardial oxygen demand and reduce supply? \n  Answer More rapid/forceful work raises demand, while marked shortening of diastole reduces the time when left coronary perfusion is greatest. \n\n Routes and drainage \n The  left coronary artery  typically divides into  left anterior descending (anterior interventricular)  and  circumflex  branches; the  right coronary artery  supplies right-sided branches and often the posterior descending artery, depending on coronary dominance. Dominance is defined by the origin of the  posterior descending artery , not simply by which coronary artery looks bigger. Cardiac venous blood returns mainly through the  coronary sinus  to the right atrium, with some anterior and smallest cardiac veins draining separately. \n Why oxygen delivery relies on more flow \n Myocardial oxygen extraction is relatively high even at rest. When heart rate, contractility or wall stress increases oxygen requirements, the principal normal adjustment is  increased coronary blood flow . Local metabolites including  adenosine , changes in oxygen tension, potassium and endothelial  NO  help vasodilate coronary resistance vessels. Sympathetic α receptors may constrict directly, while β₁ stimulation of the heart raises metabolism and typically causes  secondary metabolic coronary dilation ; the lecturer's wording that β₁-generated metabolites constrict coronary arteries is inaccurate. \n In the  left ventricle , intramyocardial vessels are compressed during systole, so much flow occurs in  diastole . Extreme tachycardia shortens diastole and simultaneously increases oxygen demand. A narrowed coronary artery may supply adequate flow at rest yet fail during exercise ( demand ischaemia/angina ); sustained inadequate oxygen delivery can injure myocytes ( infarction ). \n  Practice:  Why can severe aortic stenosis increase risk of myocardial ischaemia? High ventricular pressure raises wall stress and oxygen demand; altered pressure relations may also compromise subendocardial perfusion. \n"},{"slug":"pulmonary","title":"Pulmonary Circulation","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"30-32","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"one-flow-different-pressures","title":"One flow, different pressures"},{"id":"gravity-and-distribution","title":"Gravity and distribution"},{"id":"the-unusual-response-to-low-oxygen","title":"The unusual response to low oxygen"},{"id":"oedema-prevention","title":"Oedema prevention"},{"id":"pulmonary-vs-systemic-circuits","title":"Pulmonary vs systemic circuits"},{"id":"gravity-and-vascular-recruitment","title":"Gravity and vascular recruitment"},{"id":"contrast-hypoxic-responses-in-two-circulations","title":"Contrast hypoxic responses in two circulations"}],"html":"<h1>Pulmonary Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"one-flow-different-pressures\">One flow, different pressures</h2>\n<p>The right ventricle delivers approximately the same minute output as the left ventricle, but the pulmonary circuit normally has <strong>far lower vascular resistance and pressure</strong>. Its thin vessels and capillaries permit gas exchange without excessive filtration. The <strong>bronchial circulation</strong> from systemic arteries supplies supporting lung tissues in addition to the low-pressure pulmonary circulation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gravity-and-distribution\">Gravity and distribution</h2>\n<p>In an upright person, gravity changes hydrostatic pressures across lung regions. Regional blood flow varies from apex to base as vascular pressures, alveolar pressure and recruitment/distension vary; this is often conceptualized with <strong>West lung zones</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-unusual-response-to-low-oxygen\">The unusual response to low oxygen</h2>\n<p>Systemic resistance vessels commonly dilate in metabolically hypoxic tissue, but <strong>small pulmonary arteries constrict in response to low alveolar PO₂</strong>. This diverts blood toward better-ventilated alveoli and can improve ventilation–perfusion matching. Diffuse chronic alveolar hypoxia, however, can raise pulmonary vascular resistance and contribute to pulmonary hypertension.</p>\n<h2 id=\"oedema-prevention\">Oedema prevention</h2>\n<p>Normally low pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure and efficient lymphatic drainage oppose fluid accumulation in alveoli. Raised left atrial/pulmonary venous pressure in left-heart failure increases filtration and may produce pulmonary oedema.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why is hypoxic vasoconstriction useful locally in the lungs but harmful when it affects the entire lung?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Locally it diverts blood to better-ventilated regions. Widespread hypoxia constricts many pulmonary vessels, increasing resistance and pulmonary arterial pressure.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"pulmonary-vs-systemic-circuits\">Pulmonary vs systemic circuits</h2>\n<p>Both receive nearly the same cardiac output, yet normal pulmonary arterial pressures are much lower because pulmonary vascular resistance is low. The lung has dual circulations: <strong>pulmonary vessels</strong> for gas exchange and <strong>bronchial arteries</strong> (from the systemic aortic circulation) nourishing supporting tissues. Part of bronchial venous drainage enters pulmonary veins, contributing to normal physiological venous admixture.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gravity-and-vascular-recruitment\">Gravity and vascular recruitment</h2>\n<p>In an upright lung, blood flow tends to be greater toward dependent (basal) regions because of hydrostatic pressure and vascular recruitment/distension. The classic <strong>West lung zones</strong> compare pulmonary arterial, alveolar and venous pressures; zone relationships shift with posture, lung pressure and disease. At rest pulmonary capillaries normally have low hydrostatic pressure and barrier/lymphatic protection against alveolar flooding, although pulmonary oedema can develop when those safeguards fail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"contrast-hypoxic-responses-in-two-circulations\">Contrast hypoxic responses in two circulations</h2>\n<p>When an alveolar region is poorly oxygenated, nearby pulmonary arterioles constrict (<strong>hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction</strong>) to redirect blood toward better-ventilated alveoli. This is the opposite of the typical <strong>systemic local hypoxic dilation</strong> that boosts oxygen delivery to active tissues. Diffuse sustained alveolar hypoxia can raise pulmonary vascular resistance and eventually cause pulmonary hypertension/right-ventricular strain.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why is high-altitude hypoxia capable of raising pulmonary pressure? Global alveolar hypoxia stimulates widespread pulmonary arteriolar constriction instead of merely diverting flow within one small region.</p>\n","plain":" Pulmonary Circulation \n One flow, different pressures \n The right ventricle delivers approximately the same minute output as the left ventricle, but the pulmonary circuit normally has  far lower vascular resistance and pressure . Its thin vessels and capillaries permit gas exchange without excessive filtration. The  bronchial circulation  from systemic arteries supplies supporting lung tissues in addition to the low-pressure pulmonary circulation. \n Gravity and distribution \n In an upright person, gravity changes hydrostatic pressures across lung regions. Regional blood flow varies from apex to base as vascular pressures, alveolar pressure and recruitment/distension vary; this is often conceptualized with  West lung zones . \n The unusual response to low oxygen \n Systemic resistance vessels commonly dilate in metabolically hypoxic tissue, but  small pulmonary arteries constrict in response to low alveolar PO₂ . This diverts blood toward better-ventilated alveoli and can improve ventilation–perfusion matching. Diffuse chronic alveolar hypoxia, however, can raise pulmonary vascular resistance and contribute to pulmonary hypertension. \n Oedema prevention \n Normally low pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure and efficient lymphatic drainage oppose fluid accumulation in alveoli. Raised left atrial/pulmonary venous pressure in left-heart failure increases filtration and may produce pulmonary oedema. \n  Check:  Why is hypoxic vasoconstriction useful locally in the lungs but harmful when it affects the entire lung? \n  Answer Locally it diverts blood to better-ventilated regions. Widespread hypoxia constricts many pulmonary vessels, increasing resistance and pulmonary arterial pressure. \n\n Pulmonary vs systemic circuits \n Both receive nearly the same cardiac output, yet normal pulmonary arterial pressures are much lower because pulmonary vascular resistance is low. The lung has dual circulations:  pulmonary vessels  for gas exchange and  bronchial arteries  (from the systemic aortic circulation) nourishing supporting tissues. Part of bronchial venous drainage enters pulmonary veins, contributing to normal physiological venous admixture. \n Gravity and vascular recruitment \n In an upright lung, blood flow tends to be greater toward dependent (basal) regions because of hydrostatic pressure and vascular recruitment/distension. The classic  West lung zones  compare pulmonary arterial, alveolar and venous pressures; zone relationships shift with posture, lung pressure and disease. At rest pulmonary capillaries normally have low hydrostatic pressure and barrier/lymphatic protection against alveolar flooding, although pulmonary oedema can develop when those safeguards fail. \n Contrast hypoxic responses in two circulations \n When an alveolar region is poorly oxygenated, nearby pulmonary arterioles constrict ( hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction ) to redirect blood toward better-ventilated alveoli. This is the opposite of the typical  systemic local hypoxic dilation  that boosts oxygen delivery to active tissues. Diffuse sustained alveolar hypoxia can raise pulmonary vascular resistance and eventually cause pulmonary hypertension/right-ventricular strain. \n  Practice:  Why is high-altitude hypoxia capable of raising pulmonary pressure? Global alveolar hypoxia stimulates widespread pulmonary arteriolar constriction instead of merely diverting flow within one small region. \n"},{"slug":"splanchnic","title":"Splanchnic and Hepatic Circulation","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"33-37","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"an-unusual-route-through-two-capillary-networks","title":"An unusual route through two capillary networks"},{"id":"hepatic-microcirculation","title":"Hepatic microcirculation"},{"id":"blood-flow-after-meals-and-in-shock","title":"Blood flow after meals and in shock"},{"id":"ascites","title":"Ascites"},{"id":"two-inflows-and-the-hepatic-microvascular-route","title":"Two inflows and the hepatic microvascular route"},{"id":"digestion-shock-and-venous-storage","title":"Digestion, shock and venous storage"},{"id":"explain-ascites-without-one-misleading-protein-story","title":"Explain ascites without one misleading protein story"}],"html":"<h1>Splanchnic and Hepatic Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"an-unusual-route-through-two-capillary-networks\">An unusual route through two capillary networks</h2>\n<p>The abdominal organs receive arterial blood mainly through the <strong>coeliac trunk</strong>, <strong>superior mesenteric artery</strong> and <strong>inferior mesenteric artery</strong>. Venous blood from much of the gut passes through the <strong>portal vein</strong> to liver sinusoids before draining through hepatic veins to the inferior vena cava. Thus, absorbed nutrients are delivered to the liver before much systemic distribution.</p>\n<h2 id=\"hepatic-microcirculation\">Hepatic microcirculation</h2>\n<p>Branches of portal vein and hepatic artery enter near portal tracts, blood mixes in liver <strong>sinusoids</strong>, and drains toward <strong>central veins</strong>. Zone 1 hepatocytes near inflow have greater oxygen availability, while <strong>zone 3</strong> hepatocytes near central veins are more vulnerable to hypoxic damage. Hepatocytes receive oxygen from both portal and arterial sources.</p>\n<h2 id=\"blood-flow-after-meals-and-in-shock\">Blood flow after meals and in shock</h2>\n<p>Intestinal metabolic activity increases after feeding, promoting splanchnic blood flow. This region contains a sizeable venous reservoir. During haemorrhage, sympathetic constriction can mobilize splanchnic blood and reduce nonessential regional flow, although severe or prolonged hypoperfusion risks intestinal ischaemia.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ascites\">Ascites</h2>\n<p>Portal hypertension can increase hydrostatic pressure in abdominal microvessels and hepatic sinusoids. Combined with renal sodium retention, lymph production and often low plasma albumin, this favours <strong>ascites</strong>, accumulation of fluid in the peritoneal cavity. The mechanism is multifactorial; increased protein concentration in all ascitic fluid should not be assumed.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why can severe shock injure the gut even while blood pressure is defended?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Sympathetic redistribution reduces splanchnic perfusion to favour vital organs. Prolonged local hypoperfusion can cause mucosal ischaemia despite partly preserved systemic pressure.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"two-inflows-and-the-hepatic-microvascular-route\">Two inflows and the hepatic microvascular route</h2>\n<p>Arteries from the <strong>coeliac trunk, superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric arteries</strong> supply abdominal organs; portal venous blood collects nutrients and metabolites from the gut/spleen before delivering them to the liver. The liver receives blood through <strong>portal vein and hepatic artery</strong> branches, which mix in sinusoids and drain to <strong>central veins → hepatic veins → IVC</strong>. The <strong>portal triad</strong> contains a portal venule, hepatic arteriole and bile ductule. Bile generally travels in a direction opposing sinusoidal blood drainage.</p>\n<p>In the classical lobule, periportal hepatocytes are closer to the portal triad and receive more oxygenated inflow; <strong>centrilobular (zone 3)</strong> hepatocytes near terminal hepatic venules have relatively lower oxygen availability and are especially vulnerable to some ischaemic insults. This is why the lecturer emphasises oxygen gradients within liver acini.</p>\n<h2 id=\"digestion-shock-and-venous-storage\">Digestion, shock and venous storage</h2>\n<p>Following a meal, intestinal metabolic activity increases <strong>splanchnic flow</strong> (postprandial hyperaemia). The splanchnic venous bed is a substantial <strong>blood reservoir</strong>. During marked sympathetic activation in haemorrhage, splanchnic vasoconstriction shifts blood centrally and reduces gut perfusion; prolonged severe underperfusion can injure the bowel.</p>\n<h2 id=\"explain-ascites-without-one-misleading-protein-story\">Explain ascites without one misleading protein story</h2>\n<p><strong>Ascites</strong> is fluid accumulated within the peritoneal cavity. Portal hypertension increases sinusoidal hydrostatic pressure and hepatic lymph formation; when lymphatic drainage is exceeded, fluid can accumulate in the abdomen. In cirrhosis, sodium/water retention and lowered serum albumin often contribute. The composition and protein concentration of ascitic fluid vary with the underlying cause; high-protein fluid is <strong>not universal</strong> in all ascites.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why is zone 3 particularly sensitive to low oxygen supply? It lies downstream in the sinusoidal pathway and receives blood with less remaining oxygen.</p>\n","plain":" Splanchnic and Hepatic Circulation \n An unusual route through two capillary networks \n The abdominal organs receive arterial blood mainly through the  coeliac trunk ,  superior mesenteric artery  and  inferior mesenteric artery . Venous blood from much of the gut passes through the  portal vein  to liver sinusoids before draining through hepatic veins to the inferior vena cava. Thus, absorbed nutrients are delivered to the liver before much systemic distribution. \n Hepatic microcirculation \n Branches of portal vein and hepatic artery enter near portal tracts, blood mixes in liver  sinusoids , and drains toward  central veins . Zone 1 hepatocytes near inflow have greater oxygen availability, while  zone 3  hepatocytes near central veins are more vulnerable to hypoxic damage. Hepatocytes receive oxygen from both portal and arterial sources. \n Blood flow after meals and in shock \n Intestinal metabolic activity increases after feeding, promoting splanchnic blood flow. This region contains a sizeable venous reservoir. During haemorrhage, sympathetic constriction can mobilize splanchnic blood and reduce nonessential regional flow, although severe or prolonged hypoperfusion risks intestinal ischaemia. \n Ascites \n Portal hypertension can increase hydrostatic pressure in abdominal microvessels and hepatic sinusoids. Combined with renal sodium retention, lymph production and often low plasma albumin, this favours  ascites , accumulation of fluid in the peritoneal cavity. The mechanism is multifactorial; increased protein concentration in all ascitic fluid should not be assumed. \n  Check:  Why can severe shock injure the gut even while blood pressure is defended? \n  Answer Sympathetic redistribution reduces splanchnic perfusion to favour vital organs. Prolonged local hypoperfusion can cause mucosal ischaemia despite partly preserved systemic pressure. \n\n Two inflows and the hepatic microvascular route \n Arteries from the  coeliac trunk, superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric arteries  supply abdominal organs; portal venous blood collects nutrients and metabolites from the gut/spleen before delivering them to the liver. The liver receives blood through  portal vein and hepatic artery  branches, which mix in sinusoids and drain to  central veins → hepatic veins → IVC . The  portal triad  contains a portal venule, hepatic arteriole and bile ductule. Bile generally travels in a direction opposing sinusoidal blood drainage. \n In the classical lobule, periportal hepatocytes are closer to the portal triad and receive more oxygenated inflow;  centrilobular (zone 3)  hepatocytes near terminal hepatic venules have relatively lower oxygen availability and are especially vulnerable to some ischaemic insults. This is why the lecturer emphasises oxygen gradients within liver acini. \n Digestion, shock and venous storage \n Following a meal, intestinal metabolic activity increases  splanchnic flow  (postprandial hyperaemia). The splanchnic venous bed is a substantial  blood reservoir . During marked sympathetic activation in haemorrhage, splanchnic vasoconstriction shifts blood centrally and reduces gut perfusion; prolonged severe underperfusion can injure the bowel. \n Explain ascites without one misleading protein story \n  Ascites  is fluid accumulated within the peritoneal cavity. Portal hypertension increases sinusoidal hydrostatic pressure and hepatic lymph formation; when lymphatic drainage is exceeded, fluid can accumulate in the abdomen. In cirrhosis, sodium/water retention and lowered serum albumin often contribute. The composition and protein concentration of ascitic fluid vary with the underlying cause; high-protein fluid is  not universal  in all ascites. \n  Practice:  Why is zone 3 particularly sensitive to low oxygen supply? It lies downstream in the sinusoidal pathway and receives blood with less remaining oxygen. \n"},{"slug":"skin","title":"Cutaneous Circulation","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"38-41","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"skin-blood-flow-regulates-heat","title":"Skin blood flow regulates heat"},{"id":"local-vascular-reactions-after-stroking-the-skin","title":"Local vascular reactions after stroking the skin"},{"id":"shock-and-temperature","title":"Shock and temperature"},{"id":"distinguish-blood-flow-functions","title":"Distinguish blood-flow functions"},{"id":"lewis-triple-response-to-stroking-the-skin","title":"Lewis triple response to stroking the skin"}],"html":"<h1>Cutaneous Circulation and the Triple Response</h1>\n<h2 id=\"skin-blood-flow-regulates-heat\">Skin blood flow regulates heat</h2>\n<p>Skin has a rich vascular network, including <strong>arteriovenous anastomoses</strong> in some regions. Increasing cutaneous perfusion promotes heat loss, whereas sympathetic vasoconstriction limits heat transfer and helps preserve central blood volume during haemorrhage.</p>\n<h2 id=\"local-vascular-reactions-after-stroking-the-skin\">Local vascular reactions after stroking the skin</h2>\n<p>A mild stroke can initially produce a transient pale line through local vasoconstriction (the <strong>white reaction</strong>). Firmer stroking may produce the <strong>triple response of Lewis</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Red line</strong> near the stimulated area, reflecting local vasodilatation.</li>\n<li><strong>Flare</strong>, a wider surrounding erythema mediated partly by sensory axon reflexes and vasodilators.</li>\n<li><strong>Wheal</strong>, a raised swelling caused by increased vascular permeability and fluid leakage.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Histamine contributes importantly to these responses; their timing and expression vary.</p>\n<h2 id=\"shock-and-temperature\">Shock and temperature</h2>\n<p>In haemorrhagic shock, intense skin vasoconstriction often produces cool, pale extremities. In warm environments, competing requirements for heat loss and blood-pressure maintenance influence vascular tone; skin circulation is regulated for <strong>thermoregulation</strong>, not simply nutrient demand.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> What makes a wheal different from the red flare around a skin scratch?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>A wheal is local oedema from increased permeability, whereas the flare is wider redness from arteriolar vasodilatation involving a sensory axon reflex.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"distinguish-blood-flow-functions\">Distinguish blood-flow functions</h2>\n<p>Skin circulation is unusual because its flow can greatly exceed local nutrient needs when the body must <strong>lose heat</strong>. Sympathetic-mediated cutaneous constriction conserves heat in cold conditions, whereas vasodilator mechanisms and opening of thermoregulatory arteriovenous anastomoses help increase heat loss in warmth. During haemorrhagic shock, strong skin vasoconstriction can contribute to pallor and coolness.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lewis-triple-response-to-stroking-the-skin\">Lewis triple response to stroking the skin</h2>\n<p>A firm, blunt stroke produces a <strong>red line</strong> at the site (local capillary/arteriolar dilation), a surrounding <strong>flare</strong> (wider arteriolar dilation partly due to local sensory-nerve <strong>axon reflex</strong>) and a raised <strong>wheal</strong> (local oedema as permeability rises). Histamine contributes to the reaction. The lecturer separately describes a brief <strong>white line response</strong> with gentler stroking, traditionally attributed to local constrictor response; distinguish it from the red line/flare/wheal response.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Which component of the triple response is chiefly an oedematous swelling? The <strong>wheal</strong>; the flare is spreading redness.</p>\n","plain":" Cutaneous Circulation and the Triple Response \n Skin blood flow regulates heat \n Skin has a rich vascular network, including  arteriovenous anastomoses  in some regions. Increasing cutaneous perfusion promotes heat loss, whereas sympathetic vasoconstriction limits heat transfer and helps preserve central blood volume during haemorrhage. \n Local vascular reactions after stroking the skin \n A mild stroke can initially produce a transient pale line through local vasoconstriction (the  white reaction ). Firmer stroking may produce the  triple response of Lewis : \n \n  Red line  near the stimulated area, reflecting local vasodilatation. \n  Flare , a wider surrounding erythema mediated partly by sensory axon reflexes and vasodilators. \n  Wheal , a raised swelling caused by increased vascular permeability and fluid leakage. \n \n Histamine contributes importantly to these responses; their timing and expression vary. \n Shock and temperature \n In haemorrhagic shock, intense skin vasoconstriction often produces cool, pale extremities. In warm environments, competing requirements for heat loss and blood-pressure maintenance influence vascular tone; skin circulation is regulated for  thermoregulation , not simply nutrient demand. \n  Check:  What makes a wheal different from the red flare around a skin scratch? \n  Answer A wheal is local oedema from increased permeability, whereas the flare is wider redness from arteriolar vasodilatation involving a sensory axon reflex. \n\n Distinguish blood-flow functions \n Skin circulation is unusual because its flow can greatly exceed local nutrient needs when the body must  lose heat . Sympathetic-mediated cutaneous constriction conserves heat in cold conditions, whereas vasodilator mechanisms and opening of thermoregulatory arteriovenous anastomoses help increase heat loss in warmth. During haemorrhagic shock, strong skin vasoconstriction can contribute to pallor and coolness. \n Lewis triple response to stroking the skin \n A firm, blunt stroke produces a  red line  at the site (local capillary/arteriolar dilation), a surrounding  flare  (wider arteriolar dilation partly due to local sensory-nerve  axon reflex ) and a raised  wheal  (local oedema as permeability rises). Histamine contributes to the reaction. The lecturer separately describes a brief  white line response  with gentler stroking, traditionally attributed to local constrictor response; distinguish it from the red line/flare/wheal response. \n  Practice:  Which component of the triple response is chiefly an oedematous swelling? The  wheal ; the flare is spreading redness. \n"},{"slug":"fetal","title":"Fetal Circulation and Birth Transitions","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"42-45","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"why-fetal-flow-takes-shortcuts","title":"Why fetal flow takes shortcuts"},{"id":"what-changes-after-the-first-breaths","title":"What changes after the first breaths?"},{"id":"fetal-haemoglobin","title":"Fetal haemoglobin"},{"id":"trace-the-preferentially-oxygenated-fetal-stream","title":"Trace the preferentially oxygenated fetal stream"},{"id":"what-closes-after-delivery","title":"What closes after delivery?"}],"html":"<h1>Fetal Circulation and the Transition at Birth</h1>\n<h2 id=\"why-fetal-flow-takes-shortcuts\">Why fetal flow takes shortcuts</h2>\n<p>Before birth, gas exchange happens in the <strong>placenta</strong>, not the fluid-filled lungs. Oxygenated blood returns through the <strong>umbilical vein</strong>. Some passes through the liver, while a substantial fraction bypasses hepatic sinusoids through the <strong>ductus venosus</strong> to the inferior vena cava.</p>\n<p>Because fetal pulmonary vascular resistance is high, right-heart flow partly bypasses lungs by two pathways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Foramen ovale:</strong> right atrium → left atrium (favoured by fetal pressure relations and directed IVC flow).</li>\n<li><strong>Ductus arteriosus:</strong> pulmonary trunk → descending aorta.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Two <strong>umbilical arteries</strong> carry fetal blood to the placenta. Oxygen-rich and oxygen-poor streams mix, so fetal saturation varies between vessels and chambers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-changes-after-the-first-breaths\">What changes after the first breaths?</h2>\n<p>Lung expansion and increasing alveolar oxygen lower pulmonary vascular resistance, increasing pulmonary blood flow and left atrial return. Umbilical cord clamping removes placental low-resistance flow and increases systemic vascular resistance. Rising <strong>left atrial pressure relative to right</strong> functionally closes the foramen ovale; increased oxygen and reduced prostaglandin influence constrict the ductus arteriosus.</p>\n<h2 id=\"fetal-haemoglobin\">Fetal haemoglobin</h2>\n<p><strong>HbF</strong> has higher oxygen affinity than adult HbA, in part because it binds <strong>2,3-BPG</strong> less strongly. This helps fetal blood acquire oxygen across the placenta. HbF declines after birth as adult haemoglobin replaces it over months.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why does the first breath increase blood return from lungs to the left atrium?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Alveolar expansion and increased oxygen markedly decrease pulmonary vascular resistance, allowing more right-ventricular output through the lungs into pulmonary veins and left atrium.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"trace-the-preferentially-oxygenated-fetal-stream\">Trace the preferentially oxygenated fetal stream</h2>\n<p>The placenta oxygenates fetal blood. The <strong>umbilical vein</strong> brings comparatively oxygen-rich blood toward the liver; some flows through the <strong>ductus venosus</strong> to the IVC. Preferential streaming across the <strong>foramen ovale</strong> supplies left atrium → left ventricle → ascending aorta, supporting perfusion of the developing heart and brain. Much right ventricular output enters pulmonary trunk and passes through <strong>ductus arteriosus</strong> into descending aorta, bypassing the high-resistance unventilated lungs. <strong>Umbilical arteries</strong> return lower-oxygen blood to placenta.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-closes-after-delivery\">What closes after delivery?</h2>\n<p>First breaths expand lungs and increase alveolar oxygen, sharply reducing <strong>pulmonary vascular resistance</strong> and increasing pulmonary venous return. Cord clamping removes the low-resistance placental bed and increases <strong>systemic vascular resistance</strong>. Higher left than right atrial pressure functionally closes the foramen ovale, while rising oxygen and lower prostaglandin signalling promote <strong>ductus arteriosus constriction</strong>. Anatomical closure/remodelling follows functional closure on a different time scale; persistent patency is clinically relevant.</p>\n<p><strong>HbF</strong> holds oxygen more avidly than HbA partly due to lower 2,3-BPG affinity. HbF does not invariably disappear by an exact fourth postnatal month; replacement is gradual over months.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why does pulmonary blood flow rise after birth even as systemic resistance increases? Lung expansion/oxygen directly reduce <strong>pulmonary</strong> resistance; placental separation independently raises <strong>systemic</strong> resistance.</p>\n","plain":" Fetal Circulation and the Transition at Birth \n Why fetal flow takes shortcuts \n Before birth, gas exchange happens in the  placenta , not the fluid-filled lungs. Oxygenated blood returns through the  umbilical vein . Some passes through the liver, while a substantial fraction bypasses hepatic sinusoids through the  ductus venosus  to the inferior vena cava. \n Because fetal pulmonary vascular resistance is high, right-heart flow partly bypasses lungs by two pathways: \n \n  Foramen ovale:  right atrium → left atrium (favoured by fetal pressure relations and directed IVC flow). \n  Ductus arteriosus:  pulmonary trunk → descending aorta. \n \n Two  umbilical arteries  carry fetal blood to the placenta. Oxygen-rich and oxygen-poor streams mix, so fetal saturation varies between vessels and chambers. \n What changes after the first breaths? \n Lung expansion and increasing alveolar oxygen lower pulmonary vascular resistance, increasing pulmonary blood flow and left atrial return. Umbilical cord clamping removes placental low-resistance flow and increases systemic vascular resistance. Rising  left atrial pressure relative to right  functionally closes the foramen ovale; increased oxygen and reduced prostaglandin influence constrict the ductus arteriosus. \n Fetal haemoglobin \n  HbF  has higher oxygen affinity than adult HbA, in part because it binds  2,3-BPG  less strongly. This helps fetal blood acquire oxygen across the placenta. HbF declines after birth as adult haemoglobin replaces it over months. \n  Check:  Why does the first breath increase blood return from lungs to the left atrium? \n  Answer Alveolar expansion and increased oxygen markedly decrease pulmonary vascular resistance, allowing more right-ventricular output through the lungs into pulmonary veins and left atrium. \n\n Trace the preferentially oxygenated fetal stream \n The placenta oxygenates fetal blood. The  umbilical vein  brings comparatively oxygen-rich blood toward the liver; some flows through the  ductus venosus  to the IVC. Preferential streaming across the  foramen ovale  supplies left atrium → left ventricle → ascending aorta, supporting perfusion of the developing heart and brain. Much right ventricular output enters pulmonary trunk and passes through  ductus arteriosus  into descending aorta, bypassing the high-resistance unventilated lungs.  Umbilical arteries  return lower-oxygen blood to placenta. \n What closes after delivery? \n First breaths expand lungs and increase alveolar oxygen, sharply reducing  pulmonary vascular resistance  and increasing pulmonary venous return. Cord clamping removes the low-resistance placental bed and increases  systemic vascular resistance . Higher left than right atrial pressure functionally closes the foramen ovale, while rising oxygen and lower prostaglandin signalling promote  ductus arteriosus constriction . Anatomical closure/remodelling follows functional closure on a different time scale; persistent patency is clinically relevant. \n  HbF  holds oxygen more avidly than HbA partly due to lower 2,3-BPG affinity. HbF does not invariably disappear by an exact fourth postnatal month; replacement is gradual over months. \n  Practice:  Why does pulmonary blood flow rise after birth even as systemic resistance increases? Lung expansion/oxygen directly reduce  pulmonary  resistance; placental separation independently raises  systemic  resistance. \n"}];
